window.CASES={"Blue Yonder ": "§ Background\nBlue Yonder Airlines is a global carrier headquartered in Los Angeles, California, operating domestic and international flights. The company serves millions of passengers annually through its website, mobile app, and call centers. To improve customer service efficiency and reduce call center volume, Blue Yonder is deploying an AI agent in Microsoft Copilot Studio.\nThe agent will handle customer inquiries across multiple channels – web chat, mobile app, and Microsoft Teams (for internal support staff). It will answer questions, retrieve data from enterprise systems, and escalate to human agents when needed.\nThe project is led by a cross-function team:\nProduct manager: Defines requirements and success metrics.\nLead agent author: Designs topics, intents, and generative behavior.\nFlow designers: Build agent flows and integrations.\nIT/security and compliance: Oversees identity, data protection, and Responsible AI (RAI) compliance.\n§ Current environment\n§ Channels\nPublic website: Embedded web chat\nMobile app: In-app chatbot\nMicrosoft Teams: Internal support agent access\n§ Identity and access\nCustomers: Anonymous access for general inquiries (e.g., flight status, baggage policy).\nAuthentication is required for personal data access (e.g., bookings, loyalty points).\nInternal staff: Authenticate via Microsoft Entra ID.\n§ Data sources\nReservation and Ticketing System (internal): REST API, no prebuilt connector with custom enterprise database. Flight Status and Weather APIs (external): REST APIs with API keys.\nCustomer Support Knowledge Base: SharePoint library with PDFs and policy documents.\nLoyalty Program Data: Stored in Dynamics 365 and Dataverse.\nTravel Advisory Content: Uses REST API with partner services.\n§ Integration mechanisms\nCustom connectors must be used for internal APIs that lack prebuilt connectors.\nHTTP request nodes may be used for lightweight external APIs.\nKnowledge sources must be used for unstructured content.\nAgent flows must be used to encapsulate reusable logic (e.g., rebooking).\n§ Business requirements\n§ Omnichannel support\nDeploy the agent across web, mobile, and Teams with a consistent user experience. The Teams deployment must also support internal staff.\n§ Self-service capabilities\nThe agent must handle common inquiries such as:\n§ Flight status\n§ Booking and rebooking\n§ Loyalty program questions\n§ Travel policies and baggage rules\n§ Human escalation\nIf the agent cannot resolve an issue or the user requests help, it must:\nEscalate to a human agent.\nTransfer the conversation transcript and relevant context.\nRedact any sensitive personal data before escalation.\n§ Knowledge integration\nThe agent must use scalable methods for knowledge integration and must not rely on manually authored Q&A topics for each document.\n§ Performance metrics\nFirst-contact resolution: +25%\nTier-1 call deflection: ≥20%\nResponse time: 90% of queries answered within 30 seconds\nAccuracy: ≥95% for known FAQs\nCSAT: ≥85% for AI-handled interactions\nTechnical requirements\n§ Platform constraints\nNo custom code is permitted; only Copilot Studio's built-in tools may be used.\nAll backend logic must be implemented using agent flows.\nMarkdown must be used for formatting (e.g., bold, bullet points); HTML is not supported.\n§ Authentication\nSign-in is required for personal data access.\nAnonymous access is allowed for general inquiries.\nUser identity must be used for data access; shared or builder credentials must not be used.\n§ Compliance and security\nPower Platform DLP policies must be enforced to block unauthorized data flows.\nResponsible AI content moderation filters must be enabled.\nPrompt modifications must be added to enforce tone, disclaimers, and refusal behavior.\nDisclaimers must be applied consistently across all generative responses. Manual edits to individual topics must be avoided.\n§ Monitoring and maintenance\nAll conversations and actions must be logged for auditing.\nWeekly reviews of transcripts and metrics must be conducted.\n§ Issues and constraints\nAPI rate limits: External APIs (e.g., flight status) have usage limits. Agent flows must handle retries and caching to avoid exceeding quotas.\nKnowledge base limits: Copilot Studio has limits on the number and size of indexed documents. Large files must be split or summarized.\nGenerative answer risks: Generative responses must be constrained to avoid policy violations. Prompt modifications and filters must be used to enforce tone, safety, and compliance.\nUser input variability: Users phrase questions in diverse ways. Topics must include varied trigger phrases and fallback handling.\nAuthentication UX: The agent must clearly explain when sign-in is required and handle transitions smoothly across channels.\n§ Problem statement\nBlue Yonder Airlines must deploy a secure, scalable, and policy-compliant AI agent using Microsoft Copilot Studio. The agent must deliver accurate, helpful, and safe responses across multiple channels, integrate with enterprise systems, and support both anonymous and authenticated users. It must adhere to strict data protection and Responsible AI standards while improving customer service efficiency and satisfaction.", "Fabrikam Inc": "§ Background\nFabrikam Inc. is a Canada-based manufacturer with a growing service organization that supports field technicians and internal operations teams. Fabrikam Inc. plans to launch a new internal agent solution named Operations Concierge to reduce time spent searching policy content, retrieving operational metrics, and executing routine transactions.\nThe agent will be used by three groups:\n§ Service coordinators who triage incoming service requests\nField technicians who need guided procedures and parts availability\n§ Operations managers who monitor KPIs and exceptions\nThe agent solution must work in real-world operational conditions. Users often ask questions mid-call with a customer or while coordinating parts shipments. The agents require quick, reliable outcomes. As a result, Fabrikam Inc. requires the solution to:\nProvide grounded answers with traceability when it provides guidance.\nRetrieve real-time metrics when users ask for operational status.\nExecute authenticated updates when users initiate a flow (such as creating a parts request).\nFabrikam Inc. also expects the solution to be maintained by multiple makers and developers across the year. The company has experienced duplicated logic and inconsistent behavior across different agents. This project emphasizes reuse, governance, and maintainability across teams.\n§ Current environment\nFabrikam Inc. runs three Microsoft Power Platform environments for agent development and release: Dev, Test, and Prod.\nThe team plans to build the agent and validate it in Dev and Test, then promote to Prod by using a controlled release process that supports repeatable deployments.\nFabrikam Inc. already has two assets the team wants to reuse:\nA partially completed Copilot Studio agent named Service Desk Agent, used by IT to create internal tickets and route requests\nA Microsoft Foundry agent created by a central AI team that performs specialized summarization and classification for long-form text (for example, summarizing call transcripts into an incident narrative) Fabrikam Inc. also has operational and knowledge data sources:\nA curated policy library (internal SOPs, service warranty rules, escalation criteria, and standard operating procedures)\nA set of indexed documents and procedures in an Azure AI Search service that supports vector search for the policy library\nA Microsoft Fabric workspace that includes a semantic model used by operations leadership for reporting Business requirements\nFabrikam Inc. requires Operations Concierge to meet the following business requirements:\nTraceability requirement: When the agent provides policy guidance or procedural recommendations, users must be able to see where the answer came from.\nMetrics requirement: When users ask about service performance (backlog, SLA risk, parts shortages, dispatch delays), the solution must return up-to-date metrics in a structured format that operations managers can use in weekly reviews.\nTransaction requirement: The solution must support authenticated updates initiated during conversations, including creating a parts request and updating a service case status.\nIn addition, Fabrikam Inc. wants to avoid duplicating common assets across agents:\nThe team must reuse the same set of escalation topics, MCP tool definitions, and a standard safety disclaimer across three different agents.\nOnly the platform engineering group as allowed to edit shared assets. However, all agent authors must be able to use them.\nTechnical requirements\nThe Fabrikam Inc. solution architecture uses a multi-agent approach so that specialist responsibilities are isolated and can evolve independently.\nThe Operations Concierge (primary agent) must coordinate the following specialist capabilities:\nPolicy and procedure Q&A: Use an enterprise knowledge source that supports indexed retrieval across the curated policy library and service procedures.\nOperational metrics: Delegate metric queries to a Fabric Data Agent that reads governed business data through the Fabric semantic model.\nAuthenticated updates: Use tools exposed by an existing internal Model Context Protocol (MCP) server that provides transactional operations for the service organization.\nSpecialized processing: Delegate summarization and classification requests to an existing Microsoft Foundry agent.\nFabrikam Inc. will onboard two MCP servers as tools:\nPartsOps MCP server: exposes tools for parts availability checks and parts request creation. The server requires per-user authentication because actions must be traceable to the requesting user.\nWarrantyRules MCP server: exposes a read-only tool for validating warranty coverage. The server uses an API key shared by the agent team.\nFabrikam Inc. has also defined a collaboration requirement with the existing Service Desk Agent:\nThe primary agent must delegate IT-specific requests to the existing Service Desk Agent rather than reimplement ticket creation logic.\nFinally, Fabrikarn Inc. plans to support a partner integration:\nFor shipment tracking inquiries, Fabrikam Inc. will delegate to a partner-provided agent that is only available through a standardized agent-to-agent endpoint.\n§ Issues and constraints\nDuring early testing, Fabrikam Inc. found three recurring problems:\nMakers are copying and modifying the same components across agents, resulting in inconsistent disclaimers and duplicated tools.\nUsers can obtain a correct answer, but the response is not consistently traceable to a source when the agent uses knowledge.\nThe primary agent can route some requests, but specialist capabilities are not consistently delegated (for example, some metric questions are answered generatively instead of being routed to the Fabric Data Agent).\nYou are part of the engineering team responsible for correcting the design and configuration to meet the preceding requirements and constraints."};
window.QUESTIONS=[{"id": 1, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to configure the agent in Copilot Studio to meet Blue Vender's Responsible AI and data protection requirements.\nWhich three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "§ Add custom prompt modifications\nWhy it's first: This is the base layer of safety. Custom instructions (or system prompts) establish the fundamental boundaries, personality, and contextual rules for the generative AI model inside Microsoft Copilot Studio.\nIt explicitly tells the LLM how to behave, what tone to maintain, and what topics or data sources are strictly off-limits before any user interaction happens.\n§ Configure content moderation filters\nWhy it's second: Once the core instructions are set, you apply real-time filtering layers. Content moderation filters act as an active validation shield that monitors incoming user prompts and outgoing model completions. It detects and blocks toxic, unsafe, or off-policy phrasing that a custom prompt alone might fail to stop, completely satisfying the requirement that \"filters must be used to enforce tone, safety, and compliance.\" ​Conduct adversarial testing\nWhy it's last: Testing always occurs after all defensive measures and configuration changes have been implemented.\nAdversarial testing (commonly known as red-teaming) attempts to deliberately trick, bypass, or prompt-inject the copilot. Performing this as the final step verifies that your prompt modifications and content moderation filters actually function as intended under realistic stress scenarios before deployment.\nWhy the other choices are incorrect\nDefine Data Loss Prevention (DLP) policies: DLP policies control environment-level connectors and data flow at the Power Platform administration tier, rather than controlling the specific conversational tone, safety, and compliance filters inside a generative copilot.\nIntegrate with Microsoft Purview: While Purview is excellent for enterprise compliance and data lifecycle auditing, the specific requirement explicitly emphasizes adjusting prompt instructions and filters directly within the copilot architecture.\nDisable generative answers: The case study specifically requires using generative answers safely (\"Generative responses must be constrained...\"), so disabling them completely defeats the fundamental purpose of the requested AI deployment.", "type": "order", "items": ["Add custom prompt modifications.", "Conduct adversarial testing.", "Define Data Loss Prevention (DLP) policies.", "Integrate with Microsoft Purview.", "Disable generative answers.", "Configure content moderation filters."], "answer": ["Add custom prompt modifications.", "Configure content moderation filters.", "Conduct adversarial testing."]}, {"id": 2, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent in Copilot Studio that produces structured responses using a reusable instruction template. The responses follow the required tone and format. During a compliance review, the team discovers that the responses were generated using a model that is not on the approved list.\nThe company requires that the reusable instruction template continues to be used and the model used for prompt- driven responses meets governance requirements.\nThe solution must ensure that prompt-driven responses are generated using an approved model.\nYou need to configure the solution that meets the requirements.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Why A and C are the correct choices\nA – Select an approved model from the Microsoft Foundry model catalog\nCopilot Studio allows the prompt-driven response engine to be bound to a specific model at configuration time. By explicitly selecting an approved model from the Microsoft Foundry catalog, you guarantee that every call to the language model adheres to governance and compliance policies. This action directly satisfies the requirement that “the model used for prompt-driven responses meets governance requirements” without requiring any runtime enforcement.\nC – Ensure the reusable instruction template is the one applied to the agent that generates the structured response\nThe instruction template governs the tone, format, and content of the responses. Keeping the approved template attached to the agent ensures that, even when the model changes, the output remains consistent with the required structure and style. This fulfills the stipulation that “the reusable instruction template continues to be used” while the model is swapped to an approved one.\nWhy the other options are less suitable or incorrect\nB – Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding source\nGrounding with Azure AI Search influences the data retrieved for generation but does not restrict or select the underlying language model. It does not enforce the use of an approved model, so it fails to meet the model-governance requirement.\nD – Publish the agent so that the model used by prompt-driven responses is updated automatically Publishing merely makes the latest configuration available; it does not specify which model is used. Without an explicit model selection, publishing could retain a non-approved model, violating compliance.\nE – Enable citations so generated responses can be traced and comply with governance requirements Citations improve traceability and auditability but do not control which model produces the response.\nGovernance of the model itself remains unaddressed.\nF – Add a line to the instruction template that requires the agent to use only approved models Instruction templates in Copilot Studio guide response content and style; they cannot enforce model selection. The platform does not interpret such a directive to switch models, so this action would have no effect on model compliance.\nReferences\nMicrosoft Copilot Studio documentation – Configure models for prompt-driven responses:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot-studio/configure-models\nMicrosoft Foundry model catalog – Selecting and managing approved models: https://learn.microsoft.com/en- us/azure/ai-foundry/model-catalog/overview", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Select an approved model from the Microsoft Foundry model catalog in the configuration that is used for prompt-driven response generation."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding source so the approved model requirement is enforced during response generation."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Ensure the reusable instruction template is the one applied to the agent that generates the structured response."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Publish the agent so that the model used by prompt-driven responses is updated automatically."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Enable citations so generated responses can be traced and comply with governance requirements."}, {"k": "F", "t": "Add a line to the instruction template that requires the agent to use only approved models when generating responses."}], "answer": ["A", "C"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 3, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company needs to ground an agent in Copilot Studio answers using live enterprise data from a supported system via a Microsoft Power Platform connector. To meet the business needs, the builder must meet the following requirements:\n• Authenticate using a valid connection.\n• Add the connector as a real-time knowledge source.\n• Select the tables for knowledge grounding.\nYou need to add a Power Platform connector as a real-time knowledge source for the agent.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "1. Create a connection using a Microsoft Power Platform connector.\n2. In the Copilot Studio agent, navigate to\nthe Knowledge tab.\n3. Select Add a knowledge source and choose the Power Platform connector.\n4. Select the\nspecific tables to ground the agent's answers.", "type": "order", "items": ["Select the tables to include as the knowledge source.", "Add the connector to the agent.", "Select a real-time connector from the Add knowledge dialog.", "Sign in and create a new connection or reuse an existing connection.", "Add a knowledge source."], "answer": ["Add a knowledge source.", "Select a real-time connector from the Add knowledge dialog.", "Sign in and create a new connection or reuse an existing connection.", "Select the tables to include as the knowledge source.", "Add the connector to the agent."], "note": "No answer image; order derived from Copilot Studio flow (Add knowledge > connector > connection > tables > add). The text explanation lists connection first, so the first positions are uncertain.", "prompt": "Add a Power Platform connector as a real-time knowledge source for the agent. Arrange the actions in order."}, {"id": 4, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to configure the agent in Copilot Studio to use internal and external partner knowledge sources to answer user questions about the airline services.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Technical Justification\nOption A (Correct): Use a Microsoft Graph connector to index the partner's travel advisory content.\nThe requirement specifies integrating \"Travel Advisory Content\" sourced from a partner REST API. Microsoft Graph connectors are the standard mechanism in Copilot Studio to ingest external, structured or semi- structured data from APIs (including partner services) into the semantic index, making it searchable for generative answers without custom code. This satisfies the \"Platform constraints\" (no custom code, built-in tools only) and \"Knowledge integration\" (scalable methods, no manual Q&A) requirements effectively.\nOption D (Correct): Add the internal policy documents as a knowledge source.\nThe \"Customer Support Knowledge Base\" resides in a SharePoint library containing PDFs and policy documents. Copilot Studio natively supports SharePoint sites/libraries as knowledge sources for generative answers. This directly addresses the requirement to use scalable knowledge integration for unstructured content, leveraging the existing Dataverse/SharePoint infrastructure without requiring manual topic authoring.\nOption B (Incorrect): Write individual Q&A pairs for each document as separate topics.\nThis violates the explicit \"Knowledge integration\" business requirement stating the agent \"must not rely on manually authored Q&A topics for each document.\" It also violates \"Technical requirements\" mandating the use of built-in tools (knowledge sources) over custom logic (manual topics) and creates an unmaintainable architecture given document volume and change frequency.\nOption C (Incorrect): Enable unrestricted web search for the agent.\nThis violates \"Compliance and security\" requirements mandating Responsible AI content moderation, data protection, and grounding responses in verified enterprise knowledge. Unrestricted web search introduces hallucination risks, policy violations, data leakage potential, and fails to meet the ≥95% accuracy target for known FAQs sourced from authorized internal/partner systems.\nReferences\n1. Add knowledge to your agent in Microsoft Copilot Studio\n2. Microsoft Graph connectors overview", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Use a Microsoft Graph connector to index the partner's travel advisory content."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Write individual Q&A pairs for each document as separate topics."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Enable unrestricted web search for the agent."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add the internal policy documents as a knowledge source."}], "answer": ["A", "D"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 5, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to ensure that every AI-generated response from the agent in Copilot Studio includes a disclaimer that complies with the company's security and governance policies.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Justification for Correct Options (B & C)\nB. Add a prompt modification in the generative answers node settings.\nThis is the primary, platform-native mechanism to enforce global behavioral constraints on all LLM-generated output. As explicitly stated in the technical requirements, \"Prompt modifications must be added to enforce tone, disclaimers, and refusal behavior\" and \"Disclaimers must be applied consistently across all generative responses.\" Prompt modifications inject instructions (e.g., \"Append the following disclaimer to every response: ...\") directly into the system prompt sent to the model for every Generative Answers node execution. This ensures compliance without authoring overhead, satisfies the \"no manual edits to individual topics\" constraint, and scales automatically across all topics using generative nodes.\nC. Create a disclaimer topic that always runs first.\nThis refers to configuring the Conversation Start system topic. The very first response delivered by the agent (the welcome/greeting message) is typically authored statically within this system topic and does not pass through a Generative Answers node. Therefore, prompt modifications (Option B) do not apply to this initial message. To ensure the disclaimer appears on the first AI-generated response—which is a requirement for \"every AI-generated response\"—a dedicated topic (or modification of the System Conversation Start topic) must be configured to run first and include the disclaimer. This covers the initialization edge case that prompt modifications cannot reach.\nWhy Other Options Are Incorrect\nA. Add a greeting message that includes the disclaimer.\nA simple greeting message only covers the first turn. It does not address subsequent generative responses throughout the conversation lifecycle, failing the requirement for consistency across all responses.\nD. Disable generative answers and use only pre-authored responses.\nThis violates the core business requirement for \"scalable methods for knowledge integration\" and the mandate to not \"rely on manually authored Q&A topics for each document.\" It eliminates the agent's ability to reason over unstructured knowledge sources (SharePoint/PDFs) dynamically.\nE. Edit every topic's first message to include the disclaimer.\nThis directly violates the technical requirement: \"Manual edits to individual topics must be avoided.\" It creates an unmaintainable implementation, introduces high risk of inconsistency (human error), and does not scale for net-new topics added later.\nReferences\n1. Configure generative answers node - Microsoft Copilot Studio | Microsoft Learn\n2. System topics in Microsoft Copilot Studio | Microsoft Learn", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Add a greeting message that includes the disclaimer."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Add a prompt modification in the generative answers node settings."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Create a disclaimer topic that always runs first."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Disable generative answers and use only pre-authored responses."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Edit every topic's first message to include the disclaimer."}], "answer": ["B", "C"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 6, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to determine which authentication model should be applied to each Blue Yonder Copilot interaction scenario to comply with the company's security and governance requirements.\nWhich authentication requirement should you apply to each scenario? To answer, move the appropriate authentication requirements to the correct user scenarios. You may use each authentication requirement once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Allow anonymous access.", "Require customer sign-in using an account.", "Require internal agent sign-in using Microsoft Entra ID.", "Use agent author credentials for authenticated data calls."], "slots": ["A customer asks, \"What is the baggage allowance for international flights?\"", "A customer asks, \"Can I review my upcoming trips?\"", "An internal support agent accesses the Copilot agent from Microsoft Teams to look up a loyalty member's profile."], "answer": ["Allow anonymous access.", "Require customer sign-in using an account.", "Require internal agent sign-in using Microsoft Entra ID."], "reuse": true, "prompt": "Move the appropriate authentication requirements to the correct user scenarios. Each requirement may be used once, more than once, or not at all."}, {"id": 7, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company has an existing custom connector that is approved and available in the environment. A builder wants an agent in Copilot Studio to call the connector during a conversation to retrieve information from an internal system. To meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• The agent must make the connector available for topic steps.\n• The agent must run the connector call with a valid connection.\n• The connector call must receive the required input values at runtime.\nYou need to configure the agent so the custom connector can be used as a tool.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Map topic variables to the tool inputs.", "Add the connector as a tool in the agent.", "Add the connector as a knowledge source.", "Create or reuse a connection for the connector."], "slots": ["The agent must make the connector available for topic steps.", "The agent must run the connector call with a valid connection.", "The connector call must receive the required input values at runtime."], "answer": ["Add the connector as a tool in the agent.", "Create or reuse a connection for the connector.", "Map topic variables to the tool inputs."]}, {"id": 8, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company needs to ground an agent in Copilot Studio answers using live enterprise data from a supported system via a Microsoft Power Platform connector.\nTo meet the business needs, the builder must meet the following requirements:\n• Authenticate using a valid connection.\n• Add the connector as a real-time knowledge source.\n• Select the tables for knowledge grounding.\nYou need to add a Power Platform connector as a real-time knowledge source for the agent.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "", "type": "order", "items": ["Select Sign in and create a new connection or reuse an existing connection.", "Select the tables to include as the knowledge source.", "Select Add knowledge from the agent Knowledge experience.", "Select the real-time connector from the Add knowledge dialog.", "Select Add to agent to complete the connection."], "answer": ["Select Add knowledge from the agent Knowledge experience.", "Select the real-time connector from the Add knowledge dialog.", "Select Sign in and create a new connection or reuse an existing connection.", "Select the tables to include as the knowledge source.", "Select Add to agent to complete the connection."], "prompt": "Add a Power Platform connector as a real-time knowledge source for the agent. Arrange all actions in the correct order."}, {"id": 9, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You deploy an agent in Microsoft Copilot Studio that triggers a cloud flow to update customer records in Microsoft Dataverse.\nYou observe the following after deploying the agent:\n• Several runs fail due to a temporary authentication issue.\n• The flow continues to trigger and generate additional failed runs.\nYou must:\n• Stop additional failed executions while troubleshooting.\n• Process a failed execution after resolving the authentication issue.\n• Validate if the most recent executions succeeded immediately after resolution.\nYou need to use the appropriate monitoring and management actions to restore normal flow operation and validate successful execution.\nWhich action should you perform for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Stop additional failed executions while troubleshooting.", "options": ["Delete the flow.", "Disable the flow.", "Cancel the most recent run.", "Modify the trigger condition."], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Process a failed execution after resolving the authentication issue.", "options": ["Restart the flow.", "Refresh the flow.", "Resubmit the run.", "Recreate the trigger."], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Validate if the most recent executions succeeded immediately after resolution.", "options": ["Republish the flow.", "Review the Activity history.", "Export the flow to a solution.", "Inspect the Analytics summary."], "answer": 1}]}, {"id": 10, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is preparing an agent flow so that it can be invoked by an agent during conversations.\nThe agent flow must meet the following requirements:\n• The agent must be able to trigger the flow.\n• The agent flow must be verified.\nYou need to prepare an agent flow so that it can be used by the agent.\nIn which order should you perform the actions to prepare the agent flow? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "", "type": "order", "items": ["Configure the invocation trigger that allows the agent to call the agent flow.", "Make the agent flow available for agent usage.", "Validate the agent flow behavior with representative test runs.", "Configure the actions that implement the required behavior.", "Create a new agent flow artifact in the environment."], "answer": ["Create a new agent flow artifact in the environment.", "Configure the invocation trigger that allows the agent to call the agent flow.", "Configure the actions that implement the required behavior.", "Validate the agent flow behavior with representative test runs.", "Make the agent flow available for agent usage."], "prompt": "Prepare an agent flow so that it can be invoked by the agent and is verified."}, {"id": 11, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent flow that occasionally requires human input before continuing execution.\nSome automated actions must pause until a human provides a decision or additional information. The flow must be configured to:\n• Capture a human response for use in later steps.\n• Continue processing within the same flow run after the response is submitted.\n• Wait for a manual decision before proceeding.\nYou need to configure a human-in-the-loop agent flow.\nWhich setting should you configure for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Capture a human response for use in downstream logic.", "options": ["Send a notification to a user.", "Use the action's response outputs in subsequent steps.", "Enable analytics logging for the response."], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Continue processing within the same flow run after a response is submitted.", "options": ["Republish the flow after the decision is submitted.", "Trigger a new flow run when a response is received.", "Allow the action to wait for and return a response before continuing."], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Wait for a manual decision before proceeding.", "options": ["Add a human approval action.", "Add a manual trigger to the flow.", "Send a message that asks for input."], "answer": 0}]}, {"id": 12, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent that invokes an agent flow to exchange information during a conversation.\nThe company requires that the agent send data into the flow and receive structured results back from the same flow run. To support this business need, the flow must be configured to do the following:\n• Capture the data provided by the agent.\n• Return data results to the agent.\nYou need to configure the flow so that it can exchange data with the agent.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Capture the data provided by the agent.", "options": ["Use an internal variable.", "Add a text input parameter.", "Store values only in the topic."], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Return data results to the agent.", "options": ["Log values to run history.", "Store values only in the flow.", "Add a text output parameter."], "answer": 2}]}, {"id": 13, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You are configuring an agent in Copilot Studio for an organization. The organization uses Microsoft Dataverse, Dynamics 365, and SharePoint Online.\nThe agent must retrieve information from enterprise systems and internal documents. The agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Must retrieve the current balance of a customer account stored in Dataverse.\nMust retrieve the real-time shipping status of an order that updates throughout the day in Dynamics 365. Must answer questions based on the content of a static PDF policy document.\nMust provide a welcome message that does NOT depend on enterprise data.\nYou need to configure the agent to meet the requirements.\nHow should the agent retrieve the data? To answer, move the appropriate solutions to the correct requirements. You may use each solution once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Create a manual response in the topic.", "Connect to a Power Platform connector.", "Upload a file to Agents Knowledge Base.", "Configure a generative answer without enterprise data."], "slots": ["Retrieve the current balance of a customer account stored in Dataverse.", "Retrieve the real-time shipping status of an order that updates throughout the day in Dynamics 365.", "Answer questions based on the content of a static PDF policy document.", "Provide a welcome message that does NOT depend on enterprise data."], "answer": ["Connect to a Power Platform connector.", "Connect to a Power Platform connector.", "Upload a file to Agents Knowledge Base.", "Create a manual response in the topic."], "reuse": true}, {"id": 14, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent calls a flow. The agent requires structured output values to be returned.\nThe agent receives unexpected or empty values.\nYou need to configure the agent so that data is exchanged correctly between the agent and the flow.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Justification for Correct Answer: A. Validate parameter definitions\nIn Copilot Studio, the integration contract between an agent and a Power Automate flow is strictly defined by the parameter definitions exposed in the flow’s trigger (inputs) and the \"Respond to a Power App or flow\" action (outputs). When an agent receives unexpected, empty, or malformed structured output, it indicates a schema mismatch between the flow’s defined output parameters (name, data type, structure) and the agent’s expected variable mappings. Validating parameter definitions ensures the flow’s output schema accurately reflects the payload being returned (e.g., complex objects vs. strings, correct property names, required fields) and that the agent’s variable assignments align with this contract. This is the primary configuration mechanism governing data exchange integrity.\nWhy Other Options Are Less Suitable:\nB. Change the trigger schema: The trigger schema defines inputs passed to the flow. The symptom describes a failure in output values returned from the flow. Modifying the trigger does not resolve an output contract mismatch.\nC. Review the flow run history: This is a diagnostic step for observing runtime payloads, not a configuration action. While useful for identifying the specific data discrepancy, it does not correct the underlying parameter definition misalignment causing the exchange failure.\nD. Add a Parse JSON inside the flow: This action processes internal flow data structures. It does not modify the external interface contract (the \"Respond to a Power App or flow\" output schema) that the agent consumes. The agent interacts only with the defined output parameters, not internal flow variables.\nE. Republish the agent: Republishing deploys the current agent configuration. If the flow’s parameter definitions (the contract) are invalid, republishing the agent merely redeploys the broken integration without fixing the schema mismatch.\nReferences\n1. Use Power Automate flows to extend your agents in Microsoft Copilot Studio - Define flow inputs and outputs\n2. Respond to a Power App or flow action in Power Automate", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Validate parameter definitions."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Change the trigger schema."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Review the flow run history."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add a Parse JSON inside the flow."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Republish the agent."}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 15, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is building an agent in Copilot Studio.\nThe agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Responses must be grounded in approved internal content.\n• The agent must be able to retrieve information from configured data sources in a topic.\nYou need to configure the agent so that topic responses are grounded from custom data sources.\nWhat should you configure?", "expl": "Justification for Correct Answer: C\nConfigure knowledge sources in a generative answers node.This is the correct architectural pattern in Copilot Studio for grounding topic-specific responses in custom data. The Generative Answers node is the explicit workflow component designed to perform Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) within a topic flow. By configuring knowledge sources (e.g., SharePoint, Dataverse, custom connectors) directly on this node, the author scopes the retrieval operation to specific approved content for that specific topic context. This satisfies both requirements: it enforces grounding strictly against the configured internal sources and enables retrieval execution at the precise point in the topic conversation flow.\nWhy Other Options Are Incorrect:\nA. Enable analytics: Analytics provides telemetry on topic triggering and usage metrics. It is a monitoring feature and does not configure data grounding or retrieval mechanisms.\nB. Add a custom prompt: System prompts or custom instructions guide LLM behavior but cannot inject or enforce retrieval from external data sources. Without a Generative Answers node (or similar RAG orchestration), the model relies solely on its parametric knowledge, violating the grounding requirement. D. Add SharePoint as a knowledge source only at the agent level: Agent-level knowledge sources enable global generative orchestration (the \"Conversational Boosting\" system topic). However, the requirement specifies retrieval must happen \"in a topic.\" Agent-level configuration does not allow topic-specific scoping of knowledge sources or deterministic placement of retrieval logic within a custom topic flow; that requires the Generative Answers node.\nE. Increase the response length limit: This configuration only adjusts the maximum token output length for generated responses. It has no impact on data retrieval, grounding sources, or the factual accuracy of the content.\nReferences\n1. Use generative answers in a topic - Microsoft Copilot Studio Documentation\n2. Add knowledge to your agent - Microsoft Copilot Studio Documentation", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Enable analytics to review which topics users trigger most often."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Add a custom prompt that tells the agent to answer only from company data."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Configure knowledge sources in a generative answers node."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add SharePoint as a knowledge source only at the agent level."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Increase the response length limit to improve answer completeness."}], "answer": ["C"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 16, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is building an agent in Copilot Studio topic that must retrieve real-time status information from an external REST API using a Send HTTP request node.\nTo meet the business needs, the topic must meet the following requirements:\n• The request must include the required authentication header.\n• The request must call the endpoint using the correct HTTP method.\nThe response must be configured with an appropriate response data type based on a schema and saved so the topic can reuse the returned values.\nYou need to configure the Send HTTP request node.\nWhich configuration should you use for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Add required headers to the HTTP request.", "Set the method to GET in the HTTP request.", "Select JSON data type and save response to a variable.", "Set the method to POST and send JSON in a request body."], "slots": ["Configure authentication for the request.", "Select the correct HTTP method for retrieving data.", "Configure structured data handling and storage of data for reuse in the topic."], "answer": ["Add required headers to the HTTP request.", "Set the method to GET in the HTTP request.", "Select JSON data type and save response to a variable."]}, {"id": 17, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is deploying an agent for employees.\nThe company has the following requirements:\n• Access must be restricted to the internal employees only.\n• End-user authentication must be enforced.\nYou need to configure the agent based on the company requirements.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nOption A is correct because selecting Authenticate with Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure AD) configures the agent to enforce organizational identity validation. This setting requires users to sign in using their corporate credentials (Entra ID identities) before interacting with the agent. It inherently restricts access to internal employees who possess valid accounts in the tenant, satisfying both the requirement for restricted internal access and mandatory end-user authentication. This is the standard mechanism for deploying secure, internal-facing Copilot Studio agents within the Microsoft 365 ecosystem.\nOption B is incorrect because \"Require Secure Access\" in the Web channel settings enforces HTTPS/TLS transport security for the communication channel, but it does not perform user identity authentication or authorization. It secures the data in transit but allows anonymous access unless paired with an authentication configuration.\nOption C is incorrect because publishing as a \"public website experience\" explicitly disables authentication requirements, allowing anonymous, unauthenticated access to anyone with the URL. This directly contradicts the requirements for restricted internal access and enforced authentication.\nOption D is incorrect because \"Authenticate with personal account\" configures the agent to accept Microsoft Accounts (MSA) such as Outlook.com or Gmail identities. This would grant access to external users and fail to restrict access to the organization's internal employees managed in Microsoft Entra ID.\nReferences\n1. Configure authentication for your agent in Copilot Studio\n2. Microsoft Entra ID authentication for Copilot Studio agents", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Select Authenticate with Microsoft Entra ID for the agent."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Enable Require Secure Access in the Web channel security."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Publish the agent as a public website experience."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Select Authenticate with personal account for the agent."}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 18, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is integrating an agent in Copilot Studio into a custom application by using the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK.\nThe company has the following requirements for the application:\n• The deployment workflow must follow a dependency driven sequence.\n• The agent must be published before configuration values are retrieved.\n• End-to-end validation must occur before production deployment.\nYou need to plan the deployment workflow to integrate the agent.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "", "type": "order", "items": ["Configure and enable the agent's target integration channel.", "Retrieve current agent configuration values.", "Deploy the web app to production.", "Implement the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK in the app using the agent configuration.", "Publish the agent.", "Validate the end-to-end conversation workflow in a test environment."], "answer": ["Publish the agent.", "Retrieve current agent configuration values.", "Configure and enable the agent's target integration channel.", "Implement the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK in the app using the agent configuration.", "Validate the end-to-end conversation workflow in a test environment.", "Deploy the web app to production."], "prompt": "Plan the deployment workflow to integrate a Copilot Studio agent into a custom application using the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK."}, {"id": 19, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent in Copilot Studio already exists in a development environment.\nYou need to prepare the agent so it can be moved to a production environment using the supported solution-based approach.\nYou need to create an exportable package that contains the existing agent.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Technical Justification\nE. Create a custom solution and add the existing agent to the solution.This is the mandatory first step. In the Power Platform ALM framework, only components contained within a custom unmanaged solution can be packaged for transport. Agents (copilots) created directly in the default environment solution or via the Copilot Studio portal are not automatically added to a specific custom solution. You must explicitly create a custom solution (unmanaged) in the development environment and use the \"Add existing\" functionality to include the agent and its required dependencies (topics, entities, connections, Power Automate flows). This ensures the solution layer captures the complete component graph required for a consistent import into the target environment.\nC. Export the unmanaged solution that contains the agent.Once the agent is added to the custom unmanaged solution, exporting that unmanaged solution produces the .zip package required for deployment. The Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) best practice dictates exporting the unmanaged solution from the source (dev) environment. This package contains the component definitions and metadata. Upon import into the target (prod) environment, the solution can be converted to managed (via solution upgrade or manual conversion) to enforce layering and prevent direct customization in production. Exporting a managed solution directly from dev is not the standard supported flow for initial promotion; managed solutions are typically artifacts produced by a build pipeline or during the import/upgrade process in downstream environments.\nWhy other options are incorrect:\nA. Remove agent topics directly from the solution to prevent export errors.Removing topics breaks the agent's functional integrity. Topics are core components of the agent; the solution export process automatically calculates and includes required dependencies. Manually removing them causes import failures or runtime errors in production due to missing logic.\nB. Convert the solution to a managed solution so it can be exported.You cannot \"convert\" a solution to managed in the source development environment for the purpose of export. Solutions are developed as unmanaged. Managed solutions are a destination state applied during import into a downstream environment (or via a build pipeline). Attempting to mark a solution as managed in dev prevents further customization and is not the supported mechanism for creating a transport package.\nD. Publish the agent to a channel and export the channel configuration.Channel configuration (e.g., Teams, Web Chat, Direct Line) defines runtime endpoints and authentication settings, not the agent's logic, topics, or data schema. Exporting channel config does not package the agent definition. Channels are typically configured per-environment post-deployment because authentication secrets (Client IDs/Secrets) and endpoint URLs differ between dev and prod.\nReferences\n1. Add an existing copilot to a solution – Microsoft Learn: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft- copilot-studio/add-copilot-to-solution\n2. Export and import solutions – Microsoft Learn (Power Platform ALM): https://learn.microsoft.com/en- us/powerapps/maker/data-platform/export-import-solutions", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Remove agent topics directly from the solution to prevent export errors."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Convert the solution to a managed solution so it can be exported."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Export the unmanaged solution that contains the agent."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Publish the agent to a channel and export the channel configuration."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Create a custom solution and add the existing agent to the solution."}], "answer": ["C", "E"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 20, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company wants to use content from a non-Microsoft enterprise knowledge base to ground agent responses. To meet the business needs, the knowledge source must meet the following requirements:\n• Support grounding from indexed data.\n• Use existing access controls from the source system.\n• Support read-only operations.\nYou need to determine the correct behavior to meet the requirements.\nWhich behavior meets each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Support grounding from indexed data.", "options": ["Retrieve indexed content", "Execute runtime operations.", "Retrieve live data at runtime."], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Use existing access controls from the source system.", "options": ["Respect existing permissions.", "Bypass permissions for faster retrieval.", "Respect existing permissions from agent authors."], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Support read-only operations.", "options": ["Prevent transactional updates.", "Allow authenticated write operations.", "Enable updates for enhanced performance."], "answer": 0}]}, {"id": 21, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "An agent uses a flow that calls an external service which can occasionally fail or time out.\nWhen a failure occurs, the agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Must not terminate silently.\n• Must send a notification containing the error details.\nYou need to configure the agent flow so that failures are handled in a controlled and predictable way.\nSolution: Retry the failing action without handling failure.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nThe proposed solution does not meet the stated goals. Configuring a \"Retry\" policy on an action (via Configure run after or the Retry Policy settings) instructs the flow engine to automatically re-attempt the action a specified number of times upon failure. However, this mechanism only delays the final failure state; it does not constitute \"handling\" the failure in the context of business logic requirements.\nSpecifically, this solution fails both requirements:\n1. Silent Termination Risk: If all retry attempts are exhausted, the action enters a Failed state. Without a parallel branch configured to run \"has failed\" (via Configure run after), the flow terminates at that point. While the flow run history records the error, the agent/execution context receives no programmatic handling—effectively terminating the business process silently from the agent's perspective.\n2. No Notification Logic: A retry policy contains no capability to execute auxiliary actions such as sending an email, posting a Teams message, or calling a logging API with the error details (result() ['error']). It merely pauses and re-executes the same HTTP request.\nWhy other approaches are required:To satisfy the requirements, the flow must implement a Scope or a specific Parallel Branch configured with Run after: \"has failed\" (or \"has timed out\"). Inside this branch, you must explicitly add actions to:\nCapture error details using expressions like result('ActionName') or actions('ActionName').outputs.body. Execute a notification action (e.g., Send email V2, Post message in Teams).\nOptionally, use terminate() with a specific status (e.g., Failed) to ensure the agent receives a controlled completion signal rather than an unhandled runtime error.\nReferences\nHandle errors and exceptions in cloud flows (Microsoft Learn)\nConfigure run after behavior (Microsoft Learn)", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 22, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "An agent uses a flow that calls an external service which can occasionally fail or time out.\nWhen a failure occurs, the agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Must not terminate silently.\n• Must send a notification containing the error details.\nYou need to configure the agent flow so that failures are handled in a controlled and predictable way.\nSolution: Configure run after conditions for failure paths.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nThe solution meets the goal because Configure run after conditions is the native Power Automate mechanism for implementing deterministic error handling logic directly within the flow topology. By setting the Run After configuration on a notification action (e.g., \"Post message in Teams\" or \"Send email\") to trigger on has failed and has timed out states of the external service call, the flow execution continues explicitly along a defined failure path rather than terminating silently. This satisfies the first requirement. Furthermore, the failure path action can utilize the result() function or actions('ActionName').outputs expressions to capture the error code, message, and stack trace from the failed scope, dynamically injecting these details into the notification payload to satisfy the second requirement. This approach is declarative, requires no custom code or external monitoring services, and adheres to the platform's \"design-time\" error handling best practices for cloud flows. The alternative answer (No) would imply the solution is insufficient, which is incorrect because Run After conditions are the specific, supported feature designed precisely for branching logic based on upstream action outcomes (Success, Failure, Timeout, Skipped) without terminating the run instance.\nReferences\n1. Handle errors and run-after behavior in Power Automate\n2. Use run after settings to configure error handling", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 23, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "An agent uses a flow that calls an external service which can occasionally fail or time out.\nWhen a failure occurs, the agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Must not terminate silently.\n• Must send a notification containing the error details.\nYou need to configure the agent flow so that failures are handled in a controlled and predictable way.\nSolution: Send a notification with relevant error details.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "Justification for Option A (Yes)\nThe solution meets the goal because it implements the standard Power Automate error handling pattern using Configure Run After settings. By adding a notification action (e.g., \"Send an email (V2)\", \"Post message in a chat or channel\", or \"Create a row in Dataverse\") immediately downstream of the external service call and configuring its Run After properties to trigger on 'has failed', 'has timed out', and 'has skipped', the flow achieves controlled failure management.\nThis configuration ensures:\n1. No Silent Termination: The flow execution continues into the notification branch instead of halting immediately on the external service error. The agent receives a successful flow completion status (or a handled error response) rather than an unhandled platform exception, allowing the agent to respond gracefully to the user.\n2. Error Detail Notification: The notification action can utilize expressions like result('ActionName') or actions('ActionName').outputs.errors to capture the specific HTTP status code, response body, and error message from the failed external service call, satisfying the requirement to send error details.\nWhy Option B (No) is Incorrect\nOption B would only be valid if the solution omitted the critical Configure Run After configuration or relied solely on the default \"is successful\" trigger. However, the solution statement \"Send a notification with relevant error details\" implicitly defines the design pattern of adding a compensating action configured for failure paths. In the context of Copilot Studio agent integration, this pattern is the prescribed method to surface backend failures to the orchestration layer without causing an unhandled agent crash or silent failure.\nReferences\nHandle errors and exceptions in Power Automate flows (Configure run after)\n§ Error handling in Copilot Studio agent flows", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 24, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to evaluate whether the current configuration decisions for the Blue Yonder Copilot agent comply with the company's security and governance policies.\nWhich compliance status should you assign to each configuration decision? To answer, move the appropriate compliance statuses to the correct configuration decisions. You may use each compliance status once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Compliant", "Non-compliant"], "slots": ["The agent allows anonymous access general inquiries such as baggage policies.", "The agent uses an open web search to answer questions beyond internal knowledge.", "The team relies only on default content filters without custom RAI instructions.", "The agent uses one shared admin credential for all internal data access."], "answer": ["Compliant", "Non-compliant", "Non-compliant", "Non-compliant"], "reuse": true, "prompt": "Assign a compliance status to each configuration decision for the Blue Yonder Copilot agent."}, {"id": 25, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to deploy the Blue Yonder Copilot agent to the public website and Microsoft Teams while ensuring compliance with the company's security and Responsible AI requirements.\nWhich two actions should you perform before making the agent available on both channels? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Technical Justification for Correct Options (A and D)\nOption A (Configure prompt modifications to enforce tone, disclaimers, and refusal behavior at the system level): This is mandated by the Compliance and security requirements which explicitly state: \"Prompt modifications must be added to enforce tone, disclaimers, and refusal behavior\" and \"Disclaimers must be applied consistently across all generative responses. Manual edits to individual topics must be avoided.\" Configuring these at the system (agent) level ensures consistent enforcement across the Web channel, Mobile app, and Microsoft Teams without relying on per-topic maintenance, satisfying the Responsible AI (RAI) compliance mandate for standardized generative behavior.\nOption D (Configure Power Platform DLP policies to restrict unauthorized data connectors): The Compliance and security requirements explicitly dictate: \"Power Platform DLP policies must be enforced to block unauthorized data flows.\" Since the agent integrates with internal systems (Reservation System via custom connector, Dynamics 365/Dataverse) and external APIs (Flight Status, Travel Advisory), DLP policies are the platform-native governance mechanism to prevent data leakage by restricting which connectors can be used in specific environments. This must be configured at the environment/tenant level before the agent is deployed to any channel to ensure the solution architecture adheres to data protection standards.\nWhy Other Options Are Less Suitable\nOption B (Manually add disclaimers to each topic before publishing): This directly violates the stated requirement: \"Manual edits to individual topics must be avoided.\" It introduces maintenance overhead, inconsistency risk across the omnichannel deployment (Web, Mobile, Teams), and fails to scale for generative answers which are not bound to specific authored topics.\nOption C (Embed the web channel and then rely on channel-level settings to enforce content moderation): Responsible AI content moderation filters are configured at the agent/system level, not the channel level. The requirements state: \"Responsible AI content moderation filters must be enabled\" as a platform constraint. Relying on channel settings would leave the Teams and Mobile channels unprotected and contradicts the requirement for consistent policy enforcement across all channels.\nOption E (Publish the agent and then enable Responsible AI filters individually for each channel): RAI filters and content moderation are global agent settings, not channel-specific configurations. Enabling them post- publishing per channel creates a compliance gap during the interim period and operational inconsistency. Security and compliance guardrails (DLP, RAI filters, Prompt modifications) must be validated and enabled before the agent is made available to end users on any channel.\nReferences\n1. Configure prompt modifications for generative answers in Microsoft Copilot Studio\n2. Data loss prevention (DLP) policies in Power Platform", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Configure prompt modifications to enforce tone, disclaimers, and refusal behavior at the system level."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Manually add disclaimers to each topic before publishing."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Embed the web channel and then rely on channel-level settings to enforce content moderation."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Configure Power Platform DLP policies to restrict unauthorized data connectors."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Publish the agent and then enable Responsible AI filters individually for each channel."}], "answer": ["A", "D"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 26, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to configure the Blue Yonder Copilot agent's responses in accordance with the company's content control and platform requirements.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "B. Configure prompt instructions that include disclaimer text.\nThe case study mandates that \"Disclaimers must be applied consistently across all generative responses\" and \"Manual edits to individual topics must be be avoided.\" Configuring prompt instructions (system-level prompt modifications) is the native Copilot Studio mechanism to inject mandatory content—such as disclaimers, tone directives, and refusal behaviors—into every generative response automatically, ensuring centralized governance and compliance without per-topic maintenance.\nC. Use Markdown syntax within response content.\nThe technical requirements explicitly state: \"Markdown must be used for formatting (e.g., bold, bullet points); HTML is not supported.\" This is a platform constraint requiring all formatted agent output to adhere to Markdown syntax to render correctly across the supported channels (web, mobile, Teams).\nWhy other options are incorrect:\nA and E violate the directive to avoid manual topic edits and ensure consistency; embedding or duplicating disclaimer text in individual topics creates maintenance overhead and drift risk.\nD directly contradicts the platform constraint prohibiting HTML formatting.\nReferences\n§ Copilot Studio - Configure generative AI prompt instructions\n§ Copilot Studio - Authoring content with Markdown", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Add required disclaimer text inside each individual topic."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Configure prompt instructions that include disclaimer text."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Use Markdown syntax within response content."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Insert HTML formatting directly into topic responses."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Duplicate disclaimer text across reusable topics."}], "answer": ["B", "C"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 27, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "An agent uses a flow that calls an external service which can occasionally fail or time out.\nWhen a failure occurs, the agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Must not terminate silently.\n• Must send a notification containing the error details.\nYou need to configure the agent flow so that failures are handled in a controlled and predictable way.\nSolution: Terminate the flow immediately on error.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "The solution of terminating the flow immediately on error does not meet the stated requirements. An abrupt termination would cause the agent to stop without any explicit indication of the problem, violating the requirement that it “must not terminate silently.” Moreover, terminating the flow would prevent the execution of downstream actions needed to compose and send a notification containing the error details, so the requirement to “send a notification containing the error details” cannot be satisfied.\nA proper error-handling strategy should include a scope-level Try/Catch (or a Parallel Branch with a Configure Run after setting on each action) that catches failures, then uses a Compose or Set variable action to capture the error context (e.g., outputs('HTTP')?['statusCode'], outputs('HTTP')?['body']?['message']), followed by a Send an email/v2 (or Teams/Post a message) action to deliver the notification. Finally, the flow can either continue with a fallback path or end with a Terminate action set to Succeeded or Failed only after the notification has been sent, ensuring the failure is handled in a controlled and predictable manner.\nWhy the other option is unsuitable\nOption A (Yes) would imply that immediate termination fulfills both requirements, which it does not; silent termination contradicts the need for explicit error reporting and prevents the notification step from executing.\nReferences\nMicrosoft Learn: Handle errors in Power Automate – https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/error- handling\nMicrosoft Learn: Configure run after settings for actions – https://learn.microsoft.com/power- automate/configure-run-after\nAnswer: B. No", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 28, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company requires an agent in Copilot Studio to answer questions using content stored in a third-party knowledge base system and reuse that content for knowledge grounding.\nThe agent must ground answers using indexed enterprise knowledge from a non-Microsoft system.\nYou need to choose the correct integration approach.\nWhat should you use?", "expl": "§ Justification\nOption D – Add a Copilot Connector knowledge source: Copilot Studio provides a Connector knowledge source that can ingest data from third-party systems via OData, REST, or SharePoint-like endpoints. When configured, the connector securely indexes the external content and makes it available for knowledge grounding directly within the Copilot runtime. This approach satisfies the requirement to reuse the same bounded content for multiple turns without needing to invoke an external API on every turn, provides built-in authorization and refresh policies, and is the officially supported way to surface non-Microsoft knowledge in Copilot Studio.\nWhy Option A is unsuitable: Calling the third-party API from a topic for each question works, but it incurs latency, duplicated calls, and lacks the built-in indexing and caching that Copilot’s knowledge grounding provides. It also bypasses the platform’s security model (e.g., scoped permissions, refresh schedules) and forces the developer to handle pagination, retry logic, and response shaping manually.\nWhy Option B is unsuitable: Adding a “Copilot Studio tool as a real-time knowledge source” is not a defined capability; tools in Copilot are actions (flows, Power Automate, or custom code) that execute logic, not passive data indexes. A tool cannot be used as a knowledge source for grounding without additional wrapper logic, making this option ambiguous and unsupported.\nWhy Option C is unsuitable: Adding a Copilot Studio tool and invoking it from a topic is appropriate for executing business logic or calling APIs, but it does not provide the persistent, indexed knowledge base needed for grounding. Each invocation would still require a live call to the external system, offering no advantage over Option A and lacking the connector’s refresh and authorization features.\nConclusion: The Copilot Connector knowledge source (Option D) is the only method that natively enables secure, indexed, and reusable grounding of external content within Copilot Studio.\nReferences\nMicrosoft Learn: Add a connector knowledge source in Copilot Studio – https://learn.microsoft.com/copilot- studio/knowledge-sources/connector-knowledge-source\nMicrosoft Learn: Ground your copilot with external data – https://learn.microsoft.com/copilot- studio/grounding/external-data-grounding", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Call the third-party API from a topic for each question."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Add a Copilot Studio tool as a real-time knowledge source."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Add a Copilot Studio tool and call it from a topic."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add a Copilot connector knowledge source for authorized content."}], "answer": ["D"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 29, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses a supported Power Platform connector to provide real-time access to enterprise records for knowledge grounding in an agent in Copilot Studio.\nTo meet the business needs, the knowledge source must provide the following functionality:\n• Avoid copying enterprise data into an index.\n• Keep source-system access controls enforced.\n• Access data by calling the system at runtime.\nYou need to configure the connector knowledge source.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "To meet the requirements for real-time access to enterprise records in Copilot Studio while maintaining data sovereignty and security:* Avoid copying enterprise data into an index: Configure Search endpoint to point to the connector/API rather than an indexed data store.* Keep source-system access controls enforced: Use Connector authentication (or User-delegated authentication) to ensure the system respects the user's specific permissions.* Access data by calling the system at runtime: Configure Action-based knowledge (which utilizes Power Platform connectors to fetch data dynamically at runtime).Answer:* Avoid copying enterprise data into an index: Connector* Keep source-system access controls enforced: Connector authentication* Access data by calling the system at runtime: Action-based knowledge", "type": "match", "items": ["Use real-time connector.", "Use existing permissions.", "Retrieve data without indexing.", "Add Azure AI Search as a knowledge source."], "slots": ["Avoid copying enterprise data into an index.", "Keep source-system access controls enforced.", "Access data by calling the system at runtime."], "answer": ["Retrieve data without indexing.", "Use existing permissions.", "Use real-time connector."], "reuse": true, "note": "No answer image; answer derived from the stem answer text (Connector / Connector authentication / Action-based knowledge) mapped to closest options."}, {"id": 30, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A tenant administrator configures a Copilot connector that indexes content from an external knowledge system into Microsoft Graph.\nA builder is building an agent that must:\n• Use the indexed enterprise knowledge during answer generation.\n• Avoid duplicating or manually uploading the same content.\n• Avoid invoking the system as a transactional tool.\nYou need to configure the agent so that responses are grounded in the authorized knowledge sources.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Why option A is correct\nThe tenant has already created a Copilot connector that indexes the external knowledge source into Microsoft Graph. Once indexed, the content is available as an enterprise knowledge source that can be added directly to an agent’s knowledge base via the “Add knowledge source” option in Copilot Studio.\nWhen the source is added in this way, Copilot Studio automatically uses the indexed Graph data for grounded answer generation without requiring additional data movement, duplication, or manual uploads. This satisfies the three requirements:\n1. The agent uses the indexed enterprise knowledge during generation.\n2. No duplicate copies are created; the agent reads from the shared Graph index.\n3. No transactional tool invocation is needed because the knowledge is consumed as a static data source, not as an API call.\nWhy the other options are less suitable\nOption B – Create a new custom connector inside the agent configuration:\nBuilding another connector would duplicate the indexing effort, require additional setup, and would likely create a second copy of the same content, violating the “avoid duplicating” rule. Moreover, a custom connector would typically be invoked as a tool, conflicting with the requirement to avoid transactional invocation.\nOption C – Add the connector as a tool and invoke it from a topic:\nTreating the connector as a tool forces the agent to call it at runtime (e.g., via an API or action), which constitutes a transactional interaction. This would also bypass the built-in grounding mechanism that uses Graph-indexed knowledge, potentially leading to latency and extra permission management overhead.\nOption D – Upload documents from the external system directly into the agent’s file knowledge store:\nManual uploading creates a separate copy of the content inside the agent’s file store, leading to duplication and maintenance drift. It also bypasses the authorized, centrally-managed Graph index, making it harder to enforce security and compliance policies that the administrator configured for the connector.\nReferences\nMicrosoft Learn: Add knowledge sources to your agent in Copilot Studio – https://learn.microsoft.com/copilot- studio/knowledge-sources\nMicrosoft Learn: Configure Copilot connectors for Microsoft Graph – https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft- 365-copilot/connectors/configure-graph-connector", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Add the configured enterprise knowledge source to the agent’s knowledge."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Create a new custom connector inside the agent configuration."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Add the connector as a tool and invoke it from a topic."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Upload documents from the external system directly into the agent’s file knowledge store."}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 31, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A solution containing an agent in Copilot Studio is deployed to multiple environments that require different configuration values.\nConfiguration values must vary by environment without modifying the solution contents.\nConfiguration values must meet the following requirements:\n• Allow values to be set during import.\n• Exclude values from the agent definition.\n• Run a dedicated agent instance for each individual environment.\nYou need to associate each requirement with the configuration approach.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "* Allow values to be set during import: Environment variables* Exclude values from the agent definition: Environment variables* Run a dedicated agent instance for each individual environment: Environment-specific configuration", "type": "match", "items": ["Use channel-specific settings.", "Define environment variables in the solution.", "Publish distinct agent versions for each environment.", "Hardcode the configuration values in the agent configuration."], "slots": ["Allow values to be set during import.", "Exclude values from the agent definition.", "Run a dedicated agent instance for each individual environment."], "answer": ["Define environment variables in the solution.", "Define environment variables in the solution.", "Publish distinct agent versions for each environment."], "reuse": true, "note": "No answer image; first two from stem text; third mapped from text 'Environment-specific configuration' to closest option (uncertain)."}, {"id": 32, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team uses environment variables to separate configuration from an agent so that the same solution can be moved between environments.\nThe team requires the following scenarios to be addressed:\n• An administrator updates a sensitive configuration value and needs the change to take effect at runtime without republishing the agent.\n• A builder needs to confirm a configuration value but must not be able to modify it from Copilot Studio.\n• An administrator updates a non-secret configuration value and needs the updated value to be used by the published agent at runtime.\nYou need to apply the correct handling behavior for different environment-variable scenarios.\nWhich behavior should you apply for each scenario? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Update a sensitive configuration value; the change takes effect at runtime without republishing the agent.", "options": ["Republish the agent to apply the update.", "Use a secret environment variable type to allow for runtime resolution.", "Make the update directly in Copilot Studio."], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Confirm a configuration value but do not modify it from Copilot Studio.", "options": ["Environment variables are stored as topic variables.", "Environment variables are read-only in Copilot Studio.", "Environment variables can be edited by any builder in Copilot Studio."], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Update a non-secret configuration value and the updated value needs to be used by the published agent at runtime.", "options": ["The value can only be changed by hardcoding it into the agent.", "The agent must be republished for the updated value to be effective at runtime.", "The agent automatically pulls the updated Current Value from the environment at the start of every session."], "answer": 2}], "prompt": "Apply the correct handling behavior for different environment-variable scenarios."}, {"id": 33, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is configuring an agent flow that uses multiple action types to perform operations and process data. The flow must be configured as follows:\n• An HTTP action must connect to an external service.\n• A data action must reference the correct data to process.\nYou need to configure the settings for each action type.\nWhich setting should you configure for each action? To answer, move the appropriate configuration settings to the correct actions. You may use each configuration setting once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Input and mapping", "Table relationships and keys", "Endpoint and authentication", "Connection timeout and retry policy"], "slots": ["HTTP action", "Data action"], "answer": ["Endpoint and authentication", "Input and mapping"], "reuse": true}, {"id": 34, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company has agents in Copilot Studio.\nThe company needs to prevent the agents from using disallowed connectors and insecure data flows.\nAdministrators must be able to enforce the governance controls to prevent and report violations.\nYou need to enforce governance controls for agents.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Justification for Correct Answer: D (Configure data policies)\nNative Governance Enforcement: Data policies in Copilot Studio (configured via the Power Platform Admin Center) are the designated mechanism for enforcing Data Loss Prevention (DLP) rules at the environment or tenant level. They explicitly control which connectors are classified as Business, Non-Business, or Blocked, directly preventing agents from utilizing disallowed connectors and securing data flows between services. Administrative Control & Reporting: These policies provide the administrative interface to define, enforce, and audit connector usage compliance. Violations are surfaced in the Power Platform Center of Excellence (CoE) toolkit and admin center analytics, satisfying the requirement for reporting violations.\nWhy Other Options Are Less Suitable:\nA. Configure Application Insights: This captures operational telemetry (performance, errors, user interactions) for debugging and monitoring. It lacks the policy engine to prevent connector usage or enforce DLP classifications; it is observability, not governance.\nB. Review security FAQs: This is a passive educational activity. It provides knowledge of default protections (e.g., encryption, RBAC) but offers no technical mechanism to enforce custom connector restrictions or data flow controls.\nC. Review agent analytics: Analytics provide retrospective usage metrics (sessions, outcomes, billing). While they may surface risky connector usage after the fact, they possess no enforcement capability to prevent the violation from occurring.\nReferences\n1. Data loss prevention (DLP) policies in Power Platform\n2. Governance and administration for Copilot Studio", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Configure Application Insights to collect runtime telemetry."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Review security FAQs to understand default protections."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Review agent analytics to detect risky connector usage."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Configure data policies."}], "answer": ["D"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 35, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is designing an agent flow that includes human in the loop stages during execution.\nThe company requires that the agent flow perform the following actions:\n• Must wait for a human decision before it resumes.\n• Must collect information and then continue execution.\nYou need to configure the flow behavior.\nWhich flow behavior should you configure for each stage? To answer, move the appropriate flow behaviors to the correct stages. You may use each flow behavior once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Wait for a response and capture inputs.", "Send a request and continue immediately.", "Send a notification for approval and continue.", "Wait for a response and branch based on the decision."], "slots": ["Approval", "Information collection"], "answer": ["Wait for a response and branch based on the decision.", "Wait for a response and capture inputs."], "prompt": "Select the flow behavior for each human in the loop stage."}, {"id": 36, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is building an agent topic in Copilot Studio. The agent topic uses a generative answers node to respond to user questions based on authorized knowledge sources.\nTo meet the business needs, the generative answers node must meet the following requirements:\n• Use the user’s message in the generative answers node.\n• Scope the topic node to query only designated data.\n• Restrict responses to approved enterprise data.\nYou need to configure the generative answers node to meet the requirements.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Select Search only selected sources.", "Select Activity.Text in the Input field.", "Enable generative orchestration for the agent.", "Clear Allow the AI to use its own general knowledge."], "slots": ["Use the user's message in the generative answers node.", "Scope the topic node to query only designated data.", "Restrict responses to approved enterprise data."], "answer": ["Select Activity.Text in the Input field.", "Select Search only selected sources.", "Clear Allow the AI to use its own general knowledge."]}, {"id": 37, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is building an agent topic in Copilot Studio.\nThe topic must meet the following requirements:\n• Collect structured user input in a single step.\n• Store the submitted values for use later in the conversation.\nYou need to configure the topic based on the requirements.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Technical Justification\nWhy C and D are Correct:\nD. Add an Ask with Adaptive Card node to the topic: This is the designated node type in Copilot Studio for presenting an Adaptive Card and pausing the conversation flow to wait for user input. It natively supports the Input.* elements defined in the card payload and automatically maps the submitted JSON payload to topic variables upon receipt of the Action.Submit payload. This satisfies the requirement to collect structured input in a single conversational step and persist the values for later use in the dialogue context.\nC. Add input fields to the card and include an Action.Submit button: The Input.* elements (e.g., Input.Text, Input.ChoiceSet, Input.Date) define the structured data schema collected from the user. The Action.Submit button is the mandatory mechanism that triggers the submission of the input values back to the Copilot Studio runtime. Without this specific action type, the Ask with Adaptive Card node would not receive the payload required to populate the output variables.\nWhy A, B, and E are Incorrect:\nA. Use the latest Adaptive Card schema version in all channels to ensure full feature support: This is a general development best practice, not a configuration action required to implement the specific functional requirements. Furthermore, channel support for Adaptive Card versions varies (e.g., Teams vs. Web Chat); enforcing the absolute latest schema version across all channels often results in rendering failures or fallback behaviors rather than guaranteed feature support.\nB. Paste the card JSON into a Message node to collect the user’s response: A standard Message node is designed for one-way communication (output only). It renders the Adaptive Card but does not pause the topic execution or provide a mechanism to capture the Invoke activity returned by Action.Submit. Consequently, the submitted values cannot be stored in variables for later use.\nE. Use Action.Execute for the button so the card can run commands across channels: Action.Execute is designed for Universal Actions (e.g., triggering a Bot Framework skill or specific backend logic) and requires specific channel support and bot configuration (Invoke activities). It does not natively submit form data back to the Ask with Adaptive Card node for variable assignment in Copilot Studio. Action.Submit is the correct, standard schema action for form submission within the Copilot Studio authoring canvas.\nReferences\n1. Use Adaptive Cards in Copilot Studio (Microsoft Learn)\n2. Ask with Adaptive Card node documentation (Microsoft Learn)", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Use the latest Adaptive Card schema version in all channels to ensure full feature support."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Paste the card JSON into a Message node to collect the user’s response."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Add input fields to the card and include an Action.Submit button."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add an Ask with Adaptive Card node to the topic."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Use Action.Execute for the button so the card can run commands across channels."}], "answer": ["C", "D"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 38, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to evaluate whether the current configuration decisions for the Blue Yonder Copilot agent comply with the company's security and governance policies.\nWhich compliance status should you assign to each configuration decision? To answer, move the appropriate compliance statuses to the correct configuration decisions. You may use each compliance status once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "(Compliant): Baggage policies are public informational data. Allowing unauthenticated (anonymous) access for standard customer service FAQs is completely secure and follows standard architectural practices.\n(Non-compliant): Utilizing ungrounded open web searches introduces severe risks of hallucinations and off- policy responses. This directly violates the case study's core mandate that generative answers must be securely constrained.\n(Non-compliant): As established in the safety deployment requirements, custom system instructions (RAI prompts) are mandatory to enforce the specific tone, safety compliance, and operational boundaries of the company. Relying solely on default settings is insufficient.\n(Non-compliant): Employing a single shared administrator credential for all internal backend access breaches basic Zero Trust security guidelines and data privacy principles (Least Privilege). It allows users to potentially bypass data access controls or view internal data they are not authorized to see.", "type": "match", "items": ["Compliant", "Non-compliant"], "slots": ["The agent allows anonymous access general inquiries such as baggage policies.", "The agent uses an open web search to answer questions beyond internal knowledge.", "The team relies only on default content filters without custom RAI instructions.", "The agent uses one shared admin credential for all internal data access."], "answer": ["Compliant", "Non-compliant", "Non-compliant", "Non-compliant"], "reuse": true}, {"id": 39, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to deploy the Blue Yonder Copilot agent to the public website and Microsoft Teams while ensuring compliance with the company's security and Responsible AI requirements.\nWhich two actions should you perform before making the agent available on both channels? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "A. Configure prompt modifications to enforce tone, disclaimers, and refusal behavior at the system level. D. Configure Power Platform DLP policies to restrict unauthorized data connectors.\nAction A (Responsible AI Compliance): Relying on system-level prompt modifications ensures that regardless of the topic or channel (Public Web or Microsoft Teams), the generative AI model inherently follows enterprise guardrails regarding tone, required disclaimers, and refusal bounds.\nAction D (Security Compliance): Power Platform Data Loss Prevention (DLP) policies act as the security firewall. They control and block unauthorized connectors at the environment tier, preventing the copilot from accidentally leaking or retrieving data through non-compliant external channels.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nB is an operational nightmare; manually updating individual topics fails to secure globally generated AI answers.\nC & E are architectural misconceptions; content moderation and Responsible AI filters in Microsoft Copilot Studio are centrally managed at the agent/system tier before publication, rather than handled piece-by-piece on distinct channels.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Configure prompt modifications to enforce tone, disclaimers, and refusal behavior at the system level."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Manually add disclaimers to each topic before publishing."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Embed the web channel and then rely on channel-level settings to enforce content moderation."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Configure Power Platform DLP policies to restrict unauthorized data connectors."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Publish the agent and then enable Responsible AI filters individually for each channel."}], "answer": ["A", "D"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 40, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to configure the Blue Yonder Copilot agent's responses in accordance with the company's content control and platform requirements.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "B. Configure prompt instructions that include disclaimer text.\nC. Use Markdown syntax within response content.\nAction B (Content Control Compliance):\n​Adding required disclaimer instructions at the system prompt level ensures that the underlying generative model consistently appends or enforces the necessary legal and safety compliance guidelines globally, preventing the need to manually update static topics.\nAction C (Platform Requirement Compliance):\nMicrosoft Copilot Studio natively supports and recommends Markdown syntax within response message nodes. It ensures content renders consistently (such as bolding, lists, and hyperlinks) across both the public website channel and Microsoft Teams.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Add required disclaimer text inside each individual topic."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Configure prompt instructions that include disclaimer text."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Use Markdown syntax within response content."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Insert HTML formatting directly into topic responses."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Duplicate disclaimer text across reusable topics."}], "answer": ["B", "C"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 41, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to configure the agent in Copilot Studio to use internal and external partner knowledge sources to answer user questions about the airline services.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "A. Use a Microsoft Graph connector to index the partner's travel advisory content.\nFor structured or third-party partner data (like external travel advisory sites or repositories), a Microsoft Graph connector is the ideal modern solution. It indexes the external data directly into the enterprise search index, allowing Copilot Studio to safely ingest and query partner information without exposing it to the open web.\nD. Add the internal policy documents as a knowledge source.\nAdding the internal policy documents directly as native knowledge sources in Copilot Studio allows the agent to immediately use Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to ground answers based only on approved, private company content.\nWhy the Other Options are Incorrect\nB. Write individual Q&A pairs for each document: Manually writing hardcoded Q&A pairs for entire sets of documents is highly inefficient, does not scale, and defeats the purpose of using Copilot's automated, generative AI retrieval features.\nC. Enable unrestricted web search: Turning on a broad, unrestricted web search will crawl public search engine data (like Bing). This can lead to unverified or incorrect generic answers rather than pulling exclusively from the verified external partner knowledge you need.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Use a Microsoft Graph connector to index the partner's travel advisory content."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Write individual Q&A pairs for each document as separate topics."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Enable unrestricted web search for the agent."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add the internal policy documents as a knowledge source."}], "answer": ["A", "D"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 42, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to ensure that every AI-generated response from the agent in Copilot Studio includes a disclaimer that complies with the company's security and governance policies.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "B. Add a prompt modification in the generative answers node settings.\nC. Create a disclaimer topic that always runs first.\nWhy These Actions are Correct\n1. Add a prompt modification in the generative answers node settings (Option B):\n2. By modifying the custom instructions (prompt engineering) within the Generative Answers node, you can explicitly instruct the large language model (LLM) to automatically append the required\ngovernance and security disclaimer text to the end of every answer it dynamically synthesizes from your data sources.\n3. Create a disclaimer topic that always runs first (Option C):\n4. Creating a dedicated topic with an orchestration trigger (such as the system trigger \"An AI- generated response is about to be sent\") acts as a universal interception layer. This ensures that regardless of which topic or knowledge base was queried, the disclaimer logic evaluates and\nappends the warning message uniformly before sending the payload out to the user.\nWhy the Other Options are Incorrect\nA. Add a greeting message: A greeting message is only shown once at the absolute beginning of a user session. It does not fulfill the compliance mandate that requires it on every individual AI-generated response. D. Disable generative answers: While this ensures no unapproved answers are sent, it completely removes the conversational AI capability from the agent, which violates the premise of handling AI-generated responses.\nE. Edit every topic's first message: This is an administrative nightmare that is highly prone to human error, does not scale, and only covers hardcoded pre-authored messages rather than dynamically generated conversational responses.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Add a greeting message that includes the disclaimer."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Add a prompt modification in the generative answers node settings."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Create a disclaimer topic that always runs first."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Disable generative answers and use only pre-authored responses."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Edit every topic's first message to include the disclaimer."}], "answer": ["B", "C"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 43, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to determine which authentication model should be applied to each Blue Yonder Copilot interaction scenario to comply with the company's security and governance requirements.\nWhich authentication requirement should you apply to each scenario? To answer, move the appropriate authentication requirements to the correct user scenarios. You may use each authentication requirement once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Baggage allowance is general, non-sensitive public information. Customers should be able to view these details directly on a public website without creating an account or logging in, making Allow anonymous access the appropriate choice.\nReviewing personal upcoming trips involves accessing Personally Identifiable Information (PII) and private booking records. To ensure data privacy, the copilot must securely identify who the user is by forcing a customer sign-in using an account.\nSince an internal customer support representative is accessing the system within the company’s internal tenant (Microsoft Teams) to view sensitive loyalty customer profiles, they must authenticate securely via the enterprise identity provider system, which is Microsoft Entra ID.", "type": "match", "items": ["Allow anonymous access.", "Require customer sign-in using an account.", "Require internal agent sign-in using Microsoft Entra ID.", "Use agent author credentials for authenticated data calls."], "slots": ["A customer asks, \"What is the baggage allowance for international flights?\"", "A customer asks, \"Can I review my upcoming trips?\"", "An internal support agent accesses the Copilot agent from Microsoft Teams to look up a loyalty member's profile."], "answer": ["Allow anonymous access.", "Require customer sign-in using an account.", "Require internal agent sign-in using Microsoft Entra ID."], "reuse": true, "prompt": "Determine which authentication requirement applies to each Blue Yonder Copilot interaction scenario. Items may be used once, more than once, or not at all."}, {"id": 44, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company has an existing custom connector that is approved and available in the environment. A builder wants an agent in Copilot Studio to call the connector during a conversation to retrieve information from an internal system. To meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• The agent must make the connector available for topic steps.\n• The agent must run the connector call with a valid connection.\n• The connector call must receive the required input values at runtime.\nYou need to configure the agent so the custom connector can be used as a tool.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "In Microsoft Copilot Studio, actions or connectors must be registered or enabled within the bot's plugin architecture to become visible. Choosing Add the connector as a tool in the agent allows authors to select and execute the custom connector inside regular topic authoring canvas steps.\nConnectors are definitions of APIs, but they cannot authenticate or talk to a backend without a concrete security context. You must Create or reuse a connection for the connector to establish valid authentication credentials (like OAuth or API keys) so the engine can execute the request successfully.\nFor a custom connector tool to dynamically fetch accurate data (such as searching for a flight using a specific booking ID), data collected from the user during the chat session must pass into the API request. You Map topic variables to the tool inputs to satisfy this requirement.", "type": "match", "items": ["Map topic variables to the tool inputs.", "Add the connector as a tool in the agent.", "Add the connector as a knowledge source.", "Create or reuse a connection for the connector."], "slots": ["The agent must make the connector available for topic steps.", "The agent must run the connector call with a valid connection.", "The connector call must receive the required input values at runtime."], "answer": ["Add the connector as a tool in the agent.", "Create or reuse a connection for the connector.", "Map topic variables to the tool inputs."], "reuse": true}, {"id": 45, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company needs to ground an agent in Copilot Studio answers using live enterprise data from a supported system via a Microsoft Power Platform connector.\nTo meet the business needs, the builder must meet the following requirements:\n• Authenticate using a valid connection.\n• Add the connector as a real-time knowledge source.\n• Select the tables for knowledge grounding.\nYou need to add a Power Platform connector as a real-time knowledge source for the agent.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "Select Add knowledge from the agent Knowledge experience.\nStart from the agent configuration area. The Knowledge experience is where you add external data sources. Select the real-time connector from the Add knowledge dialog.\nIn the Add knowledge dialog, choose the appropriate connector type. For REST API data, select the real-time connector option so the agent can retrieve information dynamically rather than storing a static copy.\nSelect Sign in and create a new connection or reuse an existing connection.\nThe connector requires authentication. You either create a new connection by providing credentials/details or select an existing connection that already has the required authentication settings.\nSelect the tables to include as the knowledge source.\nAfter the connection is established, the available data objects/tables exposed by the connector are displayed. Choose which tables or data entities should be available to the agent as knowledge.\nSelect Add to agent to complete the connection.\n​Confirm the selection and attach the configured knowledge source to the agent. The agent can now use the connected REST API data when responding to user queries.", "type": "order", "items": ["Select Sign in and create a new connection or reuse an existing connection.", "Select the tables to include as the knowledge source.", "Select Add knowledge from the agent Knowledge experience.", "Select the real-time connector from the Add knowledge dialog.", "Select Add to agent to complete the connection."], "answer": ["Select Add knowledge from the agent Knowledge experience.", "Select the real-time connector from the Add knowledge dialog.", "Select Sign in and create a new connection or reuse an existing connection.", "Select the tables to include as the knowledge source.", "Select Add to agent to complete the connection."]}, {"id": 46, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You deploy an agent in Microsoft Copilot Studio that triggers a cloud flow to update customer records in Microsoft Dataverse.\nYou observe the following after deploying the agent:\n• Several runs fail due to a temporary authentication issue.\n• The flow continues to trigger and generate additional failed runs.\nYou must:\n• Stop additional failed executions while troubleshooting.\n• Process a failed execution after resolving the authentication issue.\n• Validate if the most recent executions succeeded immediately after resolution.\nYou need to use the appropriate monitoring and management actions to restore normal flow operation and validate successful execution.\nWhich action should you perform for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Disable the flow.\nDisabling the flow prevents the trigger from firing and stops any new runs from being created while you investigate the issue. Deleting the flow is permanent, canceling only affects one run, and modifying the trigger condition is unrelated to temporarily stopping executions.\nResubmit the run.\nAfter fixing the authentication problem (for example, updating the connection), use Resubmit the run to execute the failed flow instance again. This retries the same inputs without waiting for a new trigger event. Review the Activity history.\nThe Activity history (Run history) displays the latest flow executions, including their status (Succeeded, Failed, Running), timestamps, and error details. This is the quickest way to verify whether the issue has been resolved. Analytics provides aggregated trends rather than immediate run-by-run validation.", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Stop additional failed executions while troubleshooting.", "options": ["Delete the flow.", "Disable the flow.", "Cancel the most recent run.", "Modify the trigger condition."], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Process a failed execution after resolving the authentication issue.", "options": ["Restart the flow.", "Refresh the flow.", "Resubmit the run.", "Recreate the trigger."], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Validate if the most recent executions succeeded immediately after resolution.", "options": ["Republish the flow.", "Review the Activity history.", "Export the flow to a solution.", "Inspect the Analytics summary."], "answer": 1}]}, {"id": 47, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is preparing an agent flow so that it can be invoked by an agent during conversations.\nThe agent flow must meet the following requirements:\n• The agent must be able to trigger the flow.\n• The agent flow must be verified.\nYou need to prepare an agent flow so that it can be used by the agent.\nIn which order should you perform the actions to prepare the agent flow? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "1. Create the artifact: You begin inside your Power Platform environment by creating a new automated or instant cloud flow package/artifact designated to handle your specialized automation logic.\n2. Configure the invocation trigger: Next, you define the entry point—typically using the \"Run a flow from Copilot\" trigger node—where you declare exactly what input variables, types, and schemas the agent must provide when invoking the flow.\n3. Configure the actions: With the input parameters established, you construct the internal business logic by adding connector blocks, data operations, variables, or API calls that execute the intended process.\n4. Validate behavior: Before publishing, you perform runtime tests or debug cycles within the flow builder canvas using mock or representative parameters to verify that the steps execute smoothly and output data correctly.\n5. Make available for usage: Once verified, you save, turn on, or share the flow so it can be successfully discovered, mapped, and executed inside Copilot Studio as an operational tool.", "type": "order", "items": ["Configure the invocation trigger that allows the agent to call the agent flow.", "Make the agent flow available for agent usage.", "Validate the agent flow behavior with representative test runs.", "Configure the actions that implement the required behavior.", "Create a new agent flow artifact in the environment."], "answer": ["Create a new agent flow artifact in the environment.", "Configure the invocation trigger that allows the agent to call the agent flow.", "Configure the actions that implement the required behavior.", "Validate the agent flow behavior with representative test runs.", "Make the agent flow available for agent usage."]}, {"id": 48, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent flow that occasionally requires human input before continuing execution.\nSome automated actions must pause until a human provides a decision or additional information. The flow must be configured to:\n• Capture a human response for use in later steps.\n• Continue processing within the same flow run after the response is submitted.\n• Wait for a manual decision before proceeding.\nYou need to configure a human-in-the-loop agent flow.\nWhich setting should you configure for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Use the action's response outputs in subsequent steps.\nWhy it fits: \"Downstream logic\" in an automated cloud workflow refers to any action cards or condition evaluations that execute later in the sequence. To pass a choice made by a person (such as an approval outcome or typed remarks) further down the line, you must reference the dynamic outputs produced by that specific approval or response action.\n2. Allow the action to wait for and return a response before continuing.\nWhy it fits: By default, asynchronous approval actions inside Power Automate pause the execution thread of that specific, active flow run. By enabling the step to wait for and return a response, the exact same workflow instance goes into a dormant state and safely wakes back up to finish its remaining actions the moment the reviewer submits their decision.\n3. Add a human approval action.\nWhy it fits: The Power Automate Approvals connector is engineered specifically to implement human-in-the- loop validation checkpoints. It generates a formal, auditable task envelope via Teams, Email, or the Power Automate portal, forcing the cloud flow execution engine to halt completely until an authorized user actively clicks a decision button.", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Capture a human response for use in downstream logic.", "options": ["Send a notification to a user.", "Use the action's response outputs in subsequent steps.", "Enable analytics logging for the response."], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Continue processing within the same flow run after a response is submitted.", "options": ["Republish the flow after the decision is submitted.", "Trigger a new flow run when a response is received.", "Allow the action to wait for and return a response before continuing."], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Wait for a manual decision before proceeding.", "options": ["Add a human approval action.", "Add a manual trigger to the flow.", "Send a message that asks for input."], "answer": 0}], "prompt": "Configure a human-in-the-loop agent flow."}, {"id": 49, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent that invokes an agent flow to exchange information during a conversation.\nThe company requires that the agent send data into the flow and receive structured results back from the same flow run. To support this business need, the flow must be configured to do the following:\n• Capture the data provided by the agent.\n• Return data results to the agent.\nYou need to configure the flow so that it can exchange data with the agent.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Add a text input parameter.\nWhy it fits: When an agent (Copilot) invokes an automated cloud flow to offload processing logic, it needs a way to hand off variables collected from the user conversation (such as names, account numbers, or text commands) to the flow. Configuring an explicit input parameter directly on the flow's invocation trigger establishes the required inbound interface slots to accept this incoming data.\n2. Add a text output parameter.\nWhy it fits: Once the automated flow concludes its backend processing, data operations, or API lookups, it must send the finalized results back into the conversation context. Defining a text output parameter on the flow's termination response block creates the outbound interface payload that the agent can read and reuse in its subsequent conversation topics.", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Capture the data provided by the agent.", "options": ["Use an internal variable.", "Add a text input parameter.", "Store values only in the topic."], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Return data results to the agent.", "options": ["Log values to run history.", "Store values only in the flow.", "Add a text output parameter."], "answer": 2}]}, {"id": 50, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You are configuring an agent in Copilot Studio for an organization. The organization uses Microsoft Dataverse, Dynamics 365, and SharePoint Online.\nThe agent must retrieve information from enterprise systems and internal documents. The agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Must retrieve the current balance of a customer account stored in Dataverse.\nMust retrieve the real-time shipping status of an order that updates throughout the day in Dynamics 365. Must answer questions based on the content of a static PDF policy document.\nMust provide a welcome message that does NOT depend on enterprise data.\nYou need to configure the agent to meet the requirements.\nHow should the agent retrieve the data? To answer, move the appropriate solutions to the correct requirements. You may use each solution once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1 & 2. Connect to a Power Platform connector.\nWhy it fits: Both Microsoft Dataverse and Dynamics 365 are transactional enterprise data stores. When an agent needs to pull precise, transactional variables (like an account balance) or track highly transient information that changes continuously throughout the day (like a shipping status), it cannot rely on scheduled search indexing. Instead, it must utilize a Power Platform connector to run a direct, real-time query against the source system at that exact moment in the conversation.\n3. Upload a file to Agents Knowledge Base.\nWhy it fits: Static documents (such as employee handbooks, corporate guidelines, or PDF policy manuals) contain unstructured informational text that doesn't change frequently. Uploading the PDF directly into the agent's Knowledge Base allows the built-in system to parse, chunk, and index the file so the foundational AI model can reference it for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG).\n4. Create a manual response in the topic.\nWhy it fits: Simple conversational steps like greetings, introductions, or fixed welcome messages do not require external databases, AI search crawls, or live data logic. Standardizing this greeting as a manual message node within a topic ensures a quick, uniform user experience completely independent of enterprise data pipelines.", "type": "match", "items": ["Create a manual response in the topic.", "Connect to a Power Platform connector.", "Upload a file to Agents Knowledge Base.", "Configure a generative answer without enterprise data."], "slots": ["Retrieve the current balance of a customer account stored in Dataverse.", "Retrieve the real-time shipping status of an order that updates throughout the day in Dynamics 365.", "Answer questions based on the content of a static PDF policy document.", "Provide a welcome message that does NOT depend on enterprise data."], "answer": ["Connect to a Power Platform connector.", "Connect to a Power Platform connector.", "Upload a file to Agents Knowledge Base.", "Create a manual response in the topic."], "reuse": true}, {"id": 51, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent calls a flow. The agent requires structured output values to be returned.\nThe agent receives unexpected or empty values.\nYou need to configure the agent so that data is exchanged correctly between the agent and the flow.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "A. Validate parameter definitions.\nAligning Mapped Variables: When a Copilot Studio agent calls a Power Automate flow, data exchange relies strictly on a contract of input and output variables. If the agent receives unexpected or empty values, it is typically because the parameter names, case sensitivity, or data types (e.g., text, number, boolean) defined in the flow's output action mismatch what the agent's calling node expects. Validating and matching these parameter definitions ensures data maps correctly at runtime.\nWhy the Other Options are incorrect:\nB. Change the trigger schema: The trigger schema controls what data enters the flow from the agent, whereas the problem states that the agent is experiencing an issue receiving the returned structured values from the flow.\nC. Review the flow run history: While useful for verifying if the flow completed successfully, it won't fix the schema binding contract mismatch that is causing the agent to drop or misunderstand the payload.\nD. Add a Parse JSON inside the flow: Parse JSON is used inside a flow to handle unstructured data payloads received from an external API. It does not dictate how structured data properties are mapped back to the agent's expected output block.\nE. Republish the agent: Republishing makes recent configuration changes live, but it will not resolve a structural data mismatch between the parameter bindings.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Validate parameter definitions."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Change the trigger schema."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Review the flow run history."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add a Parse JSON inside the flow."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Republish the agent."}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 52, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is building an agent in Copilot Studio.\nThe agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Responses must be grounded in approved internal content.\n• The agent must be able to retrieve information from configured data sources in a topic.\nYou need to configure the agent so that topic responses are grounded from custom data sources.\nWhat should you configure?", "expl": "C. Configure knowledge sources in a generative answers node.\nTopic-Level Grounding: To satisfy the requirement of retrieving information from custom data sources directly within a topic, you must use a Generative answers node inside that specific topic's authoring canvas. Data Customization: Within the properties of that specific generative answers node, you can explicitly configure, scope, and override knowledge sources (such as specific SharePoint paths, public websites, or uploaded documents), ensuring the agent grounds its answers using that approved content during that specific conversational path.\nWhy the Other Options are Incorrect\nA. Enable analytics: Analytics are used to monitor usage, track deflection rates, and find unhandled intents, but they have no functional impact on data grounding or information retrieval.\nB. Add a custom prompt: While prompts guide an LLM's behavioral persona, they cannot securely retrieve or search through external, un-indexed custom data sources on demand without a retrieval mechanism.\nD. Add SharePoint at the agent level only: The requirement explicitly states that the agent must retrieve information from data sources in a topic. Configuring it only at the agent level applies a global fallback search rather than target-scoped topic rules.\nE. Increase the response length limit: This changes how much text the agent can output, but it does nothing to connect the agent to raw internal data repositories.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Enable analytics to review which topics users trigger most often."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Add a custom prompt that tells the agent to answer only from company data."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Configure knowledge sources in a generative answers node."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add SharePoint as a knowledge source only at the agent level."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Increase the response length limit to improve answer completeness."}], "answer": ["C"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 53, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is building an agent in Copilot Studio topic that must retrieve real-time status information from an external REST API using a Send HTTP request node.\nTo meet the business needs, the topic must meet the following requirements:\n• The request must include the required authentication header.\n• The request must call the endpoint using the correct HTTP method.\nThe response must be configured with an appropriate response data type based on a schema and saved so the topic can reuse the returned values.\nYou need to configure the Send HTTP request node.\nWhich configuration should you use for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Add required headers to the HTTP request.\nWhy it fits: When configuring custom raw HTTP actions in Copilot Studio, explicit authentication credentials (such as an API Key or Authorization: Bearer <token>) are manually included within the request's key-value metadata tables as specialized network headers.\n2. Set the method to GET in the HTTP request.\nWhy it fits: Under standard REST API conventions, the GET HTTP verb is specifically designed and reserved for safely fetching or retrieving data from a target server without altering any state or records on the backend.\n3. Select JSON data type and save response to a variable.\nWhy it fits: Handling data structures requires converting the raw text output from the API into structured objects. Selecting the JSON type tells the engine to parse the oncoming payload, while saving it into a topic variable preserves the details so subsequent nodes can read and reuse the data fields.", "type": "match", "items": ["Add required headers to the HTTP request.", "Set the method to GET in the HTTP request.", "Select JSON data type and save response to a variable.", "Set the method to POST and send JSON in a request body."], "slots": ["Configure authentication for the request.", "Select the correct HTTP method for retrieving data.", "Configure structured data handling and storage of data for reuse in the topic."], "answer": ["Add required headers to the HTTP request.", "Set the method to GET in the HTTP request.", "Select JSON data type and save response to a variable."]}, {"id": 54, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is deploying an agent for employees.\nThe company has the following requirements:\n• Access must be restricted to the internal employees only.\n• End-user authentication must be enforced.\nYou need to configure the agent based on the company requirements.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "A. Select Authenticate with Microsoft Entra ID for the agent.\nEnterprise Security Standards: Selecting Authenticate with Microsoft Entra ID as the identity provider ensures that only individuals with a valid account inside your organization's tenant can log in and interact with the agent.\nAccess Control: Enabling this setting automatically prompts end-users to sign in using their corporate credentials before the conversation can begin. This setup works seamlessly with enterprise single sign-on (SSO), conditional access, and multi-factor authentication (MFA) policies.\nWhy the other options are incorrect:\nOption B (Require Secure Access in the Web channel) handles token exchange security for specific web hosting environments but does not inherently restrict identity access control to specific internal tenant users.\nOption C (Public website experience) explicitly violates the requirement by exposing the agent to the open internet.\nOption D (Personal account) relies on consumer MSA accounts (like Outlook or Xbox logins) rather than the organization's enterprise tenant directory.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Select Authenticate with Microsoft Entra ID for the agent."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Enable Require Secure Access in the Web channel security."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Publish the agent as a public website experience."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Select Authenticate with personal account for the agent."}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 55, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is integrating an agent in Copilot Studio into a custom application by using the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK.\nThe company has the following requirements for the application:\n• The deployment workflow must follow a dependency driven sequence.\n• The agent must be published before configuration values are retrieved.\n• End-to-end validation must occur before production deployment.\nYou need to plan the deployment workflow to integrate the agent.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "1. Publish the agent: Any structural dialog models, topics, or configurations authored inside the studio workspace must be compiled and made live before channel integrations can successfully route\nrequests.\n2. Configure and enable the channel: Navigate to the agent's security and channel configuration parameters to activate the target portal (such as a custom web app or token routing endpoint).\n3. Retrieve configuration values: Gather the distinct operational parameters exposed by your newly activated channel setup, such as the Bot ID, Tenant ID, and Token Endpoint URL.\n4. Implement the SDK: Inject these target identifier values into your custom web application's codebase using the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK to initialize the real-time chat payload handshakes.\n5. Validate in a test environment: Run comprehensive quality assurance cycles in a sandboxed staging or developer environment to guarantee that user tokens pass securely and the conversation executes correctly end-to-end.\n6. Deploy to production: Once the integration code is thoroughly validated, push the finalized container or custom web application build into your live production environment for public consumption.", "type": "order", "items": ["Configure and enable the agent's target integration channel.", "Retrieve current agent configuration values.", "Deploy the web app to production.", "Implement the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK in the app using the agent configuration.", "Publish the agent.", "Validate the end-to-end conversation workflow in a test environment."], "answer": ["Publish the agent.", "Configure and enable the agent's target integration channel.", "Retrieve current agent configuration values.", "Implement the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK in the app using the agent configuration.", "Validate the end-to-end conversation workflow in a test environment.", "Deploy the web app to production."]}, {"id": 56, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent in Copilot Studio already exists in a development environment.\nYou need to prepare the agent so it can be moved to a production environment using the supported solution-based approach.\nYou need to create an exportable package that contains the existing agent.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Add Existing Agent (E): Because the agent already exists standalone in your development environment, your first step is to create a new custom unmanaged solution container in the Maker portal. Then, you must use the Add existing ➔ Chatbot (or Agent) option to bring the agent and all its subcomponents into this solution. Exporting the Solution (C): Once the agent is bundled within your unmanaged custom solution container, you must export that unmanaged solution. The system will prompt you during this wizard to download it as a managed zip package, which is the official format for deployment into a downstream production environment.\nWhy the other options are incorrect:\nOption A is incorrect because removing topics from the solution will break the agent, preventing it from functioning correctly in the production environment.\nOption B is incorrect because solutions exist as unmanaged in the source environment; you do not \"convert\" it inside the development environment before exporting. The conversion to a managed file format happens during the export wizard process.\nOption D is incorrect because exporting a channel configuration does not package the actual underlying logic, triggers, or framework of the agent itself.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Remove agent topics directly from the solution to prevent export errors."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Convert the solution to a managed solution so it can be exported."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Export the unmanaged solution that contains the agent."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Publish the agent to a channel and export the channel configuration."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Create a custom solution and add the existing agent to the solution."}], "answer": ["C", "E"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 57, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company wants to use content from a non-Microsoft enterprise knowledge base to ground agent responses. To meet the business needs, the knowledge source must meet the following requirements:\n• Support grounding from indexed data.\n• Use existing access controls from the source system.\n• Support read-only operations.\nYou need to determine the correct behavior to meet the requirements.\nWhich behavior meets each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Retrieve indexed content\nWhy it fits: When an agent grounds its generative responses using indexed data, it relies on a pre-built search catalog. Choosing Retrieve indexed content instructs the engine to look up data that has already been crawled and compiled for semantic discovery.\n2. Respect existing permissions.\nWhy it fits: To ensure data compliance and security, the agent must never bypass security boundaries or display information a user isn't legally allowed to see. Selecting Respect existing permissions. ensures that access control lists (ACLs) and security trimming from the original host repository are strictly enforced.\n3. Prevent transactional updates.\nWhy it fits: A read-only operational boundary strictly blocks mutations, data inputs, or overrides to the source material. Selecting Prevent transactional updates. explicitly drops or restricts writing permissions, keeping the connection entirely read-only.", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Support grounding from indexed data.", "options": ["Retrieve indexed content", "Execute runtime operations.", "Retrieve live data at runtime."], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Use existing access controls from the source system.", "options": ["Respect existing permissions.", "Bypass permissions for faster retrieval.", "Respect existing permissions from agent authors."], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Support read-only operations.", "options": ["Prevent transactional updates.", "Allow authenticated write operations.", "Enable updates for enhanced performance."], "answer": 0}], "prompt": "Determine the correct behavior of a non-Microsoft enterprise knowledge base used to ground agent responses."}, {"id": 58, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "An agent uses a flow that calls an external service which can occasionally fail or time out.\nWhen a failure occurs, the agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Must not terminate silently.\n• Must send a notification containing the error details.\nYou need to configure the agent flow so that failures are handled in a controlled and predictable way.\nSolution: Retry the failing action without handling failure.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "§ B. No\n§ A. Yes — INCORRECT\nWhy: A retry policy only repeats an action during transient network errors.\nIf all retry attempts are exhausted, the action simply reports Failed or TimedOut.\nBecause failure handling was intentionally skipped, execution stops dead in its tracks—violating both requirements.\n§ B. No — CORRECT\nWhy: A retry policy is a resilience tactic, not an error-handling architecture.\nCapturing runtime error data and dispatching alerts requires structured exception handling downstream.\nExam Concept: The AB-620 Standard Pattern\nTo satisfy both criteria in Power Automate / Copilot Studio flows, implement a Try-Catch Scope pattern: Try Scope: Houses the external API/service call (with retry policy enabled for transient faults).\nCatch Scope: Configured via \"Configure run after\" to execute only if the Try Scope has failed or has timed out. Notification Action: Inside the Catch scope, extract the error using result('Try_Scope') and alert administrators via email or Microsoft Teams.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 59, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "An agent uses a flow that calls an external service which can occasionally fail or time out.\nWhen a failure occurs, the agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Must not terminate silently.\n• Must send a notification containing the error details.\nYou need to configure the agent flow so that failures are handled in a controlled and predictable way.\nSolution: Configure run after conditions for failure paths.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "§ A. Yes\nControlled Failure Paths: In Power Automate (used by Copilot Studio agent flows), configuring \"Run after\" conditions is the native and correct way to handle errors and timeouts. By default, actions only run if the previous step is successful. [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nCapturing and Notifying Error Details: By adding a notification action (such as sending an email or posting a message) immediately following the external service node and changing its \"Run after\" behavior to fire if the previous action \"has failed\" or \"has timed out\", you ensure the flow catches the exception. This directly fulfills both requirements: the flow handles the error predictably without terminating silently, and it allows you to dynamically extract and pass the specific error details into your notification layout. [1]", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 60, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "An agent uses a flow that calls an external service which can occasionally fail or time out.\nWhen a failure occurs, the agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Must not terminate silently.\n• Must send a notification containing the error details.\nYou need to configure the agent flow so that failures are handled in a controlled and predictable way.\nSolution: Send a notification with relevant error details.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "B. No.\nSimply stating that you will \"send a notification with relevant error details\" is a functional description of the outcome, but it does not configure the flow to handle the failure.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 61, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A company is configuring an agent in Copilot Studio that uses a generative answers node inside multiple topics. The company requires responses to meet the following requirements:\n• Must use an executive summary format.\n• Must apply only within a specific topic.\n• Must continue using configured knowledge sources.\n• Must comply with connector security controls.\nYou need to configure a custom prompt to meet the formatting requirements.\nSolution: Remove the generative answers node and rely only on the custom prompt to produce formatted responses.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "§ B. No\nLosing Knowledge Connections: The requirements state that the agent must continue using configured knowledge sources. The Generative answers node is the core component in Copilot Studio responsible for handling information retrieval and querying those external knowledge bases.\nBreaking Functionality: If you completely remove the generative answers node, the agent loses its bridge to search and fetch the required content. A standalone custom prompt tool can only format text using its base training data; it cannot independently query or reference the agent's configured live knowledge data sources.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 62, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to determine how the agent in Copilot Studio should integrate with two different systems in the Blue Yonder environment, based on their characteristics and the tools available.\nWhich integration mechanism should you use for each system? To answer, move the appropriate integrations to the correct system or data sources. You may use each integration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Internal Reservation → Custom connector\nWhy it fits: An internal reservation system is a proprietary business application unique to an organization. To wrap its custom API endpoints into a reusable, structured tool interface within Copilot Studio and the Power Platform ecosystem, developers build a Custom connector. This allows the agent to safely execute specific operations like creating or updating bookings.\n2. Flight Status and Weather Service → HTTP request\nWhy it fits: Public or standard web-hosted transactional utilities (such as dynamic flight status trackers or live weather web services) expose real-time endpoints. When you need to pull transient or highly dynamic status updates on demand during a conversation, sending a direct HTTP request is the most efficient approach.", "type": "match", "items": ["HTTP request", "Custom connector", "Dataverse connector", "Generative AI knowledge"], "slots": ["Internal Reservation", "Flight Status and Weather Service"], "answer": ["Custom connector", "HTTP request"], "reuse": true}, {"id": 63, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to determine an appropriate implementation related to the use of agent flows that is based on Blue Vender's design and compliance standards.\nWhich implementation method should you use for each scenario? To answer, move the appropriate implementation methods to the correct scenarios. You may use each implementation method once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Use a Go to another topic node.\nWhy it fits: To adhere to software design principles and maintain a clean conversational architecture, you should avoid replicating the same sequence of dialog actions across multiple places. If a conversation flow needs to reuse a sequence that has already been created elsewhere, you redirect the conversation path using a Go to another topic node.\n2. Use a generative answers node.\nWhy it fits: When a user asks how a process works, and the agent only needs to explain or summarize information extracted from documentation or manuals rather than actually performing a database transaction, a generative answers node is used. It extracts and summarizes the text dynamically using foundational AI models.\n3. Use a tool node.\nWhy it fits: Executing a core transactional modification in an external system (such as changing a status, modifying dates, or writing data to a booking repository) requires an active workflow execution. In Copilot Studio's unified canvas, calling these reusable operational processes is handled via a tool node.", "type": "match", "items": ["Use a tool node.", "Use a generative answers node.", "Use a Go to another topic node.", "Prompt the user for manual input.", "Manually rebuild the logic in the topic."], "slots": ["A topic includes a series of steps that replicate logic already defined in another flow.", "A topic provides a natural language explanation of a process but does not execute it.", "A topic includes a step that triggers a reusable process to update booking details."], "answer": ["Use a Go to another topic node.", "Use a generative answers node.", "Use a tool node."], "prompt": "Select the implementation method for each agent flow scenario."}, {"id": 64, "case": "Fabrikam Inc", "series": false, "text": "You need to enable Operations Concierge to delegate shipment tracking inquiries according to Fabrikam Inc.'s defined architecture and technical requirements.\nWhich integration approach should you use?", "expl": "C. Configure the partner capability as an A2A integration and supply its endpoint information.\nResolving Delegation Failures: The third constraint explicitly notes that the primary agent is failing to delegate requests to specialist capabilities correctly (instead relying on general generative answers). Implementing an Agent-to-Agent (A2A) integration and supplying the destination endpoint is the native Copilot Studio mechanism designed to establish multi-agent routing boundaries [C].\nSeamless Hand-offs: This configuration registers the partner's specialized system as a distinct external agent. The primary orchestrator (Operations Concierge) can then seamlessly delegate conversational control and route shipment tracking inquiries to that specialist agent rather than trying to summarize it broadly using base model knowledge.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Configure the partner capability as an enterprise knowledge source with indexed retrieval enabled."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Configure the partner capability as an MCP tool and invoke it as a transactional operation."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Configure the partner capability as an A2A integration and supply its endpoint information."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Configure the primary agent to handle shipment tracking through generative responses after publishing."}], "answer": ["C"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 65, "case": "Fabrikam Inc", "series": false, "text": "You need to integrate Fabrikam Inc.'s existing Foundry agent so Operations Concierge can delegate summarization requests.\nWhich action should you perform for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate actions to the correct requirements. You may use each action once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Add an external agent of type Microsoft Foundry.\nWhy it fits: When establishing a collaborative multi-agent architecture where a primary orchestrator copilot delegates specific sub-tasks to a specialized external agent created in Azure AI Foundry, you navigate to the Agents tab in Microsoft Copilot Studio and select Microsoft Foundry from the available external agent connection list.\n2. Enter the Foundry project endpoint URL to create the connection.\nWhy it fits: To ensure the external agent connection points directly to your targeted environment and specific model resources, the configuration requires establishing a handshake via the unique network backbone.\nSupplying the precise project endpoint or connection string bridges the platform boundary to register the designated project container.", "type": "match", "items": ["Add an external agent of type Microsoft Foundry.", "Publish the Copilot Studio agent to enable external calls.", "Add the Foundry output as an indexed knowledge source.", "Enter the Foundry project endpoint URL to create the connection."], "slots": ["The main agent must be able to call an external Foundry agent.", "The external Foundry agent must reference the correct Foundry project and agent."], "answer": ["Add an external agent of type Microsoft Foundry.", "Enter the Foundry project endpoint URL to create the connection."], "prompt": "Integrate Fabrikam Inc.'s existing Foundry agent so Operations Concierge can delegate summarization requests."}, {"id": 66, "case": "Fabrikam Inc", "series": false, "text": "You need to plan how Fabrikam Inc. will reuse shared components across multiple agents while limiting who can change the shared components.\nWhich approach should you use for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate components to the correct requirements. You may use each component once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Share the component collection with teammates while controlling edit access.\nWhy it fits: When a team shares building blocks (like prompts, knowledge schemas, or dialog models) but wants to protect them from unintended alterations by secondary makers, they utilize fine-grained sharing controls. Granting read-only or run-only permissions to the collection prevents unauthorized modifications.\n2. Create a component collection and move shared components into it.\nWhy it fits: Instead of manually duplicating individual topics or logic chains across several independent chatbots—which leads to an unmaintainable codebase—builders centralize these common assets into a singular component collection. This collection serves as a shared library that multiple agents link to directly without duplication.\n3. Restrict component collection to the primary agent.\nWhy it fits: If a highly specialized group of reusable assets is strictly intended for a single core agent, you configure its distribution settings to limit consumption boundaries. Restricting the collection to only the designated primary orchestrator prevents other agents in the environment from picking it up.", "type": "match", "items": ["Restrict component collection to the primary agent.", "Create a component collection and move shared components into it.", "Export the primary agent as a solution and copy assets into other agents.", "Share the component collection with teammates while controlling edit access."], "slots": ["Prevent shared assets from being modified by most builders.", "Reuse the same set of components across multiple agents without copying.", "Limit a shared set of components so it can be used only by its intended primary agent."], "answer": ["Share the component collection with teammates while controlling edit access.", "Create a component collection and move shared components into it.", "Restrict component collection to the primary agent."]}, {"id": 67, "case": "Fabrikam Inc", "series": false, "text": "You need to configure Operations Concierge so that prompt-driven summarization behavior aligns with Fabrikam Inc.'s architectural requirements.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "C. Select a model from the Foundry model catalog in the custom prompt configuration.\nE. Create a reusable custom prompt template and apply it where summarization responses are generated.\nWhy these are the correct choices:\nWhy C fits: To adhere to enterprise governance and ensure compliance with Fabrikam Inc.'s security standards, you must bypass the standard out-of-the-box model. Selecting a vetted, corporate-approved foundational model directly from the Azure AI Foundry catalog within the prompt configuration ensures that the summarization workload is processed strictly by authorized infrastructure.\nWhy E fits: Creating a structured, reusable custom prompt template ensures that formatting rules, tone parameters, and operational constraints are uniformly applied across all areas where summaries are generated. This template acts as the central definition of the agent's summarization behavior across the application lifecycle.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding source for summarization prompts."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Publish the agent to ensure the selected model is enforced for summarization."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Select a model from the Foundry model catalog in the custom prompt configuration."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Enable citations to control how summarization output is generated."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Create a reusable custom prompt template and apply it where summarization responses are generated."}], "answer": ["C", "E"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 68, "case": "Fabrikam Inc", "series": false, "text": "You need to connect Operations Concierge to Fabrikam Inc.'s Azure AI Search knowledge index while complying with security requirements.\nWhich configuration should you use for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Select the service principal as the authentication type.\nWhy it fits: Governance requirements for enterprise integrations typically rule out using shared keys or individual user accounts. Selecting a service principal provides a non-interactive, highly secure, and auditable identity layer to handle authentication between the agent and Azure AI Search.\n2. Enter the name of the Azure AI Search index.\nWhy it fits: An individual Azure AI Search service instance can host many different document sets. Specifying the exact index name ensures that the agent targets the precise data store containing the curated policy and procedure files rather than looking through unrelated enterprise indexes.\n3. Provide the Azure AI Search Endpoint URL in the connection details.\nWhy it fits: To navigate traffic to the correct cloud infrastructure instance where your search engine runs, you must provide its physical resource route. The Endpoint URL establishes the baseline connection to that specific service instance hosting your indexes.", "type": "match", "items": ["Enable citations for grounded answers.", "Enter the name of the Azure AI Search index.", "Select the service principal as the authentication type.", "Provide the Azure AI Search Endpoint URL in the connection details."], "slots": ["Ensure the Azure AI Search connection complies with the governance requirement for authenticated access.", "Ensure policy and procedure Q&A uses the required curated, indexed enterprise knowledge source.", "Ensure the connection targets the Azure AI Search service instance that stores the indexed policy documents."], "answer": ["Select the service principal as the authentication type.", "Enter the name of the Azure AI Search index.", "Provide the Azure AI Search Endpoint URL in the connection details."], "prompt": "Connect Operations Concierge to Fabrikam Inc.'s Azure AI Search knowledge index while complying with security requirements. Items may be used once, more than once, or not at all."}, {"id": 69, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is building a Copilot Studio solution that must answer policy questions, retrieve operational metrics from structured data, and execute authenticated updates in backend systems. The company decides to use multiple specialized agents rather than a single monolithic agent. Custom agents for each action already exist. To meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• The solution must retrieve up to date metrics from structured data.\n• The solution must answer policy questions using curated knowledge.\n• The solution must execute authenticated updates during conversations.\nYou need to select the appropriate agent design for each requirement.\nWhich approach should you use for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Delegate the request to a data-access agent.\nWhy it fits: When an agent needs to retrieve up-to-date business metrics, KPIs, or specific records stored inside organized relational databases or structured tables (like Microsoft Dataverse, SQL, or tabular systems), it delegates the task to a specialized data-access agent optimized for handling database queries and structured schemas.\n2. Delegate the request to a knowledge-focused agent.\nWhy it fits: Answering policy, compliance, or procedural questions involves searching through unstructured text corpora (such as company handbooks, PDFs, and manuals). A knowledge-focused agent is specifically designed for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and semantic exploration over curated text indexes.\n3. Delegate the request to a tools-first agent.\nWhy it fits: Executing data mutations or active operations (such as making modifications, triggering write workflows, or executing transactional updates) requires an agent architecture focused on API invocation. A tools-first agent specializes in discovering, configuring inputs for, and reliably triggering actions or connectors to write changes back to external source systems under an authenticated user context.", "type": "match", "items": ["Delegate the request to a single agent.", "Delegate the request to a tools-first agent.", "Delegate the request to a data-access agent.", "Delegate the request to a knowledge-focused agent."], "slots": ["The solution must retrieve up to date metrics from structured data.", "The solution must answer policy questions using curated data sources.", "The solution must execute authenticated updates during conversations."], "answer": ["Delegate the request to a data-access agent.", "Delegate the request to a knowledge-focused agent.", "Delegate the request to a tools-first agent."], "reuse": true}, {"id": 70, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company has an approved custom connector that uses a REST API for an internal system.\nAn agent in Copilot Studio must call this connector during conversations to retrieve or update data.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Make the connector actions available for the agent to invoke.\n• Ensure authentication is handled at the service level, not per user.\n• Pass conversation context into the connector when it is called.\n• Surface the returned data of the connector to the user in the conversation.\nYou need to configure the agent and the connector action to meet the requirements.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Add the connector as a tool in the agent.\nWhy it fits: Before an agent can explore, identify, or execute specific actions or operations from a connector, the connector itself must be formally registered into the conversational runtime ecosystem by declaring it as an active agent tool.\n2. Create or reuse a connection for the connector.\nWhy it fits: Connections handle the security boundaries and identity layers for external services in the Power Platform. Setting up a dedicated connection credentials profile allows the connector to run under a unified service account tier rather than prompting each unique end-user to log in individually.\n3. Map topic variables to the tool inputs.\nWhy it fits: In order for the connector action to run using data gathered during the active conversation (such as an order ID, name, or date), you bind those dynamic, in-memory topic variables directly to the designated input slots expected by the tool payload.\n4. Parse the JSON response and assign it to a topic variable.\nWhy it fits: Raw API payloads return unstructured strings or JSON records that cannot be shown to a user directly. Parsing the JSON maps these fields into strongly-typed conversational variables, which can then be formatted into natural text nodes or cards to display to the user.", "type": "match", "items": ["Map topic variables to the tool inputs.", "Parse the JSON response and assign it to a topic variable.", "Add the connector as a tool in the agent.", "Create or reuse a connection for the connector."], "slots": ["Make connector actions available for agent to invoke.", "Handle authentication at service level, not per user.", "Pass conversation context into connector when called.", "Surface connector's returned data to the user."], "answer": ["Add the connector as a tool in the agent.", "Create or reuse a connection for the connector.", "Map topic variables to the tool inputs.", "Parse the JSON response and assign it to a topic variable."]}, {"id": 71, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company has an Azure AI Search vector index that contains enterprise content and wants to use it as a knowledge source for an agent in Copilot Studio.\nTo meet business needs, the knowledge source must meet the following requirements:\n• The agent must connect to the Azure AI Search service.\n• The agent must use a supported authentication method for the connection.\n• The agent must ensure answers are grounded using the specified Azure AI Search index.\nYou need to connect to Azure AI Search as a knowledge source for the agent.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "1. Initiate the process: Within Microsoft Copilot Studio, navigate to the agent's Knowledge settings tab and select the option to introduce a new knowledge repository to launch the configuration wizard.\n2. Create a connection: Select the Azure AI Search connector option from the marketplace integration catalog to initiate a fresh connection interface.\n3. Configure authentication: Input the baseline structural fields required for the secure handshake, including the service Endpoint URL and selecting the appropriate credentials protocol (such as an API Key or Service Principal).\n4. Specify the index: Choose or type the targeted Search Index container name where your documents have been compiled so the agent points to the correct partition.\n5. Associate with the agent: Conclude the setup by finalizing the entry to bind and register the validated knowledge pipeline into the conversational agent's active runtime.", "type": "order", "items": ["Configure the authentication method and provide the required connection details.", "Associate the configured knowledge source with the agent.", "Create a new Azure AI Search connection.", "Initiate the process to add a knowledge source to the agent.", "Specify the Azure AI Search index to use for grounding."], "answer": ["Initiate the process to add a knowledge source to the agent.", "Create a new Azure AI Search connection.", "Configure the authentication method and provide the required connection details.", "Specify the Azure AI Search index to use for grounding.", "Associate the configured knowledge source with the agent."]}, {"id": 72, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses a primary agent in Copilot Studio to handle user intake.\nFor some requests, the company wants the primary agent to delegate work to another Copilot Studio agent that already exists.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must allow agents to perform the following on the connected agent: Must allow calls from other agents.\nMust allow agents to use the connected agent for collaboration.\nMust allow agents to invoke the connected agent in conversations.\nYou need to integrate the existing Copilot Studio agent with the connected agent.\nWhich setting should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Publish the connected agent.\nWhy it fits: Before any external service, application, or secondary conversational agent can discover, connect to, or successfully pass payload calls to an agent, that target agent's live schema and runtime code must be compiled and deployed to the cloud by executing a Publish operation.\n2. Add the connected agent.\nWhy it fits: To enable a multi-agent architectural design pattern where a primary orchestrator can collaborate with and pass tasks to a subordinate sub-agent, the primary agent's workspace configuration must explicitly reference that target agent. You fulfill this by selecting Add the connected agent within the workspace.\n3. Configure connections to the connected agent.\nWhy it fits: Allowing one agent to safely invoke another during live production chat sessions requires satisfying security policies and endpoint handshakes. Setting up the authentication layer, trust relationship, and routing rules is done under Configure connections to the connected agent.", "type": "match", "items": ["Publish the connected agent.", "Add the connected agent.", "Configure connections to the connected agent.", "Add the connected agent as an indexed knowledge source."], "slots": ["Must allow calls from other agents", "Must allow agents to use the connected agent for collaboration.", "Must allow agents to invoke the connected agent in conversations."], "answer": ["Publish the connected agent.", "Add the connected agent.", "Configure connections to the connected agent."]}, {"id": 73, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses multiple Copilot Studio agents that perform specialized tasks.\nThe company needs to enable one agent to directly delegate and invoke another agent within Copilot Studio by using a standard Copilot Studio interface.\nYou need to configure collaboration among agents by using Copilot Studio.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "B. Provide A2A endpoint information for the target agent.\nThe Agent-to-Agent (A2A) Protocol: Copilot Studio utilizes the native Agent2Agent (A2A) protocol as an open standard to facilitate multi-agent communication, orchestration, and task delegation.\nOnboarding via the Interface: To bind a target agent using this framework, you navigate to the Agents tab of the orchestrator agent, select Add an agent, choose Agent2Agent, and then provide the unique Endpoint URL of the target agent. This registers the second agent as an executable tool that the main agent can dynamically invoke during multi-turn user conversations.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Add the target agent as a Microsoft Fabric Data Agent."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Provide A2A endpoint information for the target agent."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Upload documentation about the target agent for referencing."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add the target agent as an indexed knowledge source."}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 74, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent in Copilot Studio that produces structured responses using a reusable instruction template. The responses follow the required tone and format. During a compliance review, the team discovers that the responses were generated using a model that is not on the approved list.\nThe company requires that the reusable instruction template continues to be used and the model used for prompt- driven responses meets governance requirements.\nThe solution must ensure that prompt-driven responses are generated using an approved model.\nYou need to configure the solution that meets the requirements.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Template Continuity (E): Since the company explicitly requires that the reusable instruction template continues to be used to dictate tone and format, you must verify that this specific instruction template remains bound to the node or topic where the agent generates the structured response.\nGovernance and Compliance (B): Enabling citations directly satisfies the needs of the compliance review team. By forcing the agent to output explicit references and citations for the data it synthesizes, compliance officers can trace exactly where the model sourced its information, ensuring the prompt-driven answers align with organizational compliance and data governance policies.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Publish the agent so that the model used by prompt-driven responses is updated automatically."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Enable citations so generated responses can be traced and comply with governance requirements."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Add a line to the instruction template that requires the agent to use only approved models when generating responses."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding source so the approved model requirement is enforced during response generation."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Ensure the reusable instruction template is the one applied to the agent that generates the structured response."}], "answer": ["B", "E"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 75, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team has an agent in Copilot Studio that handles user intake. For some requests, the team wants the agent to delegate work to a Microsoft Foundry agent that performs specialized processing. The Foundry agent already exists.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• The primary agent must be able to call the Foundry agent.\n• The external agent connection must reference the correct Foundry project and agent.\nYou need to integrate the primary agent with the Foundry agent.\nWhich two actions should you take? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Add an external agent (A): To connect your primary user-facing Copilot Studio agent to an external processor like Microsoft Foundry, you navigate to your main agent's configuration, open the Agents page, select Add an agent, and under Connect to an external agent, choose Microsoft Foundry.\nProject endpoint URL (C): During the connection setup process within Copilot Studio, to securely reference and communicate with the specific background Foundry asset registry, you are explicitly prompted to create a new connection by providing the target Foundry project endpoint URL along with the specific Foundry Agent ID.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Add an external agent."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Configure the Copilot Studio endpoint URL."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Configure the project endpoint URL."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add an indexed knowledge source."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Publish the Copilot Studio agent."}], "answer": ["A", "C"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 76, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company must enable an agent in Copilot Studio to execute operations exposed by an external MCP server. The MCP server publishes callable tools and requires authentication.\nThe agent must be able to invoke MCP tools during conversations.\nYou need to configure MCP tools so the agent can execute MCP tool calls.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "D. Add the MCP server to the agent's tool configuration.\nNative Integration Interface: To connect an external MCP server to Copilot Studio, you must explicitly register it under the agent's configuration settings. You do this by navigating to the Agents configuration tab, choosing Tools, and selecting the option to add an existing MCP server.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Upload documentation about the MCP server so the agent can answer questions about it."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Enable generative answers so the model can automatically invoke MCP tools."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Publish the agent and rely on default settings for tool availability."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add the MCP server to the agent's tool configuration."}], "answer": ["D"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 77, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company needs an agent to answer questions using live enterprise records and to execute a limited set of operations in the same system.\nTo meet the business needs, the agent must meet the following requirements:\n• The agent must update a record using authenticated access.\n• The agent must answer questions using live data at runtime.\n• The agent must avoid indexing enterprise data into a search index.\nYou need to select the correct Power Platform connector approach for each requirement.\nWhich option should you use for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate solutions to the correct requirements. You may use each solution once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Power Platform connector tool.\nWhy it fits: In Copilot Studio, when an agent needs to perform an active operation or mutation (such as creating, updating, or deleting a record), it utilizes a tool (formerly known as an action or plugin). These tools execute operations via standard Power Platform connectors under authenticated user context or predefined API connections.\n2. Power Platform connector as real-time knowledge.\nWhy it fits: When an agent needs to provide answers using the most current, up-to-the-minute live data directly at the moment a user asks a question, the connector is added to the agent's knowledge base as a real-time source. The agent issues queries directly to the source system at runtime instead of relying on cached or scheduled data.\n3. Power Platform connector as real-time knowledge.\nWhy it fits: Choosing a real-time knowledge source bypasses the ingest-and-index pipeline. Instead of scraping and copying enterprise data into an external storage database or a search service (which is what a knowledge source for indexed content does), it pulls data directly on-the-fly and drops it from memory once the response is formulated, preventing data from being indexed.", "type": "match", "items": ["Power Platform connector tool.", "Power Platform connector as real-time knowledge.", "Copilot connector knowledge source for indexed content."], "slots": ["The agent must update a record using authenticated access.", "The agent must answer questions using live data at runtime.", "The agent must avoid indexing enterprise data into a search index."], "answer": ["Power Platform connector tool.", "Power Platform connector as real-time knowledge.", "Power Platform connector as real-time knowledge."], "reuse": true, "prompt": "Select the correct Power Platform connector approach for each requirement. Items may be used once, more than once, or not at all."}, {"id": 78, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent in Copilot Studio to coordinate user interactions.\nThe company wants the agent to delegate requests to a Microsoft Fabric Data Agent that accesses governed business data.\nThe agent must retrieve structured analytical data while enforcing data security and scope controls.\nYou need to select the integration approach that meets the requirements.\nWhat should you use?", "expl": "A. A Microsoft Fabric Data Agent and bind it to the model.\nAgentic Collaboration: One of the primary deployment mechanisms for Microsoft Fabric Data Agents is to add them directly to a custom AI agent in Microsoft Copilot Studio as a connected agent. This establishes a native multi-agent architecture via Model Context Protocols (MCP).\nSecurity & Scope Enforcement: Instead of querying database tables directly, binding a published Fabric Data Agent to your orchestration model allows Copilot Studio to delegate complex data queries to Fabric. This delegation natively respects and enforces all pre-configured enterprise data governance policies, permissions, and security scope definitions (such as row-level or column-level security) tied to the user's data context.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "A Microsoft Fabric Data Agent and bind it to the model."}, {"k": "B", "t": "A REST API action that queries Fabric tables directly."}, {"k": "C", "t": "A Power Automate flow and call it from a topic."}, {"k": "D", "t": "A Copilot connector knowledge source that indexes Fabric data."}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 79, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is building an agent in Copilot Studio that must answer questions using enterprise systems and must also perform authenticated operations.\nTo meet the business needs, the agent must meet the following requirements.\nThe agent must provide searchable answers from indexed enterprise content.\nThe agent must retrieve up-to-date facts at runtime without indexing data.\nThe agent must perform an authenticated update operation during the conversation.\nYou need to select the correct integration approach for each requirement.\nWhich option should you use for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate solutions to the correct requirements. You may use each solution once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Copilot connector knowledge source\nWhy it fits: When enterprise content needs to be crawled, parsed, and optimized for broad semantic search across an entire repository, it is configured as an indexed knowledge source. The content is ingested into a search index ahead of time to allow fast retrieval of complex answers.\n2. Power Platform connector for real-time knowledge\nWhy it fits: If data cannot be cached or stored in an external search index due to security restrictions or a need for instant freshness, using a connector as a real-time knowledge source queries the live external system directly at runtime. It pulls the necessary facts on the fly without persisting them to an index.\n3. Power Platform connector tool\nWhy it fits: Any action that alters data or executes a transactional business operation (such as updating a status field, changing a record, or executing a workflow) is handled as a connector tool (action/plugin). It uses authenticated context to safely modify data rather than just retrieving informational content.", "type": "match", "items": ["Power Platform connector tool", "Copilot connector knowledge source", "Power Platform connector for real-time knowledge"], "slots": ["The agent must provide searchable answers from indexed enterprise content.", "The agent must retrieve up-to-date facts at runtime without indexing data.", "The agent must perform an authenticated update operation during the conversation."], "answer": ["Copilot connector knowledge source", "Power Platform connector for real-time knowledge", "Power Platform connector tool"], "reuse": true}, {"id": 80, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is extending agents in Copilot Studio with both external capabilities and enterprise content access. Some user requests require grounded answers, while other requests require executing actions.\nTo meet the business needs, the agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Ground answers using results from enterprise data.\n• Invoke callable functions exposed through a standardized tool interface.\n• Write an update to a system by using an authenticated action during the conversation.\nYou need to integrate the correct approach to meet the requirements.\nWhat should you integrate for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate integrations to the correct requirements. You may use each integration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Add Dataverse tables as a structured data knowledge source\nWhy it fits: Microsoft Dataverse holds the core transactional and organizational data of an enterprise. By adding these tables directly as a structured knowledge source, the conversational agent can natively search, retrieve, and ground its generative responses using authoritative enterprise database records.\n2. Connect to an MCP server and add its tools to the agent\nWhy it fits: The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open, modern standard engineered specifically to act as a universal, standardized interface between AI models and external development tools or API systems.\nConnecting to an MCP server allows the agent to safely discover and invoke exposed functions using a uniform schema.\n3. Add a Power Platform connector tool and call it from a topic\nWhy it fits: When a conversation demands an active side-effect or state change in an external system (such as writing or modifying a record), the agent triggers a custom connector tool. Triggering this tool from a specific conversation topic ensures that the operation executes with proper user authentication and transactional integrity.", "type": "match", "items": ["Connect to an MCP server and add its tools to the agent.", "Add Dataverse tables as a structured data knowledge source.", "Add a Power Platform connector tool and call it from a topic.", "Add a Copilot connector knowledge source for indexed content."], "slots": ["Ground answers using results from enterprise data.", "Invoke callable functions exposed through a standardized tool interface.", "Write an update to a system by using an authenticated action during the conversation."], "answer": ["Add Dataverse tables as a structured data knowledge source.", "Connect to an MCP server and add its tools to the agent.", "Add a Power Platform connector tool and call it from a topic."]}, {"id": 81, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company plans to use Copilot connectors to ground answers from a third-party system for an agent in Copilot Studio.\nTo meet the business needs, responsibilities for configuring and using Copilot connectors must be separated, based on the following requirements:\n• For tenant administration, the connector must be available at the tenant level.\n• For agent runtime behavior, answers must be grounded from authorized sources.\n• For builder configuration, you must add the connector to the agent.\nYou need to determine how Copilot connector set-up responsibilities are handled across tenant, runtime, and builder stages.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configuration to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Configure the Copilot connector in Microsoft 365 Admin Center.\nWhy it fits: Broad administrative operations, tenant-wide data access permissions, data ingestion management, and deployment authorization of organizational connectors into the enterprise ecosystem fall under the purview of Tenant administration within the Microsoft 365 Admin Center.\n2. Call the connector knowledge source.\nWhy it fits: This represents an operational event that happens dynamically during an end-user session. When a user asks a question, the agent triggers real-time querying to retrieve context from the connected data source, defining its Agent runtime behavior.\n3. Add the Copilot connector as a knowledge source.\nWhy it fits: This is an implementation step handled by the app maker or bot author. Within Microsoft Copilot Studio, the maker links a validated data connector to the agent's knowledge repository so it can ground generative answers, constituting Builder configuration.", "type": "match", "items": ["Call the connector knowledge source.", "Add the Copilot connector as a knowledge source.", "Create a new Copilot connector directly in Copilot Studio.", "Configure the Copilot connector in Microsoft 365 Admin Center.", "Configure the Copilot connector in Power Platform Admin Center."], "slots": ["Tenant administration", "Agent runtime behavior", "Builder configuration"], "answer": ["Configure the Copilot connector in Microsoft 365 Admin Center.", "Call the connector knowledge source.", "Add the Copilot connector as a knowledge source."]}, {"id": 82, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent in Copilot Studio to answer questions based on enterprise content.\nThe content is indexed in Azure AI Search. Responses must be generated by a Microsoft Foundry model.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Users are able to trace each answer back to its source.\n• The agent retrieves information from a centralized data index.\n• Responses are generated by an enterprise-approved foundation model.\nYou need to configure generative answers for the agent.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Instruct the agent to cite sources in knowledge settings.\nWhy it fits: Enabling source citation forces the agent to append inline references, links, or footnotes pointing to the exact documents or snippets used to generate the output. This fulfills the capability for end-users to verify and trace facts back to their origin.\n2. Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding data source.\nWhy it fits: Azure AI Search acts as a centralized search index for large enterprise data silos. Connecting it as a knowledge source allows Copilot Studio to perform advanced hybrid/vector searches over pre-indexed company assets to ground generative AI outputs.\n3. Deploy a model in Foundry to an agent and connect to Copilot Studio.\nWhy it fits: When enterprise compliance prohibits the default model and mandates a vetted, highly tailored LLM, you deploy that specific foundational model inside Azure AI Foundry (formerly Azure AI Studio) and map it back into Copilot Studio as the primary model engine.", "type": "match", "items": ["Upload documents directly into the agent.", "Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding data source.", "Instruct the agent to cite sources in knowledge settings.", "Deploy a model in Foundry to an agent and connect to Copilot Studio."], "slots": ["Users trace each answer back to its source.", "The agent retrieves information from a centralized data index.", "Responses are generated by an enterprise-approved foundational model."], "answer": ["Instruct the agent to cite sources in knowledge settings.", "Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding data source.", "Deploy a model in Foundry to an agent and connect to Copilot Studio."]}, {"id": 83, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent in Copilot Studio to generate structured responses for internal users.\nThe agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Consistently follow a reusable instruction template for response tone and structure.\n• Use an enterprise-approved foundation model for responses generated through the template.\nYou need to configure custom prompts so the agent uses the Microsoft Foundry model catalog.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "D. Select a model from the Foundry model catalog for the custom prompt configuration.\nE. Create a custom prompt template and apply it where the agent generates the structured response.\nCustom Prompt Template (E): Creating a reusable prompt template (using the Prompt tool in Copilot Studio) is the native method to enforce a consistent instruction format, tone, and response structure. Once crafted, this template or prompt action can be seamlessly applied across any node or topic where a structured response is required.\nSelecting a Foundry Model (D): Under the Bring Your Own Model (BYOM) capability within the prompt tool configuration, makers can change the model from the default managed choices to a custom enterprise- approved option. By clicking the model selection menu, you can connect and select a specific base or fine- tuned model deployed via the Azure AI Foundry / Microsoft Foundry model catalog.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Publish the agent to apply the Foundry model selection automatically."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding source, so the model choice is enforced."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Allow topic authors to define tone and structure directly in topic steps."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Select a model from the Foundry model catalog for the custom prompt configuration."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Create a custom prompt template and apply it where the agent generates the structured response."}, {"k": "F", "t": "Enable citations, so the model is restricted to approved outputs."}], "answer": ["D", "E"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 84, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company integrates an MCP service that exposes ticket-management operations.\nThe MCP service requires authenticated requests and exposes multiple callable operations that must be selectively governed for agent use.\nTo meet the business needs, the MCP configuration must meet the following requirements:\n• Only the allowed capabilities must be callable during conversations.\n• Requests must succeed without embedding credentials in topic steps.\n• The agent must be configured to send requests to the correct backend.\nYou need to configure MCP tools for the agent.\nWhich setting should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate settings to the correct requirements. You may use each setting once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. CreateTicket, EscalateIncident, GetTicket, UpdateSLA\nWhy it fits: This comma-separated list defines specific operations or functions. By explicitly declaring this restricted set of endpoints or tool paths during configuration, you limit the agent's scope, ensuring it can only invoke those specific capabilities.\n2. API key header X-API-Key\nWhy it fits: To comply with security best practices and keep credentials out of cleartext dialog fields, authentication parameters are abstracted to the tool configuration level. Setting up the token format here handles credentials globally for the connection.\n3. Server URL\nWhy it fits: The Server URL represents the base network path (endpoint) where the target backend service or API server resides, ensuring that outbound API requests reach the intended destination system.", "type": "match", "items": ["Server URL", "API key header X-API-Key", "CreateTicket, EscalateIncident, GetTicket, UpdateSLA"], "slots": ["Only the allowed capabilities must be callable during conversations.", "Requests must succeed without embedding credentials in topic steps.", "The agent must be configured to send requests to the correct backend."], "answer": ["CreateTicket, EscalateIncident, GetTicket, UpdateSLA", "API key header X-API-Key", "Server URL"], "prompt": "Configure MCP tools for the agent. Settings may be used once, more than once, or not at all."}, {"id": 85, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent in Copilot Studio must complete tasks by automating interactions with a legacy desktop application that does not use APIs.\nDuring testing, the builder must confirm that the automation runs in a supported execution context and can inspect how each automation step executes during conversations.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must:\n• Execute UI automation in a supported, platform-managed environment.\n• Provide visibility into how automation steps run during agent conversations.\nYou need to configure the agent based on the requirements.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "B. Inspect automation execution details by using the agent runtime view.\nC. Enable computer use for the agent so UI automation runs in the supported execution environment.\nEnable computer use (C): The Computer use tool in Copilot Studio is explicitly designed to solve the problem of automating legacy applications without native APIs. Enabling this allows the agent to interact dynamically with desktop or web screens (typing, clicking, and viewing UI layouts) based on natural language guidelines. Agent Runtime View (B): When executing or diagnosing a computer-use-enabled agent, the testing pane or runtime view provides a live session feedback log. This allows makers to look at step-by-step screenshots, action latency, function matching logs, and full execution trace logs to audit how the agent interacted with the legacy UI.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Publish the agent so automation settings are applied at runtime."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Inspect automation execution details by using the agent runtime view."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Enable computer use for the agent so UI automation runs in the supported execution environment."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add the desktop application as a knowledge source so the agent can reference its UI structure."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Configure a custom connector to simulate UI interactions through REST calls."}], "answer": ["B", "C"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 86, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company configures an agent flow that interacts with multiple systems to complete user requests.\nThe company must integrate external services, retrieve data, and notify users as part of the same automated flow run. To support this scenario, the agent flow must be able to do the following:\n• Call an external API.\n• Retrieve stored data from a supported internal data source.\n• Send a Teams message.\nYou need to select a connector or action type for each requirement.\nWhat should you use? To answer, move the appropriate connectors or actions to the correct requirements. You may use each connector or action once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Make an HTTPS request.\nWhy it fits: When an agent needs to communicate directly with an external API endpoint that doesn't have a pre-built connector wrapper, making a raw HTTP/HTTPS request allows you to define the exact method (GET, POST, etc.), headers, and payload structure.\n2. Add a standard connector.\nWhy it fits: Out-of-the-box Microsoft ecosystem data repositories (such as SharePoint, SQL Server, Excel, or OneDrive) are integrated by utilizing the platform's library of standard connectors. Adding these allows the agent to read and query stored data securely without writing custom integration code.\n3. Trigger a notification.\nWhy it fits: Sending an asynchronous update or posting a dynamic message directly into a Microsoft Teams chat or channel serves the specific functional goal of triggering an outbound system notification to alert a user or a team.", "type": "match", "items": ["Trigger a notification.", "Make an HTTPS request.", "Add a standard connector."], "slots": ["Call an external API.", "Retrieve stored data from a supported internal data source.", "Send a Teams message."], "answer": ["Make an HTTPS request.", "Add a standard connector.", "Trigger a notification."], "reuse": true}, {"id": 87, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent in Copilot Studio must retrieve real-time data from an external system that exposes a REST API. The API returns structured JSON data and requires authentication.\nThe agent must call the API during conversations to fulfill user requests.\nThe agent must retrieve real-time data from the external system by calling an authenticated REST API during a conversation.\nThe solution must use a supported mechanism that executes the REST API call and the call must be explicitly configured to run at runtime.\nYou need to determine which approaches add the REST API to the agent.\nWhich two solutions meet the goal? Each correct answer is a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "A. Configure an HTTP action and invoke it from a topic during the conversation.\nC. Add the REST API as a tool and call it either by using a topic or agent instruction.\nHTTP Action (A): Copilot Studio includes a native HTTP Request node that can be embedded directly into a topic workflow. This node allows you to explicitly provide the target endpoint URL, select the appropriate method (GET, POST, etc.), append authentication headers (such as Authorization: Bearer <token>), and parse the returning structured JSON response into a runtime variable.\nREST API as a Tool / Action (C): Under the agent customization capabilities, you can extend an agent by adding actions or tools using a REST API definition (such as uploading an OpenAPI/Swagger file specification). Once registered, this tool can either be explicitly invoked via a topic action node or left for the agent's orchestration engine to dynamically call based on user instructions and trigger parameters.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Configure an HTTP action and invoke it from a topic during the conversation."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Create a topic variable and use a Formula to parse the API endpoint string."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Add the REST API as a tool and call it either by using a topic or agent instruction."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add the REST API as a knowledge source so the agent can query it during conversations."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Enable generative answers and rely on the model to fetch the REST API data automatically."}, {"k": "F", "t": "Create a global variable to store the API URL and call it using a Redirect node."}], "answer": ["A", "C"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 88, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company must enable an agent in Copilot Studio to execute operations exposed by an external MCP server. The MCP server publishes callable tools and requires authentication.\nThe agent must be able to invoke MCP tools during conversations.\nYou need to configure MCP tools so the agent can execute MCP tool calls.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "D. Configure authentication for the MCP server connection used by the agent.\nSecurity Requirements: When connecting Copilot Studio to an external MCP server via the MCP onboarding wizard, configuring the target authentication mechanism (such as providing an API key or setting up the OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow) is a mandatory configuration requirement.\nRuntime Execution: This authentication configuration behind the scene maps to a backend Power Platform custom connector structure, enabling the agent's orchestrator to pass valid security credentials securely during runtime conversational tool calls without exposing plain text configurations.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Enable generative answers so the model can automatically invoke MCP tools."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Add a Power Platform connector as real-time knowledge to replace MCP tools."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Add the MCP server output as an indexed knowledge source."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Configure authentication for the MCP server connection used by the agent."}], "answer": ["D"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 89, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent in Copilot Studio must retrieve real-time data from an external system that exposes a REST API. The API returns structured JSON responses and requires authentication.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• The agent calls a REST API during a conversation.\n• The REST API call runs with proper authentication.\n• The REST API response is available to the topic at runtime.\nYou need to configure the REST API to meet the requirements.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at au. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Configure a REST action and call it from a topic.\nWhy it fits: To invoke an external REST API directly during a guided conversational flow, you create a custom action or tool detailing the HTTP method and endpoint. This action is then added as a node inside the conversational topic canvas to execute the API call.\n2. Build an authenticated connection for the API.\nWhy it fits: Ensuring that the API executes securely requires setting up proper credentials (such as API keys, OAuth, or Bearer tokens). Building an authenticated connection handles these security layer mappings so the endpoint doesn't reject the incoming request.\n3. Store the API response in a topic variable.\nWhy it fits: Once the REST API returns a payload (such as JSON data), the agent needs to save that information to use it in subsequent dialog nodes. Saving the outputs to a topic variable allows the conversation to access, display, or filter the returned data fields at runtime.", "type": "match", "items": ["Store the API response in a topic variable.", "Add the API as an indexed knowledge source.", "Build an authenticated connection for the API.", "Configure a REST action and call it from a topic."], "slots": ["Agent calls a REST API during a conversation.", "REST API call runs with proper authentication.", "REST API response is available to the topic at runtime."], "answer": ["Configure a REST action and call it from a topic.", "Build an authenticated connection for the API.", "Store the API response in a topic variable."]}, {"id": 90, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is configuring generative answers for an agent in Copilot Studio agent.\nThe agent must use Azure AI Search to retrieve enterprise content, and all responses must be generated by a Microsoft Foundry model.\nThe agent sometimes generates answers that are not grounded in Azure AI Search content. The company requires that the agent only generate responses when relevant indexed content is retrieved.\nYou need to configure the agent so that its generative answer behavior meets the organization's requirements.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "C. Require grounding for generative answers and use Azure AI Search as the retrieval source.\nEnforcing Strict Grounding: In Copilot Studio, turning off the \"Allow ungrounded responses\" feature or selecting options that require grounding for generative answers forces the system to only output responses when it successfully retrieves relevant, matching text blocks from its indexes. If the question is outside the scope of the data found, the agent will gracefully fail over or state that it cannot find the information, eliminating ungrounded fabrications (hallucinations).\nRetrieval Mapping: Explicitly coupling this strict grounding policy with Azure AI Search directs the orchestration engine to use your designated indexed enterprise document catalog as the exclusive, authorized source of truth for the Microsoft Foundry model to summarize.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Use Azure AI Search as a tailback source and enable answer expansion."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Upload documents into the agent and disable fallback responses."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Require grounding for generative answers and use Azure AI Search as the retrieval source."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Enable citations and configure a public model endpoint."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Enable conversational boosting and select a Foundry-approved model."}], "answer": ["C"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 91, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company wants to extend an agent in Copilot Studio by calling an external REST API to retrieve and update data. To meet the business needs, the REST API must be added following a correct dependency order.\nYou need to add a REST API to the agent so it can be invoked during conversations.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "1. Add the REST API tool: You begin by navigating to the agent's tools section and initiating the setup process by selecting New tool > REST API.\n2. Configure the API endpoint details: Next, you supply the API specification layout (typically by uploading an OpenAPI/Swagger JSON file) to define the paths, methods, input parameters, and\nendpoint routes.\n3. Configure authentication for the API: Once the structural details of the endpoint are established, you configure the necessary security protocols, such as API keys, basic authentication, or OAuth 2.0 flow parameters.\n4. Test the API configuration: Finally, you validate the end-to-end integration by launching a test session to confirm that the agent can successfully reach the API endpoint, handle credentials properly, and receive valid responses.", "type": "order", "items": ["Add the REST API tool.", "Test the API configuration.", "Configure authentication for the API.", "Configure the API endpoint details."], "answer": ["Add the REST API tool.", "Configure the API endpoint details.", "Configure authentication for the API.", "Test the API configuration."]}, {"id": 92, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company needs an agent in Copilot Studio to complete tasks by interacting with a legacy desktop application that does NOT have APIs.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• The agent must interact with the application's UI.\n• The execution must run on supported infrastructure.\n• The builder must be able to observe how the tool runs during execution.\nYou need to configure the agent based on the requirements.\nWhich setting should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate settings to the correct requirements. You may use each setting once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Enable Computer use tool for the agent.\nWhy it fits: The Computer Use capability allows AI agents to read a digital display, move a cursor, click buttons, and type text just like a human operator. Enabling this tool is a prerequisite whenever an agent needs to programmatically navigate and interact directly with an application's user interface.\n2. Use a platform-hosted execution environment.\nWhy it fits: Operating a virtual desktop canvas or interacting with desktop elements requires secure, sandboxed compute resources. A platform-hosted execution environment ensures that these automation runs occur inside isolated, compliant, and pre-configured Microsoft-managed secure infrastructure.\n3. Monitor run details in the agent runtime view.\nWhy it fits: When diagnosing or observing tool behavior, builders utilize live telemetry screens. The agent runtime view provides real-time access to operational sequences, step-by-step logs, and live execution details as the agent triggers its configured tools.", "type": "match", "items": ["Enable Computer use tool for the agent.", "Monitor run details in the agent runtime view.", "Use a platform-hosted execution environment."], "slots": ["Agent interacts with the application's UI.", "Execution runs on supported infrastructure.", "Builder observes how the tool runs during execution."], "answer": ["Enable Computer use tool for the agent.", "Use a platform-hosted execution environment.", "Monitor run details in the agent runtime view."]}, {"id": 93, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A company is configuring an agent in Copilot Studio that uses a generative answers node inside multiple topics. The company requires responses to meet the following requirements:\n• Must use an executive summary format.\n• Must apply only within a specific topic.\n• Must continue using configured knowledge sources.\n• Must comply with connector security controls.\nYou need to configure a custom prompt to meet the formatting requirements.\nSolution: Add instructions in the custom prompt tool to restrict connector access during runtime.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "§ B. No\nWrong Tool for Security: The custom prompt tool is designed to provide natural language instructions that guide how a foundation model structures, formats, and sets the tone of its text output (e.g., enforcing an \"executive summary format\"). Writing text instructions in a prompt to try to programmatically restrict backend network access or data connectors at runtime is ineffective and insecure.\nHow Security is Handled: Connector security controls and data access boundaries are strictly enforced via the environment's security layer, authentication settings, user roles, and data loss prevention (DLP) policies— not by instructing the LLM via prompt engineering.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 94, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A company is configuring an agent in Copilot Studio that uses a generative answers node inside multiple topics. The company requires responses to meet the following requirements:\n• Must use an executive summary format.\n• Must apply only within a specific topic.\n• Must continue using configured knowledge sources.\n• Must comply with connector security controls.\nYou need to configure a custom prompt to meet the formatting requirements.\nSolution: Create a custom prompt tool in the specific topic that instructs the model to respond using an executive- summary structure.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "§ A. Yes\nFormatting and Structure: Creating a custom prompt tool and giving it natural language instructions (e.g., \"Respond using an executive-summary structure\") perfectly satisfies the requirement to alter the layout and tone of the model's response.\nTopic Isolation: Adding this custom prompt tool explicitly within the node structure of the specific topic ensures that the formatting constraint applies only within that exact interaction pathway, without globally altering the generative answer nodes present in the other multiple topics.\nPreserving Features: It overrides the formatting behavior while naturally adhering to the existing data boundaries, configured knowledge sources, and backend connector security controls already established for that agent's runtime environment.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 95, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is extending agents in Copilot Studio with both external capabilities and enterprise content access. Some user requests require grounded answers, while other requests require executing actions.\nTo meet the business needs, the agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Ground answers using results from enterprise data.\n• Invoke callable functions exposed through a standardized tool interface.\n• Write an update to a system by using an authenticated action during the conversation.\nYou need to integrate the correct approach to meet the requirements.\nWhat should you integrate for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate integrations to the correct requirements. You may use each integration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Connect to an MCP server and add its tools to the agent.", "Add Dataverse tables as a structured data knowledge source.", "Add a Power Platform connector tool and call it from a topic.", "Add a Copilot connector knowledge source for indexed content."], "slots": ["Ground answers using results from enterprise data.", "Invoke callable functions exposed through a standardized tool interface.", "Write an update to a system by using an authenticated action during the conversation."], "answer": ["Add a Copilot connector knowledge source for indexed content.", "Connect to an MCP server and add its tools to the agent.", "Add a Power Platform connector tool and call it from a topic."], "prompt": "Integrate the correct approach for each requirement. Items may be used once, more than once, or not at all."}, {"id": 96, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company plans to use Copilot connectors to ground answers from a third-party system for an agent in Copilot Studio.\nTo meet the business needs, responsibilities for configuring and using Copilot connectors must be separated, based on the following requirements:\n• For tenant administration, the connector must be available at the tenant level.\n• For agent runtime behavior, answers must be grounded from authorized sources.\n• For builder configuration, you must add the connector to the agent.\nYou need to determine how Copilot connector set-up responsibilities are handled across tenant, runtime, and builder stages.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configuration to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Call the connector knowledge source.", "Add the Copilot connector as a knowledge source.", "Create a new Copilot connector directly in Copilot Studio.", "Configure the Copilot connector in Microsoft 365 Admin Center.", "Configure the Copilot connector in Power Platform Admin Center."], "slots": ["Tenant administration", "Agent runtime behavior", "Builder configuration"], "answer": ["Configure the Copilot connector in Power Platform Admin Center.", "Add the Copilot connector as a knowledge source.", "Configure the Copilot connector in Microsoft 365 Admin Center."], "reuse": true, "note": "Answer image as shown; the answer image's placement looks unusual (tenant/builder choices may be swapped in the source). Transcribed exactly as shown."}, {"id": 97, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent in Copilot Studio to answer questions based on enterprise content.\nThe content is indexed in Azure AI Search. Responses must be generated by a Microsoft Foundry model.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Users are able to trace each answer back to its source.\n• The agent retrieves information from a centralized data index.\n• Responses are generated by an enterprise-approved foundation model.\nYou need to configure generative answers for the agent.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Upload documents directly into the agent.", "Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding data source.", "Instruct the agent to cite sources in knowledge settings.", "Deploy a model in Foundry to an agent and connect to Copilot Studio."], "slots": ["Users trace each answer back to its source.", "The agent retrieves information from a centralized data index.", "Responses are generated by an enterprise-approved foundational model."], "answer": ["Instruct the agent to cite sources in knowledge settings.", "Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding data source.", "Deploy a model in Foundry to an agent and connect to Copilot Studio."]}, {"id": 98, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent in Copilot Studio to generate structured responses for internal users.\nThe agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Consistently follow a reusable instruction template for response tone and structure.\n• Use an enterprise-approved foundation model for responses generated through the template.\nYou need to configure custom prompts so the agent uses the Microsoft Foundry model catalog.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Technical Justification\nCorrect Actions: D and E\nAction D (Select a model from the Foundry model catalog for the custom prompt configuration):\nThis action directly satisfies the requirement to \"Use an enterprise-approved foundation model for responses generated through the template.\" In Copilot Studio, custom prompts allow explicit model selection decoupled from the agent's default generative model. By configuring the custom prompt to target a specific model deployed via the Azure AI Foundry (formerly Azure AI Studio) model catalog, the solution enforces the use of that specific, approved model endpoint for the structured response generation, ensuring governance and consistency.\nAction E (Create a custom prompt template and apply it where the agent generates the structured response):\nThis action satisfies the requirement to \"Consistently follow a reusable instruction template for response tone and structure.\" Custom prompts in Copilot Studio function as reusable prompt engineering assets. They encapsulate the system instructions, few-shot examples, and output formatting logic (tone/structure) into a single version-controlled component. Applying this custom prompt within the specific topic node (e.g., a \"Generate Response\" action or Generative Answers node) ensures the structured output logic is centralized, reusable across topics, and separated from the conversational flow logic.\nWhy Other Options Are Incorrect\nA (Publish the agent to apply the Foundry model selection automatically):\nPublishing an agent deploys the current configuration to users; it does not configure model selection or prompt templates. Model selection for custom prompts is a design-time configuration performed in the authoring canvas, not a runtime side-effect of publishing.\nB (Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding source, so the model choice is enforced):\nAzure AI Search provides grounding data (RAG) for generative answers, not model routing or enforcement. The data source is orthogonal to the model selection; configuring a search index does not restrict or dictate which foundation model processes the prompt.\nC (Allow topic authors to define tone and structure directly in topic steps):\nDefining instructions inline within topic steps (e.g., inside a \"Generate Response\" node's instructions field) creates tight coupling and duplication. It violates the \"reusable instruction template\" requirement because changes to tone/structure would require editing every individual topic step rather than updating a single central custom prompt asset.\nF (Enable citations, so the model is restricted to approved outputs):\nCitations provide source attribution for grounded content (verifiability). They do not constrain the model selection, nor do they enforce a specific output structure or tone. They are a retrieval augmentation feature, not a prompt template or model governance feature.\nReferences\n1. Create and use custom prompts in Copilot Studio\n2. Use Azure AI Foundry models in Copilot Studio", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Publish the agent to apply the Foundry model selection automatically."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding source, so the model choice is enforced."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Allow topic authors to define tone and structure directly in topic steps."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Select a model from the Foundry model catalog for the custom prompt configuration."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Create a custom prompt template and apply it where the agent generates the structured response."}, {"k": "F", "t": "Enable citations, so the model is restricted to approved outputs."}], "answer": ["D", "E"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 99, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company integrates an MCP service that exposes ticket-management operations.\nThe MCP service requires authenticated requests and exposes multiple callable operations that must be selectively governed for agent use.\nTo meet the business needs, the MCP configuration must meet the following requirements:\n• Only the allowed capabilities must be callable during conversations.\n• Requests must succeed without embedding credentials in topic steps.\n• The agent must be configured to send requests to the correct backend.\nYou need to configure MCP tools for the agent.\nWhich setting should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate settings to the correct requirements. You may use each setting once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Server URL", "API key header X-API-Key", "CreateTicket, EscalateIncident, GetTicket, UpdateSLA"], "slots": ["Only the allowed capabilities must be callable during conversations.", "Requests must succeed without embedding credentials in topic steps.", "The agent must be configured to send requests to the correct backend."], "answer": ["CreateTicket, EscalateIncident, GetTicket, UpdateSLA", "API key header X-API-Key", "Server URL"]}, {"id": 100, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent in Copilot Studio must complete tasks by automating interactions with a legacy desktop application that does not use APIs.\nDuring testing, the builder must confirm that the automation runs in a supported execution context and can inspect how each automation step executes during conversations.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must:\n• Execute UI automation in a supported, platform-managed environment.\n• Provide visibility into how automation steps run during agent conversations.\nYou need to configure the agent based on the requirements.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Justification for Correct Options (B and C)\nC. Enable computer use for the agent so UI automation runs in the supported execution environment.This is the foundational prerequisite. The \"Computer Use\" capability in Copilot Studio provisions a platform- managed, cloud-hosted virtual machine (VM) specifically designed to execute UI automation (Desktop Flows / Power Automate Desktop) against legacy applications lacking APIs. Without enabling this feature, the agent lacks the isolated, secure, and scalable execution context required to interact with desktop UI elements. It abstracts the infrastructure management from the builder, ensuring the automation runs in a supported Microsoft-hosted environment rather than an on-premises gateway or unsupported local context.\nB. Inspect automation execution details by using the agent runtime view.This action satisfies the requirement for visibility into step execution during conversations. The Runtime View (accessible via the \"Test your agent\" pane or Activity map) provides a real-time, step-by-step trace of the agent's orchestration logic. When an agent invokes a Desktop Flow via Computer Use, the Runtime View surfaces the specific automation action calls, input/output parameters, duration, success/failure status, and any error messages generated during the conversation turn. This diagnostic capability is essential for validating logic and debugging UI automation behavior within the conversational flow.\nWhy Other Options Are Incorrect\nA. Publish the agent so automation settings are applied at runtime.Publishing deploys the agent to a live environment (e.g., Teams, Web, custom channels) but is not required to test or verify automation execution context. Automation settings (like Computer Use) are fully functional within the Test pane in the authoring canvas during development. Publishing is a lifecycle management step, not a configuration or debugging action for execution context validation.\nD. Add the desktop application as a knowledge source so the agent can reference its UI\nstructure.Knowledge sources in Copilot Studio utilize Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to ground LLM responses in unstructured data (documents, websites, Dataverse). They do not provide the agent with the ability to execute UI interactions, nor do they expose the application's UI Control hierarchy (selectors, handles) to the automation engine. Desktop Flow UI elements are captured during the flow authoring process in Power Automate Desktop, not ingested via Knowledge Sources.\nE. Configure a custom connector to simulate UI interactions through REST calls.A Custom Connector wraps a REST API (OpenAPI definition) to expose it as an action. The premise of the scenario is that the legacy application does not use APIs. Simulating UI interactions (clicks, keystrokes, screen scraping) via REST is architecturally impossible without a middleware layer exposing an API endpoint—which contradicts the constraint. Computer Use is the specific feature designed for API-less UI automation.\nReferences\n1. Enable computer use for agents in Copilot Studio\n2. Test and debug agents using the runtime view (Activity map)", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Publish the agent so automation settings are applied at runtime."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Inspect automation execution details by using the agent runtime view."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Enable computer use for the agent so UI automation runs in the supported execution environment."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add the desktop application as a knowledge source so the agent can reference its UI structure."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Configure a custom connector to simulate UI interactions through REST calls."}], "answer": ["B", "C"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 101, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company configures an agent flow that interacts with multiple systems to complete user requests.\nThe company must integrate external services, retrieve data, and notify users as part of the same automated flow run. To support this scenario, the agent flow must be able to do the following:\n• Call an external API.\n• Retrieve stored data from a supported internal data source.\n• Send a Teams message.\nYou need to select a connector or action type for each requirement.\nWhat should you use? To answer, move the appropriate connectors or actions to the correct requirements. You may use each connector or action once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Trigger a notification.", "Make an HTTPS request.", "Add a standard connector."], "slots": ["Call an external API.", "Retrieve stored data from a supported internal data source.", "Send a Teams message."], "answer": ["Make an HTTPS request.", "Add a standard connector.", "Trigger a notification."]}, {"id": 102, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent in Copilot Studio must retrieve real-time data from an external system that exposes a REST API. The API returns structured JSON data and requires authentication.\nThe agent must call the API during conversations to fulfill user requests.\nThe agent must retrieve real-time data from the external system by calling an authenticated REST API during a conversation.\nThe solution must use a supported mechanism that executes the REST API call and the call must be explicitly configured to run at runtime.\nYou need to determine which approaches add the REST API to the agent.\nWhich two solutions meet the goal? Each correct answer is a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "§ Correct Solutions Justification\nA. Configure an HTTP action and invoke it from a topic during the conversation.\nThis is a core supported mechanism in Copilot Studio. An HTTP action (formerly Custom Connector action) allows explicit configuration of the REST endpoint, authentication (API Key, OAuth 2.0, Bearer Token), request parameters, and response schema at design time. It executes synchronously at runtime when invoked via a Call an action node within a topic, enabling real-time data retrieval during the conversation flow.\nC. Add the REST API as a tool and call it either by using a topic or agent instruction.\nIn the modern Copilot Studio architecture (Generative Orchestration), Tools (specifically Connector actions or Plugin actions) are the primary abstraction for exposing external APIs to the orchestrator. Adding the authenticated REST API as a tool registers its OpenAPI specification, allowing the agent to autonomously invoke it based on agent instructions (generative planning) or explicitly via a topic. This satisfies the requirement for explicit runtime execution configuration and real-time retrieval.\nWhy Other Options Are Incorrect\nB. Create a topic variable and use a Formula to parse the API endpoint string.\nFormulas (Power Fx) are client-side expression evaluators used for string manipulation, logic, or data shaping within the canvas. They cannot perform network I/O, handle authentication handshakes, or execute HTTP requests against external REST endpoints.\nD. Add the REST API as a knowledge source so the agent can query it during conversations.\nKnowledge sources (Dataverse, SharePoint, URLs, Custom Data) are designed for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) over static or semi-static unstructured/semi-structured content (documents, lists). They rely on vector indexing and semantic search, not real-time synchronous REST API execution with authentication and structured JSON payloads.\nE. Enable generative answers and rely on the model to fetch the REST API data automatically.\nGenerative Answers (over knowledge sources) or Generative Orchestration cannot natively \"browse\" or call arbitrary authenticated REST APIs without a configured Tool/Action definition. The LLM does not have network access or credentials to invoke external systems directly; it requires a registered tool schema to generate the function call.\nF. Create a global variable to store the API URL and call it using a Redirect node.\nA Redirect node transfers the conversation context to a different URL (typically another bot, a web page, or a Canvas App). It performs an HTTP 302/redirect in the browser channel; it does not execute a server-side REST API call, process JSON responses, or maintain the conversation context for data retrieval.\nReferences\n1. Create and use HTTP actions (custom connectors) in Copilot Studio\n2. Add tools to extend your agent's capabilities", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Configure an HTTP action and invoke it from a topic during the conversation."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Create a topic variable and use a Formula to parse the API endpoint string."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Add the REST API as a tool and call it either by using a topic or agent instruction."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add the REST API as a knowledge source so the agent can query it during conversations."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Enable generative answers and rely on the model to fetch the REST API data automatically."}, {"k": "F", "t": "Create a global variable to store the API URL and call it using a Redirect node."}], "answer": ["A", "C"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 103, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company must enable an agent in Copilot Studio to execute operations exposed by an external MCP server. The MCP server publishes callable tools and requires authentication.\nThe agent must be able to invoke MCP tools during conversations.\nYou need to configure MCP tools so the agent can execute MCP tool calls.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nCorrect Option (D): Configure authentication for the MCP server connection used by the agent.\nIn Copilot Studio, an agent interacts with an external MCP server via a configured MCP Connection. Since the prompt explicitly states the MCP server \"requires authentication,\" the agent cannot establish a trusted session to discover or invoke the published tools without valid credentials (e.g., API Key, Bearer Token, or OAuth) configured on that connection object. Configuring authentication on the connection is the mandatory prerequisite for the agent's runtime to authorize tool calls against the MCP endpoint.\nWhy Option A is incorrect:\nEnabling generative answers controls the agent's ability to synthesize responses from knowledge sources; it does not govern the runtime capability to execute external tool calls. MCP tool invocation is handled by the agent's action orchestration layer, which requires a valid, authenticated connection definition, not merely generative answer settings.\nWhy Option B is incorrect:\nAdding a Power Platform connector as real-time knowledge facilitates data retrieval for grounding generative answers (RAG pattern), not action execution. Connectors and MCP servers are distinct integration mechanisms; a connector cannot \"replace\" MCP tools for the purpose of invoking callable operations exposed specifically via the Model Context Protocol.\nWhy Option C is incorrect:\nIndexing the MCP server output as a knowledge source enables semantic search over static or cached data for generative answers. It does not provide the runtime mechanism for the agent to dynamically invoke callable tools (functions) published by the live MCP server during a conversation turn.\nReferences\n1. Add a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server connection to your agent - Microsoft Learn\n2. Authentication types for MCP connections in Copilot Studio - Microsoft Learn", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Enable generative answers so the model can automatically invoke MCP tools."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Add a Power Platform connector as real-time knowledge to replace MCP tools."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Add the MCP server output as an indexed knowledge source."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Configure authentication for the MCP server connection used by the agent."}], "answer": ["D"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 104, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent in Copilot Studio must retrieve real-time data from an external system that exposes a REST API. The API returns structured JSON responses and requires authentication.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• The agent calls a REST API during a conversation.\n• The REST API call runs with proper authentication.\n• The REST API response is available to the topic at runtime.\nYou need to configure the REST API to meet the requirements.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at au. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Store the API response in a topic variable.", "Add the API as an indexed knowledge source.", "Build an authenticated connection for the API.", "Configure a REST action and call it from a topic."], "slots": ["Agent calls a REST API during a conversation.", "REST API call runs with proper authentication.", "REST API response is available to the topic at runtime."], "answer": ["Configure a REST action and call it from a topic.", "Build an authenticated connection for the API.", "Store the API response in a topic variable."], "prompt": "Configure the REST API to meet the requirements. Items may be used once, more than once, or not at all."}, {"id": 105, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is configuring generative answers for an agent in Copilot Studio agent.\nThe agent must use Azure AI Search to retrieve enterprise content, and all responses must be generated by a Microsoft Foundry model.\nThe agent sometimes generates answers that are not grounded in Azure AI Search content. The company requires that the agent only generate responses when relevant indexed content is retrieved.\nYou need to configure the agent so that its generative answer behavior meets the organization's requirements.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nWhy Option C is Correct:Option C directly addresses the core requirement: enforcing grounding fidelity. In Copilot Studio, the \"Require grounding for generative answers\" setting (configured within the Generative Answers node or Topic configuration) forces the orchestration layer to suppress response generation if the retrieval step (Azure AI Search) returns no relevant chunks or if the confidence score falls below the threshold. This eliminates hallucinations by ensuring the Foundry model only synthesizes answers when strict factual backing exists in the enterprise index. Specifying Azure AI Search as the retrieval source connects the agent to the enterprise content boundary, satisfying the architectural mandate to use indexed organizational data rather than public web knowledge or uploaded static files.\nWhy Option A is Incorrect:Using Azure AI Search as a fallback source implies a primary source (typically the agent's internal knowledge or uploaded documents) is queried first. \"Answer expansion\" allows the model to extrapolate beyond retrieved snippets, which increases the risk of ungrounded content generation. This configuration prioritizes answer fluency over grounding strictness, contradicting the requirement to only generate responses when relevant indexed content exists.\nWhy Option B is Incorrect:Uploading documents into the agent stores them in the Dataverse-backed knowledge base, not Azure AI Search. This bypasses the enterprise search infrastructure requirement (scalability, security trimming, index freshness). While disabling fallback responses prevents the model from using its parametric knowledge, the retrieval source remains the local Copilot Studio store, failing the mandate to use Azure AI Search as the retrieval backbone.\nWhy Option D is Incorrect:Enabling citations provides provenance for generated text but does not prevent generation when retrieval fails; the model will still answer ungrounded queries and cite nothing or hallucinate citations. Configuring a public model endpoint violates the explicit requirement to use a Microsoft Foundry model (hosted via Azure AI Foundry/Model Catalog), introducing data residency, compliance, and model governance risks.\nWhy Option E is Incorrect:Conversational Boosting is a legacy/orchestration feature designed to blend general LLM knowledge with specific knowledge sources to keep conversations flowing. It inherently allows the model to \"boost\" responses using internal parametric knowledge when retrieval is sparse, directly violating the \"only generate when relevant content is retrieved\" constraint. Selecting a \"Foundry-approved model\" satisfies the model requirement but the boosting behavior negates the grounding enforcement.\nReferences\n1. Configure generative answers in Copilot Studio - Grounding and data sources\n2. Use Azure AI Search as a knowledge source in Copilot Studio", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Use Azure AI Search as a tailback source and enable answer expansion."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Upload documents into the agent and disable fallback responses."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Require grounding for generative answers and use Azure AI Search as the retrieval source."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Enable citations and configure a public model endpoint."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Enable conversational boosting and select a Foundry-approved model."}], "answer": ["C"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 106, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company wants to extend an agent in Copilot Studio by calling an external REST API to retrieve and update data. To meet the business needs, the REST API must be added following a correct dependency order.\nYou need to add a REST API to the agent so it can be invoked during conversations.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "", "type": "order", "items": ["Add the REST API tool.", "Test the API configuration.", "Configure authentication for the API.", "Configure the API endpoint details."], "answer": ["Configure authentication for the API.", "Add the REST API tool.", "Configure the API endpoint details.", "Test the API configuration."]}, {"id": 107, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company needs an agent in Copilot Studio to complete tasks by interacting with a legacy desktop application that does NOT have APIs.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• The agent must interact with the application's UI.\n• The execution must run on supported infrastructure.\n• The builder must be able to observe how the tool runs during execution.\nYou need to configure the agent based on the requirements.\nWhich setting should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate settings to the correct requirements. You may use each setting once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Enable Computer use tool for the agent.", "Monitor run details in the agent runtime view.", "Use a platform-hosted execution environment."], "slots": ["Agent interacts with the application's UI.", "Execution runs on supported infrastructure.", "Builder observes how the tool runs during execution."], "answer": ["Enable Computer use tool for the agent.", "Use a platform-hosted execution environment.", "Monitor run details in the agent runtime view."]}, {"id": 108, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A company is configuring an agent in Copilot Studio that uses a generative answers node inside multiple topics. The company requires responses to meet the following requirements:\n• Must use an executive summary format.\n• Must apply only within a specific topic.\n• Must continue using configured knowledge sources.\n• Must comply with connector security controls.\nYou need to configure a custom prompt to meet the formatting requirements.\nSolution: Add instructions in the custom prompt tool to restrict connector access during runtime.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "No, the solution does not meet the goal. The requirement is to configure a custom prompt to enforce an executive summary format and topic-scoped application while preserving knowledge source usage. The proposed solution—adding instructions to \"restrict connector access during runtime\"—addresses neither the formatting nor the scoping requirements. Furthermore, connector security controls are enforced at the platform level via connection references, authentication configurations, and Dataverse security roles; they cannot be reliably governed or restricted through natural language instructions inside a custom prompt, which offers no enforceable security boundary.\nReferences\n§ Configure generative answers in Copilot Studio\n§ Manage connections and security in Copilot Studio", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 109, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A company is configuring an agent in Copilot Studio that uses a generative answers node inside multiple topics. The company requires responses to meet the following requirements:\n• Must use an executive summary format.\n• Must apply only within a specific topic.\n• Must continue using configured knowledge sources.\n• Must comply with connector security controls.\nYou need to configure a custom prompt to meet the formatting requirements.\nSolution: Create a custom prompt tool in the specific topic that instructs the model to respond using an executive- summary structure.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nThe solution meets all stated requirements for the following reasons:\nScope Isolation (Specific Topic): Creating the custom prompt as a tool directly inside the specific topic ensures the executive summary instruction is scoped exclusively to that topic's execution context. Unlike global prompt overrides or system message modifications—which apply universally across the agent—topic- scoped tools activate only when that specific topic is triggered, satisfying the requirement to apply formatting \"only within a specific topic.\"\nFormat Enforcement (Executive Summary): A custom prompt tool allows precise instruction engineering (e.g., \"Structure the response as an executive summary with Background, Key Findings, and\nRecommendations sections\"). Because the Generative Answers node invokes this tool at runtime, the LLM output is constrained to the mandated structure before being returned to the user.\nKnowledge Source Integrity: The Generative Answers node retains its configured knowledge source connections (Dataverse, SharePoint, public websites, custom data). The custom prompt tool acts as a post- retrieval formatting layer; it instructs the model how to synthesize the retrieved grounding data but does not replace, filter, or bypass the configured knowledge base, ensuring continued use of approved sources.\nSecurity & Connector Compliance: Custom prompt tools execute within the Copilot Studio managed runtime. They inherit the agent's authentication context and respect the connector security controls (e.g., OAuth connections, API key vaults, Data Loss Prevention policies) defined for the action calls or knowledge sources used by the topic. No external middleware or custom connector invocation is introduced that would circumvent the platform's security boundary.\nWhy Alternative Approaches Fail:\nGlobal Instructions/System Message: Would enforce the format across all topics, violating the \"specific topic\" constraint.\nMessage/Question Nodes with Hardcoded Text: Cannot dynamically synthesize variable knowledge source content into a structured format.\nPower Automate Flow / Custom Connector: Introduces unnecessary architectural complexity, latency, and potential security surface area (requiring separate DLP/connector policies) for a pure formatting task natively supported by the platform's prompt engineering capabilities.\nReferences\n1. Create and use custom prompt tools in Copilot Studio\n2. Generative answers node in Copilot Studio", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 110, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company must enable an agent in Copilot Studio to execute operations exposed by an external MCP server. The MCP server publishes callable tools and requires authentication.\nThe agent must be able to invoke MCP tools during conversations.\nYou need to configure MCP tools so the agent can execute MCP tool calls.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nCorrect Option: B – Add the MCP server to the agent’s tool configuration.In Microsoft Copilot Studio, Model Context Protocol (MCP) server integration is a native capability designed to expose external tools directly to the agent's orchestration layer. By adding the MCP server endpoint and authentication details (such as API keys or OAuth credentials) to the agent's Tools configuration pane, the platform automatically discovers the published tool definitions (schemas) via the MCP tools/list endpoint. This registers the tools as invocable functions available to the LLM planner during runtime. When the generative orchestrator determines a tool is required, it constructs the parameters and invokes the MCP tools/call endpoint transparently. This approach requires zero code, preserves the external server's logic, and leverages the standard MCP specification for secure, authenticated tool execution.\nWhy Option A is incorrect:Developing a custom connector and migrating functionality introduces unnecessary development overhead, duplication of business logic, and maintenance burden. MCP is a standardized protocol specifically designed to avoid this \"migration\" pattern; the agent consumes the tools in situ on the external server. Custom connectors are intended for traditional REST/GraphQL APIs lacking MCP support, not for replacing a compliant MCP server.\nWhy Option C is incorrect:Power Platform connectors added as \"real-time knowledge\" (or dataverse/plugin actions) serve a different architectural purpose: they typically enrich the context window with data (RAG pattern) or execute specific platform-bound actions. They do not natively speak the MCP protocol (JSON-RPC over SSE/stdio) required to negotiate capabilities and invoke tools on an external MCP server. This option confuses knowledge grounding with tool invocation mechanics.\nWhy Option D is incorrect:\"Generative answers\" (generative orchestration over knowledge sources) controls how the LLM synthesizes responses from unstructured data (websites, documents, Dataverse). It does not configure or enable tool calling interfaces. Tool invocation is governed by the Tools configuration and the Generative Actions (or classic topics) orchestration settings, not the knowledge indexing pipeline. Enabling generative answers alone provides no mechanism for the agent to discover, authenticate against, or execute MCP tool calls.\nReferences\n1. Add tools to your agent in Microsoft Copilot Studio (MCP Server configuration)\n2. Model Context Protocol (MCP) integration overview in Copilot Studio", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Develop a custom connector and migrate the MCP server functionality to connector actions."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Add the MCP server to the agent’s tool configuration."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Add a Power Platform connector as real-time knowledge to replace MCP tools."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Enable generative answers so the model can automatically invoke MCP tools."}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 111, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses multiple Copilot Studio agents that perform specialized tasks.\nYou need to configure a Copilot Studio agent in your environment to connect to an external agent.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Correct Option Justification (A):\nConfiguring a Copilot Studio agent to connect to an external agent requires establishing an Agent-to-Agent (A2A) communication channel. The A2A protocol defines a standardized mechanism for agent discovery, capability negotiation, and secure task delegation. By providing the A2A endpoint information (typically the Agent Card/well-known URL) for the target agent, the orchestrating agent can dynamically discover the remote agent's skills, invoke them via structured task requests, and handle asynchronous responses or streaming updates. This native integration preserves context, supports complex multi-turn delegation, and adheres to the enterprise-grade governance model of the Power Platform.\nWhy Other Options Are Less Suitable:\nB (Add a Power Platform connector tool): Connectors are designed for invoking static REST APIs, OData services, or legacy systems via predefined OpenAPI definitions. They lack the dynamic discovery, capability negotiation, and conversational context-passing semantics required for agent-to-agent orchestration.\nWrapping an A2A endpoint in a custom connector would bypass the native orchestration layer, introducing maintenance overhead and losing built-in authentication/authorization handling.\nC (Upload documentation about the target agent): Uploading documentation enables Generative Answers or Knowledge grounding for informational queries, but it does not provide an executable runtime interface. The agent cannot \"call\" documentation; it can only retrieve text snippets. This approach cannot trigger actions, pass parameters, or receive structured results from the external agent.\nD (Enable generative answers to call the other agent): Generative Answers orchestrates LLM-based response synthesis over indexed knowledge sources. It does not expose an action invocation framework capable of performing authenticated RPC-style calls to another autonomous agent's skillset. Relying on prompt engineering to \"call\" an external agent is non-deterministic, insecure, and unsupported for production workloads.\nReferences\nMicrosoft Learn: Connect agents using Agent-to-Agent (A2A) in Copilot Studio\nMicrosoft Learn: Agent-to-Agent (A2A) protocol overview", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Provide A2A endpoint information for the target agent."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Add a Power Platform connector tool that calls the other agent."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Upload documentation about the target agent for referencing."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Enable generative answers to call the other agent."}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 112, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses multiple Copilot Studio agents that perform specialized tasks.\nYou need to configure a Copilot Studio agent in your environment to connect to an external agent.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nCorrect Option (A): Provide A2A endpoint information for the target agent.\nCopilot Studio implements the Agent-to-Agent (A2A) protocol as the native mechanism for inter-agent communication and delegation. Configuring an agent to connect to an external agent requires registering the target agent's A2A endpoint (typically a /a2a or /.well-known/agent.json URL) within the source agent's settings. This establishes a trusted, standardized contract for capability discovery, task delegation, state management, and streaming responses between the orchestrating agent and the specialized skill agent without custom code or middleware.\nWhy Option B is incorrect: Enable generative answers to call the other agent.\nGenerative Answers (or Generative Orchestration) are designed for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) over unstructured knowledge sources (dataverse, SharePoint, websites). They synthesize natural language responses from content chunks; they do not expose an invocation interface to execute code, trigger workflows, or delegate conversational control to another autonomous agent runtime.\nWhy Option C is incorrect: Add a Power Platform connector tool that calls the other agent.\nWhile technically possible to call an agent's HTTP endpoint via a custom connector, this approach bypasses the native A2A protocol benefits. It requires manual handling of authentication, schema mapping, streaming response parsing, conversation state synchronization, and error handling. The A2A endpoint configuration (Option A) abstracts this complexity, providing a first-class, managed integration surface specifically designed for agent interoperability.\nWhy Option D is incorrect: Add the target agent as an indexed knowledge source.\nIndexing an agent as a knowledge source treats it as static documentation for grounding answers. It allows the source agent to read about the target agent's capabilities but provides zero mechanism for invoking the target agent's logic, passing parameters, or receiving structured execution results. This is a read-only pattern, unsuitable for task delegation.\nReferences\n1. Connect to external agents (A2A) in Copilot Studio\n2. Agent-to-Agent (A2A) protocol overview", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Provide A2A endpoint information for the target agent."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Enable generative answers to call the other agent."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Add a Power Platform connector tool that calls the other agent."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add the target agent as an indexed knowledge source."}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 113, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is building an agent that uses an agent flow to retrieve data from an external system and return structured output.\nThe company needs to create a flow that the agent can invoke to process requests and return results. To support this business need, the flow must meet the following requirements:\n• Accept parameters from the agent.\n• Execute an action to retrieve data from the database.\n• Ensure the agent receives the relevant output information.\n• Validate before it is used by the agent.\nYou need to create a usable agent flow that the agent can invoke.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "", "type": "order", "items": ["Add the connector action and configure the connection.", "Create a new agent flow.", "Define output values for the flow.", "Test the flow run.", "Define input values for the flow."], "answer": ["Create a new agent flow.", "Define input values for the flow.", "Add the connector action and configure the connection.", "Define output values for the flow.", "Test the flow run."]}, {"id": 114, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is integrating an agent in Copilot Studio into a custom application by using the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK.\nThe project requirements specify that the deployment workflow must follow a strict dependency-driven sequence.\n• The agent must be published before configuration values are retrieved.\n• End-to-end validation must occur before production deployment.\nYou need to plan the deployment workflow to integrate the agent.\nWhich four actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "", "type": "order", "items": ["Register a new application in Microsoft Entra ID.", "Configure and enable the agent's target integration channel.", "Validate the end-to-end conversation workflow in a test environment.", "Publish the agent.", "Retrieve current agent configuration values.", "Implement the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK in the custom app by using the retrieved configuration."], "answer": ["Publish the agent.", "Retrieve current agent configuration values.", "Implement the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK in the custom app by using the retrieved configuration.", "Validate the end-to-end conversation workflow in a test environment."]}, {"id": 115, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company is deploying an agent that uses generative responses. During pilot testing, some responses are blocked by responsible AI filtering. The company has Application Insights enabled.\nYou need to enable the following:\n• Must implement safeguards to block unsafe or harmful content.\n• Must implement protections for user prompts and model-generated responses.\n• Must be able to investigate why responses are filtered.\nYou need to configure Responsible AI safeguards and diagnostics.\nWhich setting should you configure for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Block unsafe content before responses are returned.", "options": ["Disable generative responses in the agent.", "Increase the context length to improve answer completeness.", "Configure the content filtering level for AI-generated content."], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Identify why a response was filtered.", "options": ["Review Azure Application Insights telemetry.", "Move the agent to a different environment region.", "Review topic trigger phrases to reduce fallback usage."], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Determine which user input triggered the filter.", "options": ["Review publishing history.", "Check connector run history.", "Review conversation transcripts."], "answer": 2}], "prompt": "Configure Responsible AI safeguards and diagnostics."}, {"id": 116, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company must enable an agent in Copilot Studio to execute operations exposed by an external MCP server. The MCP server publishes callable tools and requires authentication.\nThe agent must be able to invoke MCP tools during conversations.\nYou need to configure MCP tools so the agent can execute MCP tool calls.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nOption A is correct because integrating an external Model Context Protocol (MCP) server into a Copilot Studio agent requires an explicit connection configuration. Since the MCP server publishes callable tools and enforces authentication, the agent cannot discover or invoke these tools until a valid connection—complete with the required authentication mechanism (e.g., API Key, OAuth, Bearer Token)—is established in the Copilot Studio authoring canvas. Configuring this authentication allows the agent's orchestrator to securely hand off function calls to the MCP endpoint at runtime.\nOption B is incorrect because publishing the agent does not automatically provision or authenticate connections to external MCP servers. Default settings only enable built-in or previously configured actions; they do not perform service discovery or credential injection for custom external tool endpoints.\nOption C is incorrect because Power Platform connectors and MCP tools represent distinct integration patterns. Connectors wrap REST APIs via OpenAPI definitions for use as actions or plug-ins, whereas MCP exposes a standardized protocol for tool invocation. Adding a connector as \"real-time knowledge\" (typically used for RAG over Dataverse or Graph) does not surface MCP tool schemas or handle the MCP protocol handshake required for tool execution.\nOption D is incorrect because enabling generative answers configures the Large Language Model (LLM) to generate responses grounded in knowledge sources (websites, Dataverse, files). It does not grant the agent the ability to execute function calls against an external MCP server. Tool invocation requires the orchestrator to have a registered, authenticated connection to the tool provider, which generative answers alone do not provide.\nReferences\n1. Connect to external tools with Model Context Protocol (MCP) in Copilot Studio\n2. Authentication for actions and connectors in Copilot Studio", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Configure authentication for the MCP server connection used by the agent."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Publish the agent and rely on default settings for tool availability."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Add a Power Platform connector as real-time knowledge to replace MCP tools."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Enable generative answers so the model can automatically invoke MCP tools."}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 117, "case": "Fabrikam Inc", "series": false, "text": "You need to design a solution that provides Fabrikam Inc. with a multi-agent design approach with existing agents.\nWhich approach should you use for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate approaches to the correct requirements. You may use each approach once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Delegate the request to a tools-first agent.", "Delegate the request to a data-access agent.", "Delegate the request to a knowledge-focused agent.", "Add all logic directly into a single agent and its child agents."], "slots": ["Answer policy questions based on technical requirements.", "Meet metric requirements.", "Meet transaction requirements."], "answer": ["Delegate the request to a knowledge-focused agent.", "Delegate the request to a data-access agent.", "Delegate the request to a tools-first agent."], "reuse": true}, {"id": 118, "case": "Fabrikam Inc", "series": false, "text": "You need to configure generative answers so the agent meets Fabrikam Inc’s business requirements.\nWhich solutions should you use? To answer, move the appropriate solutions to the correct requirements. You may use each solution once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Upload documents directly into the agent.", "Select a model from the Foundry model catalog.", "Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding data source.", "Instruct the agent to cite sources in knowledge settings."], "slots": ["Users must be able to trace each answer back to its source.", "The agent must retrieve information from a centralized index.", "Responses must be generated by an enterprise-approved foundation model."], "answer": ["Instruct the agent to cite sources in knowledge settings.", "Configure Azure AI Search as the grounding data source.", "Select a model from the Foundry model catalog."]}, {"id": 119, "case": "Fabrikam Inc", "series": false, "text": "You need to ensure that performance-related questions are handled in accordance with Fabrikam Inc.’s defined architecture and business requirements.\nWhich capability should Operations Concierge use?", "expl": "The correct capability is a data agent integrated into the multi-agent solution. This aligns directly with the defined architecture and business requirements for the following reasons:\nArchitectural Mandate: The technical requirements explicitly state that the Operations Concierge must coordinate specialist capabilities and specifically delegate \"Operational metrics\" to a Fabric Data Agent that reads governed business data through the Microsoft Fabric semantic model.\nBusiness Requirement Fulfillment: The Metrics requirement demands \"up-to-date metrics in a structured format\" for service performance indicators (backlog, SLA risk, parts shortages, dispatch delays). A Fabric Data Agent is purpose-built to query semantic models over governed, real-time operational data, ensuring accuracy and structure suitable for weekly reviews.\nResolution of Identified Defect: The case study identifies a recurring issue where \"some metric questions are answered generatively instead of being routed to the Fabric Data Agent.\" Selecting the data agent capability enforces the required delegation pattern, preventing the primary agent from hallucinating or approximating metrics using its internal knowledge.\nSeparation of Concerns: The multi-agent architecture isolates specialist responsibilities. The Foundry agent (Option A) handles summarization/classification, MCP servers (Option C) handle transactional updates and warranty validation, and Azure AI Search (Option D) handles policy retrieval. Only the Data Agent addresses the operational metrics domain.\nWhy other options are unsuitable:\nOption A (Foundry Agent): Configured strictly for summarization and classification of long-form text (e.g., call transcripts), not quantitative metric retrieval.\nOption C (MCP Server Tools): Designed for authenticated transactional operations (PartsOps) or read-only warranty validation (WarrantyRules), not for querying analytical semantic models for KPIs.\nOption D (Azure AI Search): Supports the \"Policy and procedure Q&A\" capability for grounded, traceable answers from unstructured documents, not structured, real-time operational metrics from a semantic model.\nReferences:\nCopilot Studio: Add a data agent (preview) to your agent\nMicrosoft Fabric: Data agent in Copilot Studio", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "A Foundry agent configured for summarization and classification."}, {"k": "B", "t": "A data agent integrated into the multi-agent solution."}, {"k": "C", "t": "An MCP server tool that executes transactional operations."}, {"k": "D", "t": "An Azure AI Search knowledge source configured for enterprise policy retrieval."}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 120, "case": "Fabrikam Inc", "series": false, "text": "You need to enable collaboration requirements for Operations Concierge.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Correct Answer: A and E\nTechnical Justification:\nIn Copilot Studio, agent-to-agent delegation (collaboration) requires the target agent (Service Desk Agent) to be both published and explicitly configured to allow connections from other agents.\nOption A (Enable allow connections from other agents on the Service Desk Agent): This is a mandatory security and governance setting on the target agent. Without enabling \"Allow connections from other agents\" in the Service Desk Agent's settings, the primary agent (Operations Concierge) cannot establish a delegation link or invoke it as a skill, regardless of publish state.\nOption E (Publish the Service Desk Agent): Only published agents are discoverable and invokable as collaborator skills in other agents. An agent in draft/unpublished state does not expose a runtime endpoint for delegation. Publishing creates the immutable version required for the primary agent to reference.\nWhy other options are incorrect:\nOption B (Replace the Service Desk Agent with an MCP server): The requirement explicitly mandates reusing the existing Service Desk Agent logic (ticket creation/routing) via delegation. Replacing it with an MCP server re-implements logic rather than reusing the asset, violating the \"avoid duplicating common assets\" constraint and the specific collaboration requirement.\nOption C (Publish Operations Concierge to enable delegation automatically): Publishing the source agent (Operations Concierge) does not configure the target agent (Service Desk Agent) to accept calls. Delegation is configured on the primary agent by adding a published, connection-enabled target agent; it is not an automatic side-effect of publishing the primary agent.\nOption D (Add the Service Desk Agent as an indexed knowledge source): Knowledge sources (e.g., Azure AI Search, Dataverse, SharePoint) provide grounding data for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). They do not provide executable conversational capabilities or transactional delegation. Agent collaboration is an orchestration pattern, not a knowledge retrieval pattern.\nReferences:\nCopilot Studio - Collaborate with other agents (Add a collaborator agent)\n§ Copilot Studio - Publish an agent", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Enable allow connections from other agents on the Service Desk Agent."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Replace the Service Desk Agent with an MCP server."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Publish Operations Concierge to enable delegation automatically."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Add the Service Desk Agent as an indexed knowledge source."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Publish the Service Desk Agent."}], "answer": ["A", "E"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 121, "case": "Fabrikam Inc", "series": false, "text": "You need to add Fabrikam Inc.’s MCP servers as a tool so the agent can perform the required operations during conversations.\nWhich setting should you configure for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate settings to the correct requirements. You may use each setting once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Use the same authentication type for the MCP servers.", "Configure a server description about the MCP server role.", "Select API key as the authentication type for the MCP server.", "Select OAuth 2.0 as the authentication type for the MCP server."], "slots": ["Implement PartsOps MCP server actions in alignment with the technical requirements.", "Configure the WarrantyRules MCP server in alignment with the technical requirements.", "Ensure that the agent has enough server context to decide when to call the MCP server."], "answer": ["Select OAuth 2.0 as the authentication type for the MCP server.", "Select API key as the authentication type for the MCP server.", "Configure a server description about the MCP server role."], "prompt": "Add Fabrikam Inc.'s MCP servers as a tool so the agent can perform the required operations during conversations."}, {"id": 122, "case": "Blue Yonder ", "series": false, "text": "You need to implement tool usage in topics that meet the Blue Yonder design and governance requirements.\nWhich implementation method should you use for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate implementation methods to the correct requirements. You may use each implementation method once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Enable generative orchestration.\nWhy it fits: Generative orchestration (also known as dynamic chaining) allows the conversational agent to use AI to dynamically select and execute the most appropriate tool or topic based on the user's intent, rather than following a rigidly pre-authored conversation path.\n2. Add a tool node.\nWhy it fits: If you need an action or business process to trigger at a precise, deterministic moment within a custom conversation path, you manually insert a tool node directly into that specific step of the topic canvas.\n3. Add a tool node.\nWhy it fits: In Copilot Studio's unified authoring canvas, external operations—such as calling Power Platform connectors to fetch data from live systems—are encapsulated as Tools. To execute a connector action and bring that retrieved data back into the conversation dialog, you use a tool node.", "type": "match", "items": ["Add a tool node.", "Add an Adaptive Card.", "Enable generative orchestration.", "Display a static message instead of using a tool.", "Add a Question node for the user to choose the tool."], "slots": ["Let the agent decide when to run a tool based on user input.", "Run a specific agent flow at a defined step in a topic.", "Use a connector action to retrieve data during a conversation."], "answer": ["Enable generative orchestration.", "Add a tool node.", "Add a tool node."], "reuse": true}, {"id": 123, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A company is configuring an agent in Copilot Studio that uses a generative answers node inside multiple topics. The company requires responses to meet the following requirements:\n• Must use an executive summary format.\n• Must apply only within a specific topic.\n• Must continue using configured knowledge sources.\n• Must comply with connector security controls.\nYou need to configure a custom prompt to meet the formatting requirements.\nSolution: Configure the custom prompt tool at the topic level so that other topics are not affected.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nThe solution meets the goal because configuring the custom prompt at the topic level provides the precise scope isolation required by the requirements.\nScope Isolation (Requirement: \"Apply only within a specific topic\"): In Copilot Studio, prompt configurations follow a hierarchy. A prompt configured at the Topic level overrides the Agent (global) level settings strictly for the execution context of that specific topic. This ensures the executive summary format is enforced only when the Generative Answers node runs inside that topic, leaving other topics to use the default global prompt or their own topic-level overrides.\nKnowledge Source Retention (Requirement: \"Continue using configured knowledge sources\"): The Custom Prompt tool modifies the instruction set sent to the LLM (formatting, tone, structure) but does not alter the retrieval configuration (knowledge sources, search parameters) defined on the Generative Answers node itself. The node continues to query the assigned knowledge sources and passes the retrieved chunks to the LLM alongside the custom prompt.\nSecurity Compliance (Requirement: \"Comply with connector security controls\"): Connector security controls (authentication, data loss prevention policies, Microsoft Entra ID permissions) are enforced at the connector/service principal level and the platform runtime layer. They are independent of the prompt engineering layer. Applying a custom prompt at the topic level introduces no changes to data connectivity or permission scopes.\nWhy Agent/Global Level is Incorrect: Configuring the prompt at the Agent (global) level would apply the executive summary format to all topics utilizing Generative Answers, violating the explicit requirement to restrict the behavior to a single specific topic.\nReferences\n1. Work with the Generative Answers node in Copilot Studio - Microsoft Learn\n2. Create custom prompts for generative answers in Copilot Studio - Microsoft Learn", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 124, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is developing an agent in Copilot Studio and wants to evaluate its behavior before expanding access. The team needs a repeatable, consistent method to test the agent using predefined interactions.\nYou need to ensure that agent responses can be evaluated consistently across multiple test runs.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nOption C is correct because defining test sets (formerly known as test cases or test suites) in Copilot Studio provides a structured, repeatable framework for regression testing. Test sets allow developers to curate a specific collection of user prompts (inputs) paired with expected outcomes, such as targeted topics, generated answers, or specific variable values. Executing these test sets across multiple runs produces deterministic comparison reports, enabling consistent evaluation of agent behavior, detection of regressions, and validation of fixes before broadening distribution.\nOption A is incorrect because reviewing historical production evaluation scores relies on uncontrolled, variable real-world user inputs. This approach lacks repeatability and cannot isolate specific logic changes, making it unsuitable for pre-deployment validation of specific agent behaviors.\nOption B is incorrect as enabling telemetry and analytics provides observational monitoring of live traffic (intent matching rates, fallback triggers). While valuable for post-deployment health checks, it is a passive monitoring strategy that does not offer the controlled, predefined input/expectation mechanism required for repeatable pre-release testing.\nOption D is incorrect because shifting to a qualitative evaluation method (e.g., manual human review of conversations) introduces subjectivity and lacks the automation and consistency required for repeatable execution across multiple test runs. It does not scale for regression testing.\nOption E is incorrect since publishing to additional channels expands the blast radius to real end-users. This is a distribution action, not a testing methodology, and exposes unverified agent behavior to production audiences rather than evaluating it in a controlled manner.\nReferences\n1. Test your agent with test sets in Microsoft Copilot Studio\n2. Evaluate your agent's performance in Microsoft Copilot Studio", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Review evaluation scores from previous production sessions."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Enable telemetry and analytics to monitor intent matches, fallback usage, and response frequency."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Define test sets of prompts and expectations."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Change the evaluation method to a qualitative approach."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Publish the agent to additional channels for broader usage."}], "answer": ["C"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 125, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company builds agents in Copilot Studio in a development environment.\nThe agents are packaged inside a solution and deployed by using Power Platform Pipelines to test and production environments.\nThe company has the following requirements that must be met:\n• Production deployments are limited to a defined set of users who are responsible for that stage.\n• The same solution is reused across all environments without editing its components.\n• The production environment always receives a locked-down version of the solution.\nYou need to configure the pipeline and solution behavior to meet the requirements.\nWhat should you configure? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Deploy the solution as managed.", "Deploy the solution as unmanaged.", "Use environment variables in the solution.", "Configure stage access for the production stage."], "slots": ["Limit users who can deploy to production.", "Reuse the same solution across environments without modifying its components.", "Ensure production receives a locked-down version of the solution."], "answer": ["Configure stage access for the production stage.", "Use environment variables in the solution.", "Deploy the solution as managed."], "prompt": "Configure the pipeline and solution behavior to meet the requirements. Items may be used once, more than once, or not at all."}, {"id": 126, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You run multiple evaluation tests for an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding user access.\nThe tests provide the following results:\n• Each evaluation case is reported as meeting or missing the expected response.\n• Some evaluation cases fail repeatedly across several runs.\n• Each run displays the expected response and the response generated by the agent.\nYou need to provide a conclusion based on the evaluation results.\nWhich conclusions should you make? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Evaluation case meeting or missing expected response.", "options": ["Pass or fail status", "Failure configuration", "Production readiness"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Some evaluation cases fail repeatedly.", "options": ["Prompt revisions", "Consistent outcomes", "Intermittent outcomes"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Each run displays expected response and agent response.", "options": ["Deployment approval", "Root cause of failures", "Expected and generated responses"], "answer": 2}]}, {"id": 127, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent in Copilot Studio is evaluated using a fixed test set and an automated evaluation method.\nAfter running the evaluation, the team observes that the same three interactions fail in every run, while all other interactions pass consistently.\nYou need to use the evaluation results to determine an accurate conclusion.\nWhat should you conclude from the evaluation results?", "expl": "Justification for Correct Answer (C):\nIn Copilot Studio, automated evaluation using a fixed test set is designed to measure the regression status and functional accuracy of specific topics, entities, and conversation flows. When a consistent subset of interactions fails across repeated runs while the majority pass, the results provide high-fidelity signal isolation. This deterministic failure pattern indicates specific dialogue paths, trigger phrases, or action orchestrations (e.g., Power Automate flows, Bot Framework skills, or generative answers configurations) that contain logic errors, knowledge gaps, or schema mismatches. Consequently, the evaluation output precisely scopes the remediation effort to the failing functional areas—such as specific topics requiring better trigger training, entity extraction fixes, or adaptive card rendering corrections—rather than implying systemic architectural failure.\nWhy Other Options Are Less Suitable:\nA. The agent's overall design is flawed and requires a full redesign.\nThis is an over-generalization. A >90% pass rate (implied by \"all other interactions pass consistently\") demonstrates that the core architecture, orchestration layer, and majority of the topic map are functioning as designed. A full redesign ignores the principle of targeted iteration based on empirical evidence; the data suggests localized defects, not structural unsoundness.\nB. The failing interactions are caused by an issue in the underlying language model.\nCopilot Studio evaluations test the agent configuration (topics, actions, knowledge sources, prompt engineering), not the base LLM capability in isolation. Consistent failures on identical inputs point to deterministic configuration issues—such as incorrect trigger phrases, missing entities, flawed adaptive cards, or misconfigured generative answers prompts—rather than non-deterministic LLM hallucination or capability limits. Blaming the underlying model diverts focus from the configurable layer the maker controls.\nD. The agent should not be deployed.\nDeployment gating depends on business risk tolerance and the criticality of the failing paths. Evaluation results are a diagnostic tool, not an automatic deployment blocker. If the three failing interactions represent low-risk edge cases (e.g., obscure FAQ variations), a phased deployment with a hotfix plan is standard practice. An absolute \"do not deploy\" stance contradicts the iterative ALM (Application Lifecycle Management) workflow supported by Copilot Studio’s environment strategy (Dev/Test/Prod).\nReferences:\n1. Evaluate your agent's performance in Copilot Studio\n2. Test and iterate your agent using the test pane and analytics", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "The agent's overall design is flawed and requires a full redesign."}, {"k": "B", "t": "The failing interactions are caused by an issue in the underlying language model."}, {"k": "C", "t": "The outcomes isolate which portions of the agent's functional scope require remediation."}, {"k": "D", "t": "The agent should not be deployed."}], "answer": ["C"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 128, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team has an existing agent in Copilot Studio and must move it to a different target location without rebuilding the agent. Some builders use Copilot Studio in the web app, while others use Copilot Studio in Microsoft Teams.\nYou need a supported approach to move the existing agent to the target location using ALM practices. The solution must avoid rebuilding the agent.\nYou need to move the existing agent to the target location using a supported method.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "§ Justification for Option C\nOption C is the correct approach because it leverages the Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) capabilities native to the Microsoft Power Platform, which underpins Copilot Studio. Agents are solution- aware components; adding an existing agent to a custom unmanaged solution in the source environment packages the agent definition, topics, entities, connections, and associated Power Automate flows as a single transportable unit. Exporting this solution as a managed or unmanaged file and importing it into the target environment preserves the component metadata, GUIDs, and dependencies, ensuring fidelity and enabling future patch/upgrade cycles. This is the only Microsoft-supported method for promoting agents across environments (e.g., Dev → Test → Prod) without manual reconstruction.\nWhy Other Options Are Unsuitable\nOption A (Remove agent components): Removing components from a solution breaks the referential integrity of the solution package. Solutions are designed to contain all dependencies; stripping components causes import failures or runtime errors due to missing metadata and broken references to flows, entities, or environment variables.\nOption B (Duplicate and manually recreate): Manual recreation violates core ALM principles. It introduces configuration drift, human error, and fails to preserve system-generated IDs (GUIDs), breaking links to Dataverse tables, Power Automate flows, and analytics history. It is unsupported for governance and compliance scenarios.\nOption D (Publish to channel): Channel publishing (e.g., Teams, Web, Mobile) is a runtime deployment action, not an environment transport mechanism. It makes the agent available to end-users in the current environment but does not migrate the authoring artifacts, logic, or configuration to a separate target environment (e.g., a different tenant or region).\nReferences\n1. Application lifecycle management (ALM) for agents in Copilot Studio\n2. Add existing components to a solution", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Remove agent components directly from the solution so that export and import will succeed."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Duplicate the agent in the source and manually recreate the changes in the target to avoid solution dependencies."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Create a custom solution, add the existing agent to the solution, export the solution with the agent, and import the solution into the target environment."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Publish the agent to a channel and use the channel configuration as the transport mechanism to the target environment."}], "answer": ["C"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 129, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You are setting up an evaluation for an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding access to more users.\nYou must configure the evaluation so that the agent is tested by using prepared interactions rather than impromptu conversations.\nThe evaluation should provide the following functionality for users:\nAn administrator updates a sensitive configuration value. The administrator requires the change to take effect at runtime without republishing the agent.\nA builder needs to confirm a configuration value but must not be able to modify it from Copilot Studio. An administrator updates a non-secret configuration value and requires the updated value to be used by the published agent at runtime.\nYou need to create a test set-based evaluation that supports consistent test runs for each scenario.\nWhat should you configure for each scenario? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Update a sensitive configuration value and the change takes effect at runtime without republishing the agent.", "options": ["Only in production", "Only after publishing", "Secret environment variable"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Confirm a configuration value but not modify it from Copilot Studio.", "options": ["Publishing settings", "Conversation volume", "Managed environment variable"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Update a non-secret configuration value and the updated value needs to be used by the published agent at runtime.", "options": ["String environment variable", "Live chat history", "Channel rollout status"], "answer": 0}]}, {"id": 130, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is preparing to evaluate an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding access to additional users. The team must select an evaluation method that meets the following requirements:\n• Use a fixed set of prepared interactions.\n• Determine responses against a predefined baseline.\n• Support consistent comparison across repeated test runs.\nYou need to determine the evaluation method that satisfies the evaluation requirements.\nWhich evaluation method should you select for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate option in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Use a fixed set of prepared interactions.", "options": ["Use a curated prompt list.", "Use only live conversations.", "Expand access to increase traffic."], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Establish a baseline for determining responses.", "options": ["Confirm publishing status as success.", "Rely on general impressions of quality.", "Compare against predefined expected outcomes."], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Support repeatable comparisons across runs.", "options": ["Collect user feedback after rollout.", "Use a consistent scoring evaluation matrix.", "Change channel settings before publishing."], "answer": 1}]}, {"id": 131, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A solution containing an agent in Copilot Studio is moved across environments. The agent relies on configuration values that are different between environments and are managed as environment variables.\nYou must ensure the following:\n• Configuration values are managed through environment variables that supports ALM.\n• Runtime behavior reflects updates to environment variable values.\nYou need to implement environment-variable usage that supports ALM and ensures correct runtime behavior.\nWhich three actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Technical Justification for Correct Options (C, D, F)\nC. Use environment variables as read-only in Copilot Studio and change their values in the appropriate administrative authoring experience.\nThis is the prescribed ALM pattern. In Copilot Studio, environment variables are treated as read-only references within the agent authoring canvas (topics, actions, Power Fx formulas). The definition (schema, default value) moves with the solution, but the runtime value is intentionally decoupled and managed exclusively in the target environment via the Power Platform Admin Center or Maker Portal (Solutions > Environment Variables). This separation ensures the solution package remains immutable and portable across Dev/Test/Prod without carrying sensitive or environment-specific configuration values inside the solution zip. D. Store external reference values as environment variables so the agent solution can move between environments while keeping the agent definition the same.\nThis describes the core architectural purpose of environment variables in ALM. By externalizing configuration —such as API endpoints, connection string references, feature flags, or tenant IDs—into environment variables, the agent logic (topics, orchestration, Power Fx) references a logical name (e.g., var_API_BaseUrl). The solution exports/imports cleanly because the variable definition is included, but the value resolves contextually at runtime in each target environment. This eliminates the need to modify the agent definition (and create unmanaged layers) when promoting between environments.\nF. After an administrator updates a non-secret environment variable, republish any agents that use the variable so the change is effective at runtime.\nThis addresses the specific runtime caching behavior for non-secret environment variables in Copilot Studio. Unlike secret variables (which are retrieved securely at runtime via Key Vault integration and reflect immediately), non-secret variable values are cached/inlined into the agent's published configuration payload at the time of publishing. Therefore, updating the value in the Admin Portal does not affect running sessions or new sessions until the agent is explicitly republished. This republishing action regenerates the configuration artifact with the new value, ensuring runtime consistency.\nWhy Other Options Are Incorrect\nA. Replace environment variables with hard-coded values...\nHard-coding values violates fundamental ALM principles. It binds the solution to a specific environment's configuration (e.g., Dev URLs in Prod), prevents portable solutions, and introduces security risks (secrets in source control/solution zip). It forces manual code changes during promotion, breaking the \"build once, deploy many\" pipeline.\nB. Allow builders to change environment variable values directly in Copilot Studio during topic authoring... The Copilot Studio authoring canvas does not permit editing environment variable values. Builders can only consume (read) them. Values are set exclusively in the administrative layer (Solutions area). Allowing edits in the authoring UI would conflate design-time metadata with runtime configuration, breaking the ALM separation of concerns and causing solution import conflicts (unmanaged customizations on managed components).\nE. Assume that all environment variable updates take effect immediately at runtime for all variable types without republishing.\nThis is technically false for non-secret variables. While secret variables (stored in Azure Key Vault) are fetched at runtime and reflect immediately, non-secret values are snapshot into the agent's published package. Assuming immediate effect for all types leads to production incidents where configuration changes appear \"ignored\" because the agent was not republished.\nReferences\n1. Environment variables in Power Platform (ALM and solution awareness)\n2. Use environment variables in Copilot Studio (Read-only consumption, republishing requirements)", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Replace environment variables with hard-coded values inside the agent so that runtime behavior is always consistent across environments."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Allow builders to change environment variable values directly in Copilot Studio during topic authoring to speed deployment."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Use environment variables as read-only in Copilot Studio and change their values in the appropriate administrative authoring experience."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Store external reference values as environment variables so the agent solution can move between environments while keeping the agent definition the same."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Assume that all environment variable updates take effect immediately at runtime for all variable types without republishing."}, {"k": "F", "t": "After an administrator updates a non-secret environment variable, republish any agents that use the variable so the change is effective at runtime."}], "answer": ["C", "D", "F"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 132, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An organization uses separate development and production environments for agents in Copilot Studio.\nYou create an agent in the development environment.\nThe agent must be deployed to production by using an ALM process.\nYou must configure the solution strategy to meet the following requirements:\n• Developers continue modifying the agent in development.\n• The production deployment prevents direct modification.\n• The agent is exported from development and imported into production in a deployment-ready state.\nYou need to configure solutions to support deployment and lifecycle management of the agents.\nWhich solution type should you use for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Allow ongoing development.", "options": ["Default solution", "Managed solution", "Unmanaged solution", "Standalone agent outside a solution"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Prevent modification in production.", "options": ["Default solution", "Managed solution", "Temporary solution", "Unmanaged solution"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Export from development and import into production in deployment-ready state.", "options": ["Default solution", "Managed solution", "Unmanaged solution", "Standalone agent outside a solution"], "answer": 1}]}, {"id": 133, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is standardizing ALM for agents in Copilot Studio.\nThe team requires a custom solution that includes agents. The solution must support ALM scenarios, such as managing and transporting agent assets across environments and pipeline deployments.\nYou need to create an ALM-ready custom solution in Copilot Studio.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nOption B is correct because Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) in Copilot Studio is natively built upon the Microsoft Power Platform Solution framework. Creating a custom solution from the Solutions area (make.powerapps.com or Power Platform Admin Center) establishes a logical container that tracks all agent components—including topics, entities, connections, environment variables, and connection references—as solution-aware metadata. This enables versioning, dependency tracking, and the generation of managed/unmanaged solution packages required for pipeline-based deployments (e.g., Azure DevOps, GitHub Actions) using the Power Platform Build Tools or Power Platform CLI (pac solution). Without a custom solution, assets remain \"unmanaged\" or \"default solution\" components, which cannot be cleanly transported or version-controlled across environments.\nOption A is incorrect because publishing an agent to a channel (e.g., Teams, Web, Mobile) only configures the runtime endpoint and authentication settings for end-user access. Channels are deployment targets, not ALM containers; they do not encapsulate the agent's definition, logic, or dependencies, nor do they support versioning, patching, or pipeline-based transport between environments.\nOption C is incorrect as a creation step because it describes a downstream transport action (export/import) rather than the initial mechanism to make the agent ALM-ready. You cannot export a \"custom solution as a managed solution\" until that custom solution has first been created in the Solutions area and the agent components have been added to it. Performing an export/import without a pre-existing custom solution structure results in moving unmanaged layers, which violates ALM best practices for governed deployments.\nOption D is incorrect because exporting the agent directly (via the Copilot Studio authoring canvas \"Export\" feature) produces a .zip or .yaml file suitable only for Canvas app-style backup or manual migration. This process strips solution metadata, breaks connection references/environment variable links, and does not support managed layers, patching, or automated pipeline deployments. It treats the agent as a discrete artifact rather than a composable solution component.\nReferences\n1. ALM for agents in Copilot Studio - Microsoft Learn\n2. Create a solution - Power Apps | Microsoft Learn", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Publish the agent to a channel and use that channel configuration as the ALM container."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Create a new custom solution from the Solutions area."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Export the custom solution as a managed solution and import it into production."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Export the agent directly and then import it into another environment."}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 134, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You run the same fixed test set three times in Copilot Studio.\nDuring evaluation, you observe the following:\n• The same interaction fails in all three runs.\n• Score values range from 0.58 to 0.61.\n• The reasoning states that the response partially matches the expected answer.\n• The knowledge source that is used is internal documentation.\n• No tools are invoked.\nYou need to determine which conclusions are supported based on the evaluation results.\nWhich conclusions should you make? To answer, move the appropriate conclusions to the correct evaluation results. You may use each conclusion once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["A pattern of failure across repeated runs.", "The specific underlying cause of the failure.", "Use of a connected knowledge source during the interaction.", "The response partially matches the expected answer."], "slots": ["Evidence of a recurring issue.", "Information provided about response quality.", "Evaluation limitations."], "answer": ["A pattern of failure across repeated runs.", "The response partially matches the expected answer.", "Use of a connected knowledge source during the interaction."], "note": "Answer image shows 'Use of a connected knowledge source during the interaction.' for Evaluation limitations (as shown).", "prompt": "Determine which conclusions are supported by the evaluation results. Items may be used once, more than once, or not at all."}, {"id": 135, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team wants to automate solution deployment between environments. Deployments must follow a controlled, repeatable process.\nThe team needs to implement a pipeline-based deployment process that enforces the correct dependency order.\nYou need to implement a deployment pipeline and determine the correct sequence of actions.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "", "type": "order", "items": ["Assign environments to pipeline stages.", "Trigger a pipeline run.", "Enable pipelines for the tenant.", "Review deployment results."], "answer": ["Enable pipelines for the tenant.", "Assign environments to pipeline stages.", "Trigger a pipeline run.", "Review deployment results."]}, {"id": 136, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company needs an agent in Copilot Studio to complete tasks by interacting with a legacy desktop application that does not use APIs. The builder needs to validate that UI automation can run in the supported execution context and needs a way to inspect how the automation behaves during testing conversations.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Enable the agent to perform UI automation against the legacy application.\n• Inspect execution details for the automation during conversations.\nYou need to configure the agent based on the requirements.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Technical Justification\nThe correct actions are C and E because they directly address the native \"Computer Use\" capability in Copilot Studio, which is designed specifically for AI-driven UI automation against applications lacking APIs.\nE. Enable the Computer use capability: This is the foundational prerequisite. The Computer Use capability equips the agent with the ability to perceive screen content (via vision models) and execute mouse/keyboard actions against desktop or web applications. Without enabling this capability at the environment/agent level, the agent possesses no mechanism to drive UI automation against the legacy desktop application, rendering requirement one impossible to satisfy. Options A and B represent integration patterns (API connectors, Power Automate Desktop flows) which are distinct from the agent's native, autonomous UI interaction capability. C. Access the remote session within the testing pane to monitor the real-time execution of the agent's desktop interactions: When Computer Use is enabled, the Copilot Studio testing pane provides a \"Remote Session\" view (or similar live stream) that broadcasts the virtual machine session where the automation executes. This allows the maker to visually inspect the agent's decision-making, mouse movements, keystrokes, and application state changes in real-time during a test conversation, directly satisfying the requirement to inspect execution details during testing.\nWhy other options are incorrect:\nA. Configure a custom connector: Custom connectors wrap REST APIs (OpenAPI definitions). They cannot facilitate UI automation or interact with a legacy desktop application that explicitly \"does not use APIs.\" B. Add a Power Platform Desktop flow: While Power Automate Desktop (PAD) performs UI automation, invoking a PAD flow is an \"Action\" call from the agent, not the agent performing the automation natively via Computer Use. The scenario describes the agent completing tasks by interacting directly, implying the autonomous Computer Use loop, not orchestration of an external RPA script.\nD. Publish the agent: Publishing deploys the agent to channels (Teams, Web, etc.) for end-users. It does not enable capabilities (Computer Use must be enabled prior) nor does it provide a mechanism for the builder to inspect execution details during the development/testing phase; the testing pane is the designated tool for that.\nReferences\n1. Use Computer Use in Copilot Studio (Microsoft Learn)\n2. Test and debug your agent in Copilot Studio (Microsoft Learn)", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Configure a custom connector for the desktop app so the agent can automate the UI through authenticated calls."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Add a Power Platform Desktop flow to control the desktop app."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Access the remote session within the testing pane to monitor the real-time execution of the agent's desktop interactions."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Publish the agent so the automation runs in the supported environment automatically."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Enable the Computer use capability."}], "answer": ["C", "E"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 137, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A development team is reusing agents in Copilot Studio across development, test, and production environments.\nYou need to ensure the agents meet the following requirements:\n• Reusable components must support governed promotion across environments.\n• Environment-specific configuration must not be hard-coded.\n• Deployments must support automated, multi-stage approvals.\nYou need to configure the agents based on the business requirements.\nWhich configurations should you use for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Use an ALM pipeline.", "Export as a component collection.", "Use connection references and environment variables."], "slots": ["Share authored topics and prompts across environments.", "Move environment-specific configurations including connections and secrets.", "Automate multi-stage deployments with approvals."], "answer": ["Export as a component collection.", "Use connection references and environment variables.", "Use an ALM pipeline."]}, {"id": 138, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent calls a flow that intermittently produces unexpected results during execution.\nThe agent behaves inconsistently.\nYou need to investigate agent flow executions to diagnose the issue.\nWhich two actions should you use? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "§ Justification for Correct Options\nA. Inspect failed run details: This is the primary diagnostic action for investigating specific execution anomalies. In Power Automate, the \"Run History\" pane allows makers to open a specific failed flow run and inspect the Inputs/Outputs of every action, the Expression results, and the specific Error Message returned by the connector or runtime. Since the issue is \"intermittent\" and produces \"unexpected results\" (not just hard failures), inspecting the raw payload data of individual failed runs is essential to identify data variability, null reference exceptions, or logic branch misfires that cause the inconsistent agent behavior.\nE. Review flow run history: This provides the chronological index of all executions (succeeded, failed, cancelled) required to correlate the agent's inconsistent behavior with specific flow executions. It allows the developer to filter by status (Failed), date range, or trigger type to isolate the specific runs corresponding to the agent's malfunction timestamps. Without reviewing the run history first, there is no context to select which specific failed runs to inspect (Action A), making this the necessary entry point for the investigation.\nWhy Other Options Are Less Suitable\nB. Update the flow trigger definition: This is a remediation/configuration action, not a diagnostic one. Modifying the trigger schema (e.g., adding required parameters or changing types) alters the contract between the agent and the flow. Doing this without first diagnosing the root cause via run history/failed run details risks breaking existing functionality or masking the actual data issue causing the intermittency. C. Check the Error column in the Activity tab: The \"Activity\" tab in Copilot Studio (or the Power Apps portal) surfaces high-level session/conversation telemetry (e.g., \"Action execution failed\"), but it lacks the granular action-level input/output payloads and connector error codes available in the Power Automate Run History. It indicates that a failure occurred at the agent orchestration layer but does not provide the technical details inside the flow logic required to diagnose \"unexpected results.\"\nD. Review flow analytics summary: Analytics provides aggregated metrics (run counts, success rates, average duration, throughput) over a time window. It is useful for monitoring health trends and SLA compliance but lacks the drill-through capability to individual run payloads necessary to diagnose a specific, intermittent logic or data error affecting an agent conversation.\nF. Increase the connector timeout: This is a mitigation/configuration change for a specific error class (HTTP 408/Timeout). The scenario describes \"unexpected results\" and \"inconsistent behavior,\" which implies logic errors, data transformation issues, or transient connector 5xx errors—not necessarily timeout expirations. Increasing timeouts blindly delays failure detection without revealing the root cause of the incorrect output.\nReferences\n1. Monitor flow runs and view run history in Power Automate\n2. Debug a cloud flow in Power Automate", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Inspect failed run details."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Update the flow trigger definition."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Check the Error column in the Activity tab."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Review flow analytics summary."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Review flow run history."}, {"k": "F", "t": "Increase the connector timeout."}], "answer": ["A", "E"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 139, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You create a test set in Copilot Studio to evaluate an agent that answers policy questions and retrieves data from connected knowledge sources.\nYou run the evaluation and review the following result for one test case:\n• Expected response: Digital products are non-refundable after download.\n• Actual response: Digital products cannot be refunded once downloaded.\n• Result: Pass\n• Score: 0.92\n• Reasoning: The actual response semantically matches the expected response.\n• Knowledge sources used: RefundPolicy_KB\n• Tools invoked: None\nYou need to determine whether additional corrective action is required based on the evaluation results.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "The evaluation result indicates a Pass status with a high semantic similarity score (0.92), and the reasoning explicitly confirms the actual response semantically matches the expected response. In Copilot Studio, generative answers are validated using semantic matching, not exact string comparison; therefore, a score below 1.00 is expected and acceptable for natural language variations.\nAccept the result because semantic matching is sufficient for a passing evaluation. The test case has passed based on the defined evaluation criteria. The phrasing difference (\"non-refundable after download\" vs. \"cannot be refunded once downloaded\") conveys identical meaning, which is the goal of semantic evaluation. Requiring an exact match would create brittle tests unsuitable for LLM outputs.\nConfirm that the connected knowledge source is functioning as expected. The result shows the response was grounded in RefundPolicy_KB with no tools invoked. Best practice requires verifying the knowledge source is current, accessible, and returning accurate content to ensure the semantic match reflects factual correctness rather than a hallucination aligned with the expected answer.\nWhy other options are incorrect:\nA: The result explicitly states \"Tools invoked: None,\" making external tool configuration investigation irrelevant for this specific test case.\nB: Rerunning an evaluation because a semantic score is not 1.00 demonstrates a misunderstanding of probabilistic LLM outputs; 0.92 represents a high-confidence pass.\nE: Modifying the test case to enforce exact matching undermines the semantic evaluation framework and would cause false failures on valid paraphrases.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/evaluate-agent\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/test-agent", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Investigate external tool configuration for this test case."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Rerun the evaluation because the score is below 1.00."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Accept the result because semantic matching is sufficient for a passing evaluation."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Confirm that the connected knowledge source is functioning as expected."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Modify the test case because the response is not an exact match."}], "answer": ["C", "D"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 140, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team deploys Copilot Studio solutions by using Power Platform Pipelines.\nYou must configure a deployment strategy that meets the following requirements:\n• Ensure that the solutions deploy in dev, test, and production environments in order.\n• Identify missing connection references or environment variables before the deployment process begins.\n• Manage all pipeline stages and security from a single, unified location for all linked environments.\nYou need to configure the pipeline to meet the requirements.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Solutions deploy in dev, test and production environments in order.", "options": ["Sequential stage progression", "Direct target environment selection", "Environment-specific deployment packages"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Identify missing connection references or environment variables before deployment.", "options": ["Deployment input validation", "Managed solution deployment", "Deployment configuration override"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Manage pipeline stages and security from a single, unified location for linked environments.", "options": ["Environment-level customization", "Centralized pipeline configuration", "Builder-defined deployment settings"], "answer": 0, "note": "Answer image circles 'Environment-level customization' (index 0), though 'Centralized pipeline configuration' seems more logical; kept as circled."}]}, {"id": 141, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is preparing a test set to evaluate an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding access to additional users. The test set must support consistent evaluation across repeated runs and focus on the agent's most important behaviors.\nYou need to create a test set that enables repeatable and meaningful evaluation.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "", "type": "order", "items": ["Define the expected outcomes and success criteria for each interaction.", "Author representative user prompts that trigger the identified intents.", "Identify the high-value conversational intents the agent must handle.", "Baseline the test set to ensure deterministic evaluation across repeated runs."], "answer": ["Identify the high-value conversational intents the agent must handle.", "Author representative user prompts that trigger the identified intents.", "Define the expected outcomes and success criteria for each interaction.", "Baseline the test set to ensure deterministic evaluation across repeated runs."]}, {"id": 142, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is preparing to assess an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding access to additional users.\nThe team requires an evaluation that provides controlled, repeatable, and consistently measured test runs. The evaluation must:\n• Be triggered directly by the team to control when testing occurs.\n• Determine success based on clearly established acceptance criteria.\n• Rely on a predefined source that ensures consistency across repeated runs.\nYou need to configure the evaluation to meet the requirements.\nWhat should you configure? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct evaluation requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Execute the evaluation on demand.", "Expand rollout to additional channels.", "Enable analytics to monitor agent usage trends.", "Compare responses against defined expectations.", "Use a curated prompt list as the interaction source."], "slots": ["Triggered directly by the team to control when testing occurs.", "Determine success based on clearly established acceptance criteria.", "Rely on a predefined source that ensures consistency across repeated runs."], "answer": ["Execute the evaluation on demand.", "Compare responses against defined expectations.", "Use a curated prompt list as the interaction source."], "prompt": "Configure the evaluation to provide controlled, repeatable, and consistently measured test runs. Items may be used once, more than once, or not at all."}, {"id": 143, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You run multiple evaluation tests for an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding user access.\nYou observe the following about the evaluation results:\n• Each evaluation case is reported as meeting or missing the expected response.\n• Some evaluation cases fail repeatedly across several runs.\n• Each run displays the expected response and the response generated by the agent.\nYou need to interpret what the evaluation results reveal.\nWhat should you conclude based on each interpretation? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Outcome of individual evaluation cases", "options": ["Pass or fail status", "Failure configuration", "Production readiness"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Behavior observed across repeated runs", "options": ["Prompt revisions", "Consistent outcomes", "Intermittent outcomes"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Details presented in evaluation output", "options": ["Deployment approval", "Root cause of failures", "Expected and generated responses"], "answer": 2}]}, {"id": 144, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent in Copilot Studio in production and requires centralized monitoring for operational visibility and troubleshooting.\nThe team decides to use Application Insights as the monitoring solution.\nAn agent activity is not included in the monitoring data.\nYou need to enable monitoring the agent by using Application Insights.\nWhich four actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "", "type": "order", "items": ["Enable monitoring for the agent.", "Publish the agent so the monitoring configuration takes effect.", "Configure which monitoring destination the agent uses.", "Create or identify the Application Insights destination for telemetry.", "Configure the agent feature that generates grounded answers.", "Configure the agent feature that selects a model from a catalog."], "answer": ["Create or identify the Application Insights destination for telemetry.", "Configure which monitoring destination the agent uses.", "Enable monitoring for the agent.", "Publish the agent so the monitoring configuration takes effect."]}, {"id": 145, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A team is implementing ALM for agents in Copilot Studio across multiple environments.\nThe team requires a consistent way to manage and transport agent assets.\nYou need to recommend a solution that supports creation and use of ALM scenarios for agents in Copilot Studio.\nSolution: To export, import, and manage agents between environments, you need to create and use a custom solution.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nThe solution meets the goal because Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) in Copilot Studio is fundamentally built upon the Microsoft Dataverse Solution framework. A custom solution (unmanaged during development, managed for production deployment) is the mandatory container for transporting agent definitions, components (topics, entities, actions, connections), and environment-specific configuration between environments (e.g., Development → Test → Production).\nComponent Tracking: Only components added to a solution are tracked for versioning, dependency checking, and patch/upgrade management. The default solution cannot be exported for transport to other environments.\nEnvironment Variables & Connection References: Custom solutions allow the inclusion of Environment Variables and Connection References, enabling parameterization of endpoints and credentials during import into target environments without manual reconfiguration.\nPipeline Integration: Custom solutions generate the .zip artifacts required for automation via Power Platform Build Tools (Azure DevOps/GitHub Actions) and the pac solution CLI commands, enabling CI/CD pipelines.\nOption B (\"No\") is incorrect because relying on the Default Solution or manual export/import of Canvas apps/flows outside of a custom solution structure does not support governed ALM. The Default Solution cannot be exported for upstream deployment, lacks versioning capabilities for agent revisions, and prevents the use of Solution Layers for patching. Therefore, creating and using a custom solution is the prescribed architectural pattern for Copilot Studio ALM.\nReferences\n1. Application lifecycle management (ALM) for agents in Copilot Studio\n2. Use solutions for application lifecycle management (ALM) in Power Platform", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 146, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A team is implementing ALM for agents in Copilot Studio across multiple environments.\nThe team requires a consistent way to manage and transport agent assets.\nYou need to recommend a solution that supports creation and use of ALM scenarios for agents in Copilot Studio.\nSolution: Publishing an agent to a channel is the required ALM container for transporting agents between environments.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "No, the solution does not meet the goal.\nIn Copilot Studio, the fundamental unit for Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) and the transport of agent assets between environments is the Solution (specifically, a Dataverse Solution). Solutions act as the container for all component metadata—including topics, entities, variables, Power Automate flows, connection references, and environment variables—enabling version-controlled export from a source environment (e.g., Development) and import into target environments (e.g., Test, Production).\nPublishing an agent to a channel is strictly a runtime deployment action that makes a specific version of the agent available to end-users on a designated interface (such as Microsoft Teams, a custom website, or mobile app). It does not package the underlying component definitions, dependencies, or logic into a portable artifact. Consequently, publishing cannot be used to migrate an agent's design-time artifacts across environment boundaries; it only activates the agent within the current environment. Proper ALM requires adding the agent to a Solution, exporting that Solution, importing it into the target environment, and subsequently publishing it to the desired channels there.\nReferences\n§ ALM for agents in Copilot Studio - Microsoft Learn\n§ Solution concepts in Power Platform - Microsoft Learn", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 147, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A team is implementing ALM for agents in Copilot Studio across multiple environments.\nThe team requires a consistent way to manage and transport agent assets.\nYou need to recommend a solution that supports creation and use of ALM scenarios for agents in Copilot Studio.\nSolution: Builders can change environment variable values directly in Copilot Studio to complete ALM configuration without administrative tools.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "The solution does not meet the goal.\nALM Requires Solution-Based Transport: Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) in Copilot Studio is built on the Power Platform Solution framework. Assets (agents, flows, connections, environment variables) must be packaged into a Solution file (.zip) and transported between environments (Dev → Test → Prod) using administrative/DevOps tools such as Power Platform CLI (pac), Power Platform Build Tools in Azure DevOps, GitHub Actions, or the Power Platform Admin Center.\nEnvironment Variables are Solution Components: Environment variables are components inside a Solution.\nWhile current values (runtime values) can be set post-import in a target environment via the Maker Portal or Admin Center, the definitions and default values are version-controlled and deployed exclusively via the Solution package.\nMisalignment of Roles and Tools: The proposed approach suggests builders manually configure ALM by editing values directly in the Copilot Studio authoring canvas. This bypasses source control, automated pipelines, and the repeatable, auditable deployment process required for enterprise ALM. It treats configuration as an ad-hoc runtime activity rather than a governed deployment artifact.\nLack of Governance and Consistency: Manual value changes in the studio UI do not support versioning, rollback, or automated validation across environments, which are core tenets of the ALM scenarios the question requires.\nReferences\nApplication lifecycle management (ALM) for agents in Microsoft Copilot Studio\n§ Environment variables in solutions", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 148, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent flow that calls a connector action. The flow sends an administrative notification if the action fails or times out.\nAdministrators require the following configuration to help troubleshoot and manage failures:\n• Handle failure and timeout outcomes explicitly.\n• Continue execution after a failure is handled.\n• Avoid retrying the failed action automatically.\nYou need to configure the agent flow so that failures are handled in a controlled and predictable way.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Technical Justification\nCorrect Selections: B and D\nB. Add a scope action to group related actions.\nA Scope acts as a logical container that groups the connector action and any other dependent steps. This is essential for implementing structured error handling (try/catch pattern) because it allows the flow to evaluate the aggregate outcome of the grouped actions (Success, Failed, Aborted, Skipped) via the result('ScopeName') function. Without a Scope, handling failure for a specific block of logic explicitly—while allowing the parent flow to continue—is not possible natively.\nD. Configure the run after conditions for the notification action.\nRun After settings (configured on the notification action) explicitly define the trigger conditions for execution based on the preceding Scope's status. Setting this to has failed or has timed out ensures the administrative notification fires only on those specific terminal states. Crucially, because the notification action is placed after the Scope (at the same level) and configured with these conditions, the flow engine marks the Scope as \"handled,\" allowing the parent flow execution to Continue linearly without propagating the failure upward or triggering the default retry policy on the failed connector action.\nWhy Other Options Are Incorrect\nA. Increase the timeout on the connector action.\nThis modifies the threshold for a timeout error but does not provide a mechanism to handle the failure, continue execution, or suppress retries. It merely delays the inevitable failure state and contradicts the requirement for explicit handling of timeout outcomes.\nC. Configure concurrency control and apply to each action.\nConcurrency control manages the degree of parallelism for Apply to each loops or trigger instances. It is irrelevant to handling the success/failure/timeout outcome of a single connector action instance or controlling flow continuation logic after a failure.\nReferences\n1. Handle errors and exceptions in Power Automate flows (Official Microsoft Learn)\n2. Configure run after settings for actions in Power Automate (Official Microsoft Learn)", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Increase the timeout on the connector action."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Add a scope action to group related actions."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Configure concurrency control and apply to each action."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Configure the run after conditions for the notification action."}], "answer": ["B", "D"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 149, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team has an existing agent in Copilot Studio and must move it to a different target location without rebuilding the agent. Some builders use Copilot Studio in the web app, while others use Copilot Studio in Microsoft Teams.\nYou need a supported approach to move the existing agent to the target location using ALM practices. The solution must avoid rebuilding the agent.\nYou need to move the existing agent to the target location using a supported method.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Justification for Correct Answer: C\nOption C is the correct approach because Copilot Studio agents authored and managed within the Microsoft Teams client (Dataverse for Teams environments) utilize a distinct packaging and deployment lifecycle compared to agents in production Dataverse environments accessed via the web app. For Teams-managed agents, the supported ALM mechanism is the native Export/Import package functionality available directly within the Copilot Studio app in Teams (or via the pac copilot CLI commands targeting the Teams environment). This process packages the agent definition, topics, entities, and associated Power Automate flows into a .zip package (or .msapp format depending on version) that can be imported into a target Team/Dataverse for Teams environment. This preserves the agent's logic and configuration without requiring reconstruction, satisfying the requirement to avoid rebuilding. Since the scenario explicitly states builders use Copilot Studio in Teams, this is the prescribed path for that specific runtime context.\nWhy Other Options Are Incorrect:\nA. Remove agent components directly from the solution so that export and import will succeed.\nManipulating solution components manually by removing dependencies (such as connection references, environment variables, or sub-components) to force an export is an unsupported anti-pattern. It breaks the managed layer integrity, removes necessary metadata for runtime resolution in the target, and violates ALM principles where the Solution is the atomic unit of transport.\nB. Configure pipeline settings first and rely on pipelines to discover and package the agent automatically without adding it to a solution.\nPower Platform Pipelines (and the underlying Solution framework) require all components, including Copilot Studio agents, to be explicitly added to a Solution in the source environment. Pipelines do not perform automatic discovery of unmanaged/custom components residing outside of a Solution container. Agents built in the web app (production environments) must be solution-aware to be transported via pipelines.\nD. Duplicate the agent in the source and manually recreate the changes in the target to avoid solution dependencies.\nThis constitutes manual reconstruction (\"rebuilding\"), which the prompt explicitly forbids. It introduces configuration drift, human error, lacks version control history, does not support rollback, and fundamentally contradicts Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) practices which mandate automated, repeatable deployment via packaging.\nReferences\n1. ALM for Copilot Studio in Teams (Export/Import):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/alm-teams-export-import\n2. Solution Management for Copilot Studio Agents (Web App/Production ALM):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/alm-solutions-overview", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Remove agent components directly from the solution so that export and import will succeed."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Configure pipeline settings first and rely on pipelines to discover and package the agent automatically without adding it to a solution."}, {"k": "C", "t": "If the agent is built and managed in Copilot Studio in Teams, export the agent from the source team and import it into the target team using the Teams-based export/import approach."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Duplicate the agent in the source and manually recreate the changes in the target to avoid solution dependencies."}], "answer": ["C"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 150, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is preparing to evaluate an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding access to additional users. The team must select an evaluation method that meets the following requirements:\n• Use a fixed set of prepared interactions.\n• Determine responses against a predefined baseline.\n• Support consistent comparison across repeated test runs.\nYou need to choose an evaluation method that meets the evaluation requirements.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "Correct Option: B – Apply a standardized evaluation matrix\nA standardized evaluation matrix is the only method that satisfies all three requirements simultaneously. It operates by executing a fixed, curated dataset of test utterances (prepared interactions) against the agent, comparing the actual responses to predefined expected outcomes (baseline), and producing deterministic scores that enable regression testing and consistent comparison across iterative development cycles. This approach is intrinsic to the Copilot Studio \"Test\" and \"Evaluation\" tooling, which supports automated batch testing against golden datasets to validate topic triggering, entity extraction, and generative answers accuracy before deployment.\nWhy other options are less suitable:\nA. Manually review conversation transcripts: This is a qualitative, ad-hoc process reliant on human judgment. It lacks a fixed input set, cannot enforce a predefined baseline programmatically, and introduces observer bias, making repeated runs non-comparable and statistically unreliable for regression validation.\nC. Monitor the Analytics dashboard for session abandonment rates: This measures passive telemetry from live production traffic, not controlled test executions. It uses variable, uncontrolled user inputs rather than a fixed interaction set, provides no mechanism for response-level baseline comparison, and reflects user behavior friction rather than functional correctness against specifications.\nD. Launch a limited pilot with live users: This constitutes User Acceptance Testing (UAT) or A/B testing, which introduces uncontrolled variables (user intent variance, environmental factors). It utilizes satisfaction (CSAT) and containment—aggregate business KPIs—rather than a technical baseline for functional verification, preventing the isolated, repeatable comparison of specific agent logic required for pre-release certification.\nReferences\n1. Evaluate your copilot's performance - Microsoft Copilot Studio Documentation\n2. Test your copilot - Microsoft Copilot Studio Documentation", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Manually review conversation transcripts to identify successful outcomes."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Apply a standardized evaluation matrix."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Monitor the Analytics dashboard for session abandonment rates."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Launch a limited pilot and evaluate with customer satisfaction score and containment metrics from live users."}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 151, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A solution architect is implementing a governance process for an agent developed in Copilot Studio. Before the agent is moved to production, the organization requires a standardized validation process to ensure that updates to the underlying knowledge base do not cause regressions in response accuracy.\nThe validation process must satisfy the following technical criteria:\n• Consistency: Execute a fixed library of test cases containing specific user prompts.\n• Grounding: Measure the quality of the generated output against a verified “golden” response.\n• Comparison: Provide a performance delta between the current version and previous test runs.\nYou need to choose an evaluation method that meets the requirements.\nWhat should you use?", "expl": "Technical Justification\nCorrect Option: D — Use a predefined test set and compare results against expected outcomes across runs.\nThis option directly satisfies all three technical criteria mandated by the governance process. Copilot Studio Test Sets allow architects to define a fixed library of test cases (Consistency) containing specific user prompts and verified \"golden\" responses (Grounding). When a test set is executed against a specific agent version, the platform performs an automated evaluation—often leveraging LLM-assisted scoring—to measure semantic similarity between the generated output and the expected outcome. Critically, the Test Sets feature retains historical run data, enabling a Comparison of performance deltas (e.g., pass rates, accuracy scores) between the current version and previous test runs, which is essential for regression detection before production deployment.\nWhy Option A is Incorrect\nThe \"Test your agent\" pane is an ad-hoc, manual debugging tool designed for iterative development. It lacks the ability to execute a fixed, version-controlled library of test cases automatically, cannot systematically compare outputs against golden responses at scale, and provides no mechanism for storing historical run data to calculate performance deltas across versions. It fails the Consistency, Grounding (automated), and Comparison criteria.\nWhy Option B is Incorrect\nChannel usage metrics (e.g., session counts, escalation rates, user satisfaction scores) are post-deployment telemetry indicators. They measure business outcomes and user behavior, not the functional correctness of specific knowledge base responses against a verified baseline. They cannot execute predefined prompts, compare against golden answers, or provide a pre-production regression delta. They fail all three technical criteria for pre-production validation.\nWhy Option C is Incorrect\nConversation Transcripts and keyword search are reactive, unstructured analysis methods suitable for qualitative auditing. They require the agent to be live (post-publish), rely on sampling rather than exhaustive execution of a fixed test library, and cannot mathematically measure semantic grounding against a golden response or calculate a quantitative performance delta between versions. They fail Consistency (fixed library execution), Grounding (automated quality measurement), and Comparison (delta tracking).\nReferences\n1. Test your agent with test sets in Microsoft Copilot Studio\n2. Evaluate and improve your agent's performance", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Use the Test your agent pane to manually enter questions and verify that the correct topic or action triggers for each turn."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Publish the agent and use channel usage metrics as the primary evaluation method."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Enable Conversation Transcripts in Analytics and perform a keyword search to identify if the agent uses the correct knowledge sources."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Use a predefined test set and compare results against expected outcomes across runs."}], "answer": ["D"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 152, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is preparing to manage agents in Copilot Studio by using ALM from Power Platform.\nA solution must be created before agents and configuration of assets can be managed through ALM.\nYou need to create a solution that can contain agents and related assets.\nWhich three actions should you perform in order? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "", "type": "order", "items": ["Review the solution details page.", "Define the solution name and publisher.", "Save the solution container.", "Open the Solutions area in Copilot Studio."], "answer": ["Open the Solutions area in Copilot Studio.", "Define the solution name and publisher.", "Save the solution container."], "prompt": "Select the three actions, in order, to create a solution that can contain agents and related assets."}, {"id": 153, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is preparing to assess an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding access to additional users.\nThe team requires an evaluation that provides controlled, repeatable, and consistently measured test runs. The evaluation must:\n• Be triggered directly by the team to control when testing occurs.\n• Determine success based on clearly established acceptance criteria.\n• Rely on a predefined set of inputs that ensures consistency across repeated runs.\nYou need to configure the evaluation to meet the requirements\nWhat should you configure? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct evaluation requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "", "type": "match", "items": ["Execute the evaluation on demand.", "Schedule the evaluation to run automatically at set intervals.", "Use live user conversations as the interaction source.", "Compare responses against defined expectations.", "Use a curated prompt list as the interaction source."], "slots": ["Triggered directly by the team to control when testing occurs.", "Determine success based on clearly established acceptance criteria.", "Rely on a predefined source that ensures consistency across repeated runs."], "answer": ["Execute the evaluation on demand.", "Compare responses against defined expectations.", "Use a curated prompt list as the interaction source."]}, {"id": 154, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team has deployed an agent in Copilot Studio and needs to evaluate whether the agent is meeting its intended goals before expanding usage to a broader audience.\nThe team must evaluate agent performance using a structured and repeatable approach that produces actionable insights.\nYou need to select and apply an appropriate evaluation method by performing the required actions in the correct order.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "", "type": "order", "items": ["Collect agent interaction data and evaluation signals.", "Define the success criteria and evaluation objectives.", "Select the evaluation method and metrics aligned to the criteria.", "Review evaluation results and identify improvement opportunities."], "answer": ["Define the success criteria and evaluation objectives.", "Select the evaluation method and metrics aligned to the criteria.", "Collect agent interaction data and evaluation signals.", "Review evaluation results and identify improvement opportunities."], "prompt": "Select and apply an appropriate evaluation method for a Copilot Studio agent in the correct order."}, {"id": 155, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A company is configuring an agent in Copilot Studio that uses a generative answers node inside multiple topics. The company requires responses to meet the following requirements:\n• Must use an executive summary format.\n• Must apply only within a specific topic.\n• Must continue using configured knowledge sources.\n• Must comply with connector security controls.\nYou need to configure a custom prompt to meet the formatting requirements.\nSolution: Configure the custom prompt tool at the topic level so that other topics are not affected.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "§ A. Yes\nTopic Isolation: One of the strict requirements is that the executive summary format must apply only within a specific topic. Configuring the custom prompt tool locally at the individual topic level ensures that the unique formatting constraints are sandboxed, meaning generative answer nodes in other topics remain completely unaffected.\nCompliance & Knowledge: Applying the prompt tool at the topic level seamlessly overrides formatting behavior while continuing to inherit and honor the globally configured knowledge sources and the environment's backend data connector security controls.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 156, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is developing an agent in Copilot Studio and wants to evaluate its behavior before expanding access. The team needs a repeatable, consistent method to test the agent using predefined interactions.\nYou need to ensure that agent responses can be evaluated consistently across multiple test runs.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "C. Define test sets of prompts and expectations.\nMicrosoft Copilot Studio includes an native Agent Evaluation feature built specifically for this purpose. This capability allows development teams to create or import predefined test sets containing specific user prompts alongside their expected outcomes or validation parameters.\nWhen an evaluation is executed, the platform feeds these test cases systematically to the agent, tracks how it responds, and applies automated grading methods (such as checking for exact matches, semantic similarity, or intent accuracy). This provides an objective, repeatable quality baseline before expanding copilot access to broader user groups.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nA & B: Telemetry, historical analytics, and production logs monitor live or past user sessions qualitatively and dynamically. They cannot act as a structured, deterministic test script to replay identical interactions repeatedly.\nD: Shifting to a purely qualitative approach relies on manual, ad-hoc ad-hoc tester feedback, which reduces consistency rather than ensuring repeatable evaluation metrics across separate builds.\nE: Deploying the copilot to more channels does not solve the testing problem; it simply exposes an untested agent to real users prematurely.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Review evaluation scores from previous production sessions."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Enable telemetry and analytics to monitor intent matches, fallback usage, and response frequency."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Define test sets of prompts and expectations."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Change the evaluation method to a qualitative approach."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Publish the agent to additional channels for broader usage."}], "answer": ["C"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 157, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company builds agents in Copilot Studio in a development environment.\nThe agents are packaged inside a solution and deployed by using Power Platform Pipelines to test and production environments.\nThe company has the following requirements that must be met:\n• Production deployments are limited to a defined set of users who are responsible for that stage.\n• The same solution is reused across all environments without editing its components.\n• The production environment always receives a locked-down version of the solution.\nYou need to configure the pipeline and solution behavior to meet the requirements.\nWhat should you configure? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "In Power Platform Pipelines, permissions can be defined at individual pipeline tiers. By choosing to Configure stage access for the production stage, administrators can use role-based security assignments to ensure only authorized deployment managers or release engineers can push builds into production.\nTo move an identical solution package across different landscapes (such as QA and Production) without editing components directly, you must externalize environment-specific parameters. You Use environment variables in the solution to handle shifting credentials, base URLs, or connection parameters seamlessly during import.\nBest practice dictates that a production environment must remain immutable to accidental edits or citizen- developer interference. Choosing to Deploy the solution as managed explicitly locks down the copilot elements, ensuring that components cannot be directly customized or deleted in the target system.", "type": "match", "items": ["Deploy the solution as managed.", "Deploy the solution as unmanaged.", "Use environment variables in the solution.", "Configure stage access for the production stage."], "slots": ["Limit users who can deploy to production.", "Reuse the same solution across environments without modifying its components.", "Ensure production receives a locked-down version of the solution."], "answer": ["Configure stage access for the production stage.", "Use environment variables in the solution.", "Deploy the solution as managed."], "reuse": true}, {"id": 158, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You run multiple evaluation tests for an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding user access.\nThe tests provide the following results:\n• Each evaluation case is reported as meeting or missing the expected response.\n• Some evaluation cases fail repeatedly across several runs.\n• Each run displays the expected response and the response generated by the agent.\nYou need to provide a conclusion based on the evaluation results.\nWhich conclusions should you make? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "When an evaluation runs against a test dataset, each test case checks whether the agent's output successfully matches or fails the set expectations. This determines the overall binary Pass or fail status of that test case.\nWhen specific generative test cases consistently fail across separate runs, it indicates a deterministic behavior issue (such as bad grounding, loose boundaries, or off-topic tracking). To remediate these persistent failures, the development team must implement Prompt revisions to tighten the system-level safety instructions or custom guardrails.\nThe user interface metrics screen side-by-side presents the target expected baseline next to what the copilot dynamically spit out at runtime. This provides explicit visibility into the Expected and generated responses to allow administrators to evaluate accuracy gaps.", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Evaluation case meeting or missing expected response.", "options": ["Pass or fail status", "Failure configuration", "Production readiness"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Some evaluation cases fail repeatedly.", "options": ["Prompt revisions", "Consistent outcomes", "Intermittent outcomes"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Each run displays expected response and agent response.", "options": ["Deployment approval", "Root cause of failures", "Expected and generated responses"], "answer": 2}], "note": "Answer for row 2 taken from the answer image (Prompt revisions highlighted)."}, {"id": 159, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent in Copilot Studio is evaluated using a fixed test set and an automated evaluation method.\nAfter running the evaluation, the team observes that the same three interactions fail in every run, while all other interactions pass consistently.\nYou need to use the evaluation results to determine an accurate conclusion.\nWhat should you conclude from the evaluation results?", "expl": "C. The outcomes isolate which portions of the agent's functional scope require remediation.\nBecause the evaluation uses a fixed test set and the failures are completely deterministic (the same three interactions fail every single time while everything else passes consistently), the test has successfully isolated specific, localized gaps in the agent's knowledge or logic.\nThis indicates that the broader system architecture and general prompt design are solid. Instead of starting over or blaming the underlying LLM, the development team can precisely target and remediate the exact functional scopes (such as a missing knowledge source, a poorly phrased topic trigger, or an ungrounded system prompt instruction) causing those three specific failures.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "The agent's overall design is flawed and requires a full redesign."}, {"k": "B", "t": "The failing interactions are caused by an issue in the underlying language model."}, {"k": "C", "t": "The outcomes isolate which portions of the agent's functional scope require remediation."}, {"k": "D", "t": "The agent should not be deployed."}], "answer": ["C"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 160, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team has an existing agent in Copilot Studio and must move it to a different target location without rebuilding the agent. Some builders use Copilot Studio in the web app, while others use Copilot Studio in Microsoft Teams.\nYou need a supported approach to move the existing agent to the target location using ALM practices. The solution must avoid rebuilding the agent.\nYou need to move the existing agent to the target location using a supported method.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "C. Create a custom solution, add the existing agent to the solution, export the solution with the agent, and import the solution into the target environment.\nTo transport a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent between environments without rebuilding or duplicating its components, you must leverage standard Power Platform Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) practices.\nAgents created in both the web app and Microsoft Teams are fully solution-aware components. Packing the existing agent along with its subcomponents (such as topics, entities, and environment variables) into a custom solution container allows you to export it as a clean zip package from your source environment and import it directly into your target destination environment.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Remove agent components directly from the solution so that export and import will succeed."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Duplicate the agent in the source and manually recreate the changes in the target to avoid solution dependencies."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Create a custom solution, add the existing agent to the solution, export the solution with the agent, and import the solution into the target environment."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Publish the agent to a channel and use the channel configuration as the transport mechanism to the target environment."}], "answer": ["C"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 161, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You are setting up an evaluation for an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding access to more users.\nYou must configure the evaluation so that the agent is tested by using prepared interactions rather than impromptu conversations.\nThe evaluation should provide the following functionality for users:\nAn administrator updates a sensitive configuration value. The administrator requires the change to take effect at runtime without republishing the agent.\nA builder needs to confirm a configuration value but must not be able to modify it from Copilot Studio. An administrator updates a non-secret configuration value and requires the updated value to be used by the published agent at runtime.\nYou need to create a test set-based evaluation that supports consistent test runs for each scenario.\nWhat should you configure for each scenario? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Sensitive configurations (like API keys or tokens) are securely backed by Azure Key Vault using a Secret environment variable. Since environment variables are referenced dynamically, updating the value updates the bot's runtime behavior immediately without needing a full republish.\nWhen a solution is imported into a target environment (like UAT or Production) as Managed, the assets become read-only to prevent citizen developers from modifying them from the canvas. You can confirm or view a Managed environment variable value, but you cannot edit its core definition inside Copilot Studio. For standard non-sensitive text configurations (like a base URL or a contact email), a String environment variable is used. Any live update made to this value will be dynamically pulled by the published agent at runtime.", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Update a sensitive configuration value and the change takes effect at runtime without republishing the agent.", "options": ["Only in production", "Only after publishing", "Secret environment variable"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Confirm a configuration value but not modify it from Copilot Studio.", "options": ["Publishing settings", "Conversation volume", "Managed environment variable"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Update a non-secret configuration value and the updated value needs to be used by the published agent at runtime.", "options": ["String environment variable", "Live chat history", "Channel rollout status"], "answer": 0}]}, {"id": 162, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is preparing to evaluate an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding access to additional users. The team must select an evaluation method that meets the following requirements:\n• Use a fixed set of prepared interactions.\n• Determine responses against a predefined baseline.\n• Support consistent comparison across repeated test runs.\nYou need to determine the evaluation method that satisfies the evaluation requirements.\nWhich evaluation method should you select for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate option in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Use a curated prompt list. (Stops random variables from creeping into the test).\nEstablishing a Baseline → Compare against predefined expected outcomes. (Provides the ground truth comparison target).\nSupport Repeatable Comparisons → Use a consistent scoring evaluation matrix. (Ensures mathematical and qualitative uniformity across separate developer test runs).", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Use a fixed set of prepared interactions.", "options": ["Use a curated prompt list.", "Use only live conversations.", "Expand access to increase traffic."], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Establish a baseline for determining responses.", "options": ["Confirm publishing status as success.", "Rely on general impressions of quality.", "Compare against predefined expected outcomes."], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Support repeatable comparisons across runs.", "options": ["Collect user feedback after rollout.", "Use a consistent scoring evaluation matrix.", "Change channel settings before publishing."], "answer": 1}]}, {"id": 163, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A solution containing an agent in Copilot Studio is moved across environments. The agent relies on configuration values that are different between environments and are managed as environment variables.\nYou must ensure the following:\n• Configuration values are managed through environment variables that supports ALM.\n• Runtime behavior reflects updates to environment variable values.\nYou need to implement environment-variable usage that supports ALM and ensures correct runtime behavior.\nWhich three actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "C. Use environment variables as read-only in Copilot Studio and change their values in the appropriate administrative authoring experience.\nD. Store external reference values as environment variables so the agent solution can move between environments while keeping the agent definition the same.\nF. After an administrator updates a non-secret environment variable, republish any agents that use the variable so the change is effective at runtime.\nAction C (Platform Boundary): Environment variables are natively read-only within the Microsoft Copilot Studio authoring canvas. Their actual configuration and value management must occur externally inside the Power Apps solution explorer or admin centers.\nAction D (ALM Enablement): External reference values (such as API endpoints, base URLs, or tenant specific configurations) change when transitioning from a Development landscape into Test or Production. Moving these values into environment variables prevents hardcoding, allowing the parent solution wrapper to transport safely without modifying core topic code definitions.\nAction F (Runtime Behavior Integration): In Copilot Studio, standard non-secret environment variable parameters are captured into the agent metadata layer during the compilation phase. If an administrator makes changes to these variables, the agent must be republished for those newly updated values to take effect at production runtime.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Replace environment variables with hard-coded values inside the agent so that runtime behavior is always consistent across environments."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Allow builders to change environment variable values directly in Copilot Studio during topic authoring to speed deployment."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Use environment variables as read-only in Copilot Studio and change their values in the appropriate administrative authoring experience."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Store external reference values as environment variables so the agent solution can move between environments while keeping the agent definition the same."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Assume that all environment variable updates take effect immediately at runtime for all variable types without republishing."}, {"k": "F", "t": "After an administrator updates a non-secret environment variable, republish any agents that use the variable so the change is effective at runtime."}], "answer": ["C", "D", "F"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 164, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An organization uses separate development and production environments for agents in Copilot Studio.\nYou create an agent in the development environment.\nThe agent must be deployed to production by using an ALM process.\nYou must configure the solution strategy to meet the following requirements:\n• Developers continue modifying the agent in development.\n• The production deployment prevents direct modification.\n• The agent is exported from development and imported into production in a deployment-ready state.\nYou need to configure solutions to support deployment and lifecycle management of the agents.\nWhich solution type should you use for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Allow ongoing development ➔ Unmanaged solution\nReasoning: Unmanaged solutions are designated for active authoring, testing, and modification. They act as the primary containers for developers or makers to create new elements or configure existing configurations. There are no locking constraints placed on components within an unmanaged solution in a development sandbox environment.\n2. Prevent modification in production ➔ Managed solution\nReasoning: When a solution is installed as a managed solution in an environment (such as a production or user acceptance testing environment), it locks the underlying assets. This enforces stability and safety protocols by preventing end-users or administrators from modifying components directly in the production environment, which ensures consistency with source control.\n3. Export from development and import into production in deployment-ready state ➔ Managed solution Reasoning: To transport customizations in a finalized, secure, and deployment-ready state, you export your unmanaged development solution as a managed package. This package is then imported into the target production environment. The managed container structure allows for clean version tracking, simplified application upgrades, and complete asset removal upon uninstallation.", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Allow ongoing development.", "options": ["Default solution", "Managed solution", "Unmanaged solution", "Standalone agent outside a solution"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Prevent modification in production.", "options": ["Default solution", "Managed solution", "Temporary solution", "Unmanaged solution"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Export from development and import into production in deployment-ready state.", "options": ["Default solution", "Managed solution", "Unmanaged solution", "Standalone agent outside a solution"], "answer": 1}], "prompt": "Select the solution type for each requirement to support deployment and lifecycle management of the agents."}, {"id": 165, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is standardizing ALM for agents in Copilot Studio.\nThe team requires a custom solution that includes agents. The solution must support ALM scenarios, such as managing and transporting agent assets across environments and pipeline deployments.\nYou need to create an ALM-ready custom solution in Copilot Studio.\nWhat should you do?", "expl": "B. Create a new custom solution from the Solutions area.\nCreating the Container: Before you can build an ALM-ready agent, you must first create an unmanaged custom solution within the Solutions navigation area of the Copilot Studio Portal (or Power Apps Maker portal).\n​Managing Dependencies: Working within a dedicated custom solution allows you to group your agent with its respective components (such as topics, environment variables, flows, and connection references), ensuring that the entire bundle can be cleanly moved across environments.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Publish the agent to a channel and use that channel configuration as the ALM container."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Create a new custom solution from the Solutions area."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Export the custom solution as a managed solution and import it into production."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Export the agent directly and then import it into another environment."}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 166, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You run the same fixed test set three times in Copilot Studio.\nDuring evaluation, you observe the following:\n• The same interaction fails in all three runs.\n• Score values range from 0.58 to 0.61.\n• The reasoning states that the response partially matches the expected answer.\n• The knowledge source that is used is internal documentation.\n• No tools are invoked.\nYou need to determine which conclusions are supported based on the evaluation results.\nWhich conclusions should you make? To answer, move the appropriate conclusions to the correct evaluation results. You may use each conclusion once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Evidence of a recurring issue. ➔ A pattern of failure across repeated runs.\nInformation provided about response quality. ➔ The response partially matches the expected answer.\nEvaluation limitations. ➔ The specific underlying cause of the failure.", "type": "match", "items": ["A pattern of failure across repeated runs.", "The specific underlying cause of the failure.", "Use of a connected knowledge source during the interaction.", "The response partially matches the expected answer."], "slots": ["Evidence of a recurring issue.", "Information provided about response quality.", "Evaluation limitations."], "answer": ["A pattern of failure across repeated runs.", "The response partially matches the expected answer.", "The specific underlying cause of the failure."], "reuse": true}, {"id": 167, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team wants to automate solution deployment between environments. Deployments must follow a controlled, repeatable process.\nThe team needs to implement a pipeline-based deployment process that enforces the correct dependency order.\nYou need to implement a deployment pipeline and determine the correct sequence of actions.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "Enable pipelines for the tenant.\nAssign environments to pipeline stages.\nTrigger a pipeline run.\nReview deployment results.", "type": "order", "items": ["Assign environments to pipeline stages.", "Trigger a pipeline run.", "Enable pipelines for the tenant.", "Review deployment results."], "answer": ["Enable pipelines for the tenant.", "Assign environments to pipeline stages.", "Trigger a pipeline run.", "Review deployment results."]}, {"id": 168, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company needs an agent in Copilot Studio to complete tasks by interacting with a legacy desktop application that does not use APIs. The builder needs to validate that UI automation can run in the supported execution context and needs a way to inspect how the automation behaves during testing conversations.\nTo meet the business needs, the solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Enable the agent to perform UI automation against the legacy application.\n• Inspect execution details for the automation during conversations.\nYou need to configure the agent based on the requirements.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Testing Pane & Remote Session (C): When testing a computer-use-enabled agent inside the Copilot Studio testing pane, makers can open a live remote desktop session overlay. This lets you inspect execution details and watch the agent navigate, take screenshots, and execute mouse/keyboard events in real time during a test conversation.\nComputer use capability (E): The Computer Use tool in Copilot Studio is built exactly for this purpose. It allows AI agents to visually navigate desktop/web apps, type fields, and click buttons on a remote machine using natural language descriptions, providing resilient UI automation against legacy systems where traditional APIs do not exist.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Configure a custom connector for the desktop app so the agent can automate the UI through authenticated calls."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Add a Power Platform Desktop flow to control the desktop app."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Access the remote session within the testing pane to monitor the real-time execution of the agent's desktop interactions."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Publish the agent so the automation runs in the supported environment automatically."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Enable the Computer use capability."}], "answer": ["C", "E"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 169, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A development team is reusing agents in Copilot Studio across development, test, and production environments.\nYou need to ensure the agents meet the following requirements:\n• Reusable components must support governed promotion across environments.\n• Environment-specific configuration must not be hard-coded.\n• Deployments must support automated, multi-stage approvals.\nYou need to configure the agents based on the business requirements.\nWhich configurations should you use for each requirement? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Share authored topics and prompts across environments:\nExport as a component collection.\nMove environment-specific configurations including connections and secrets:\nUse connection references and environment variables.\nAutomate multi-stage deployments with approvals:\nUse an ALM pipeline.", "type": "match", "items": ["Use an ALM pipeline.", "Export as a component collection.", "Use connection references and environment variables."], "slots": ["Share authored topics and prompts across environments.", "Move environment-specific configurations including connections and secrets.", "Automate multi-stage deployments with approvals."], "answer": ["Export as a component collection.", "Use connection references and environment variables.", "Use an ALM pipeline."]}, {"id": 170, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "An agent calls a flow that intermittently produces unexpected results during execution.\nThe agent behaves inconsistently.\nYou need to investigate agent flow executions to diagnose the issue.\nWhich two actions should you use? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "Inspect failed run details (A): Clicking into a specific failed execution from the history allows you to view the visual execution path. You can look at each individual action step, inspect the exact inputs/outputs, and read the specific error message or status code produced by the failure to diagnose the issue.\nReview flow run history (E): This provides a complete chronological list of all recent executions (Succeeded, Failed, or Running). It serves as the starting point for seeing exactly when and how often an execution has failed.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Inspect failed run details."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Update the flow trigger definition."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Check the Error column in the Activity tab."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Review flow analytics summary."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Review flow run history."}, {"k": "F", "t": "Increase the connector timeout."}], "answer": ["A", "E"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 171, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You create a test set in Copilot Studio to evaluate an agent that answers policy questions and retrieves data from connected knowledge sources.\nYou run the evaluation and review the following result for one test case:\n• Expected response: Digital products are non-refundable after download.\n• Actual response: Digital products cannot be refunded once downloaded.\n• Result: Pass -\n• Score: 0.92 -\n• Reasoning: The actual response semantically matches the expected response.\n• Knowledge sources used: RefundPolicy_KB\n• Tools invoked: None -\nYou need to determine whether additional corrective action is required based on the evaluation results.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "C. Accept the result because semantic matching is sufficient for a passing evaluation.\nD. Confirm that the connected knowledge source is functioning as expected.\nAccepting semantic matches (C): In conversational AI and agent testing, responses generated via Large Language Models (LLMs) rarely provide an exact 100% word-for-word match to the test script. Since the evaluation checks for information quality, if a response is a partial or semantic match that fulfills the intent, it is considered a passing evaluation and requires no corrective action.\nVerifying knowledge sources (D): Because agent interactions heavily rely on grounding data, ensuring that any connected knowledge sources (such as SharePoint sites, websites, or internal repositories) are online and properly indexed is a critical diagnostic step to ensure responses remain accurate across repeated test runs.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Investigate external tool configuration for this test case."}, {"k": "B", "t": "Rerun the evaluation because the score is below 1.00."}, {"k": "C", "t": "Accept the result because semantic matching is sufficient for a passing evaluation."}, {"k": "D", "t": "Confirm that the connected knowledge source is functioning as expected."}, {"k": "E", "t": "Modify the test case because the response is not an exact match."}], "answer": ["C", "D"], "type": "multi"}, {"id": 172, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team deploys Copilot Studio solutions by using Power Platform Pipelines.\nYou must configure a deployment strategy that meets the following requirements:\n• Ensure that the solutions deploy in dev, test, and production environments in order.\n• Identify missing connection references or environment variables before the deployment process begins.\n• Manage all pipeline stages and security from a single, unified location for all linked environments.\nYou need to configure the pipeline to meet the requirements.\nWhat should you configure for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Sequential stage progression\nWhy it fits: In an ideal Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) flow, updates must move systematically from Development \\(\\rightarrow \\) Test \\(\\rightarrow \\) Production without bypassing any intermediate environments. Implementing a sequence restriction ensures that a solution cannot accidentally be pushed into a live Production environment before successfully passing the verification stages in the Test environment.\n2. Deployment input validation\nWhy it fits: Before code or solutions are applied to a target environment, the system performs a series of pre- flight checks. This automated verification evaluates if all dependency structures, connection references, and required environment variables have valid values assigned. Identifying missing parameters before execution prevents broken configurations or runtime errors post-deployment.\n3. Centralized pipeline configuration\nWhy it fits: In enterprise platforms (like Microsoft Power Platform), a single dedicated environment acts as the Pipeline Host. Administering pipeline definitions, stage connections, access controls, and deployment permissions from this one centralized location ensures consistent governance across all linked developer and target environments.", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Solutions deploy in dev, test and production environments in order.", "options": ["Sequential stage progression", "Direct target environment selection", "Environment-specific deployment packages"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Identify missing connection references or environment variables before deployment.", "options": ["Deployment input validation", "Managed solution deployment", "Deployment configuration override"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Manage pipeline stages and security from a single, unified location for linked environments.", "options": ["Environment-level customization", "Centralized pipeline configuration", "Builder-defined deployment settings"], "answer": 1}]}, {"id": 173, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is preparing a test set to evaluate an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding access to additional users. The test set must support consistent evaluation across repeated runs and focus on the agent's most important behaviors.\nYou need to create a test set that enables repeatable and meaningful evaluation.\nIn which order should you perform the actions? To answer, move all actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "Identify the high-value conversational intents the agent must handle.\nAuthor representative user prompts that trigger the identified intents.\nDefine the expected outcomes and success criteria for each interaction.\nBaseline the test set to ensure deterministic evaluation across repeated runs.\n1. Identify Intents: You must first establish the scope of what the agent needs to do by choosing the key, high-value tasks and user intentions it is responsible for handling.\n2. Author Prompts: Once the targeted intents are established, you write realistic user phrases and inputs to explicitly trigger those specific intents during evaluation.\n3. Define Success Criteria: For every test prompt created, you must specify what a correct or successful response looks like (e.g., specific entities extracted or correct topic triggered) to grade the agent fairly.\n4. Baseline the Test Set: Finally, lock in the test dataset to establish a repeatable baseline. This ensures you can run identical benchmarks later and accurately measure if future agent updates improve or degrade performance.", "type": "order", "items": ["Define the expected outcomes and success criteria for each interaction.", "Author representative user prompts that trigger the identified intents.", "Identify the high-value conversational intents the agent must handle.", "Baseline the test set to ensure deterministic evaluation across repeated runs."], "answer": ["Identify the high-value conversational intents the agent must handle.", "Author representative user prompts that trigger the identified intents.", "Define the expected outcomes and success criteria for each interaction.", "Baseline the test set to ensure deterministic evaluation across repeated runs."], "prompt": "Create a test set that enables repeatable and meaningful evaluation of a Copilot Studio agent."}, {"id": 174, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A team is preparing to assess an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding access to additional users.\nThe team requires an evaluation that provides controlled, repeatable, and consistently measured test runs. The evaluation must:\n• Be triggered directly by the team to control when testing occurs.\n• Determine success based on clearly established acceptance criteria.\n• Rely on a predefined source that ensures consistency across repeated runs.\nYou need to configure the evaluation to meet the requirements.\nWhat should you configure? To answer, move the appropriate configurations to the correct evaluation requirements. You may use each configuration once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to move the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "On-Demand Execution: Allows developers or QA teams to manually trigger an evaluation run at specific milestones (like after a major code change) instead of relying on a scheduled or automated trigger.\nComparing Against Expectations: Directly assesses whether the agent's output satisfies the pre-configured success criteria or ground truth.\nCurated Prompt List: Serves as a standardized test harness or benchmark dataset, ensuring the agent is always evaluated against the exact same inputs to prevent testing variability.", "type": "match", "items": ["Execute the evaluation on demand.", "Expand rollout to additional channels.", "Enable analytics to monitor agent usage trends.", "Compare responses against defined expectations.", "Use a curated prompt list as the interaction source."], "slots": ["Triggered directly by the team to control when testing occurs.", "Determine success based on clearly established acceptance criteria.", "Rely on a predefined source that ensures consistency across repeated runs."], "answer": ["Execute the evaluation on demand.", "Compare responses against defined expectations.", "Use a curated prompt list as the interaction source."], "reuse": true}, {"id": 175, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "You run multiple evaluation tests for an agent in Copilot Studio before expanding user access.\nYou observe the following about the evaluation results:\n• Each evaluation case is reported as meeting or missing the expected response.\n• Some evaluation cases fail repeatedly across several runs.\n• Each run displays the expected response and the response generated by the agent.\nYou need to interpret what the evaluation results reveal.\nWhat should you conclude based on each interpretation? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.", "expl": "1. Pass or fail status\nWhy it fits: When analyzing a single, distinct test case or user interaction, the most direct result is a binary evaluation—determining whether that specific case met the criteria (Pass) or missed it (Fail).\n2. Consistent outcomes\nWhy it fits: Running the exact same evaluation suite multiple times helps identify system reliability. Observing stable, identical behavior across these repeated iterations indicates consistent outcomes, confirming the system is deterministic and predictable.\n3. Expected and generated responses\nWhy it fits: To understand why a test run behaved the way it did, the granular test output reports the raw text generated by the agent side-by-side with the target ground truth. This raw comparison of expected vs.\ngenerated responses allows developers to manually audit differences.", "type": "hotspot", "rows": [{"label": "Outcome of individual evaluation cases", "options": ["Pass or fail status", "Failure configuration", "Production readiness"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Behavior observed across repeated runs", "options": ["Prompt revisions", "Consistent outcomes", "Intermittent outcomes"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Details presented in evaluation output", "options": ["Deployment approval", "Root cause of failures", "Expected and generated responses"], "answer": 2}]}, {"id": 176, "case": null, "series": false, "text": "A company uses an agent in Copilot Studio in production and requires centralized monitoring for operational visibility and troubleshooting.\nThe team decides to use Application Insights as the monitoring solution.\nAn agent activity is not included in the monitoring data.\nYou need to enable monitoring the agent by using Application Insights.\nWhich four actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.", "expl": "1. Create or identify the Application Insights destination for telemetry.\n2. Configure which monitoring destination the agent uses.\n3. Enable monitoring for the agent.\n4. Publish the agent so the monitoring configuration takes effect.\nStep 1 (Infrastructure Setup): You must begin in the Azure portal by creating an Application Insights resource (or locating an existing one) to obtain the connection string that will host and index the telemetry log streams. Step 2 (Destination Mapping): In Copilot Studio, navigate to Settings > Advanced > Application Insights and paste the copied Azure connection string. This connects the conversational agent interface to your cloud storage environment.\nStep 3 (Configuration): Once the destination is bound, toggle on or choose the specific logging behaviors (such as recording user activity properties, messages, or custom topic events) to activate telemetry capturing. Step 4 (Deployment): Telemetry configurations remain local and inactive until a build is compiled. You must Publish the agent to push these telemetry endpoints into production so that conversation logs begin routing live to Azure Monitor.", "type": "order", "items": ["Enable monitoring for the agent.", "Publish the agent so the monitoring configuration takes effect.", "Configure which monitoring destination the agent uses.", "Create or identify the Application Insights destination for telemetry.", "Configure the agent feature that generates grounded answers.", "Configure the agent feature that selects a model from a catalog."], "answer": ["Create or identify the Application Insights destination for telemetry.", "Configure which monitoring destination the agent uses.", "Enable monitoring for the agent.", "Publish the agent so the monitoring configuration takes effect."], "prompt": "Select the four actions, in order, to enable monitoring of the agent by using Application Insights."}, {"id": 177, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A team is implementing ALM for agents in Copilot Studio across multiple environments.\nThe team requires a consistent way to manage and transport agent assets.\nYou need to recommend a solution that supports creation and use of ALM scenarios for agents in Copilot Studio.\nSolution: To export, import, and manage agents between environments, you need to create and use a custom solution.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "§ A. Yes\nIn the Power Platform ecosystem, a solution (specifically a custom solution created by the development team) is the official and required ALM container used to package, manage, transport, and deploy assets— including Microsoft Copilot Studio agents, topics, and environment variables—across multiple environments (such as Development, Test, and Production).", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["A"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 178, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A team is implementing ALM for agents in Copilot Studio across multiple environments.\nThe team requires a consistent way to manage and transport agent assets.\nYou need to recommend a solution that supports creation and use of ALM scenarios for agents in Copilot Studio.\nSolution: Publishing an agent to a channel is the required ALM container for transporting agents between environments.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "§ B. No\nPublishing an agent simply makes it live and accessible to end-users on a specific channel (such as Microsoft Teams or a public website) within its current environment. It is not a deployment tool.\nThe required ALM container for transporting Microsoft Copilot Studio agents between distinct environments (e.g., from Development to Test or Production) is a Power Platform Solution.", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}, {"id": 179, "case": null, "series": true, "text": "A team is implementing ALM for agents in Copilot Studio across multiple environments.\nThe team requires a consistent way to manage and transport agent assets.\nYou need to recommend a solution that supports creation and use of ALM scenarios for agents in Copilot Studio.\nSolution: Builders can change environment variable values directly in Copilot Studio to complete ALM configuration without administrative tools.\nDoes the solution meet the goal?", "expl": "§ B. No\nWhile builders can use environment variables within Copilot Studio to change configuration data (such as API endpoints) between different environments, doing so does not provide a mechanism to actually manage and transport the copilot assets across environments.\nTo properly implement Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) for Copilot Studio agents, you must group the agent components into a Power Platform Solution and export/import that solution package (via Pipelines, DevOps, or administrative tools) between your dev, test, and production environments. Modifying variable values directly inside Copilot Studio without these transport tools does not satisfy the requirements of a consistent asset deployment strategy.\n§ Thank you\nThank you for being so interested in the premium exam material.\nI'm glad to hear that you found it informative and helpful.\nIf you have any feedback or thoughts on the bumps, I would love to hear them.\nYour insights can help me improve our writing and better understand our readers.\n§ Best of Luck\nYou have worked hard to get to this point, and you are well-prepared for the exam\nKeep your head up, stay positive, and go show that exam what you're made of!\n§ Feedback\n§ More Papers\nTotal: 179 Questions\nLink: https://certyiq.com/papers/microsoft/ab-620", "options": [{"k": "A", "t": "Yes"}, {"k": "B", "t": "No"}], "answer": ["B"], "type": "single"}];
